  {"a":"sion","b":"aatrox","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Skill","Skill","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Aatrox's full Q3 sweetspot combo with W chain-pull is online — if he lands the knockup you eat his entire rotation with no reliable escape."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught turns every passive-zombie trade into a genuine threat: R into him, knockup with charged Q, and his R cooldown becomes your kill window."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP stacking makes your W-shield massive and your zombie passive lethal — force brawls the moment Aatrox's Q is down."},{"when":"Late","text":"World Ender's amplified drain outpaces your HP stacking in long 1v1s — buy Grievous Wounds before it lands, or keep fights short."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup when Aatrox's Darkin Blade is on cooldown, then E-shred his armor and brawl","Keep the wave on your side of the lane so Aatrox must overextend into your charged-Q range","Use Unstoppable Onslaught at level 6 to close distance instantly and threaten a kill on his R cooldown"],"foe":["Land the Q3 sweetspot knockup into Infernal Chains before your Soul Furnace can absorb the burst","Land Infernal Chains to drag you out of your charging Decimating Smash and reset the trade in his favor","Activate R to deny your kill window after baiting out your charged Q, then out-heal your remaining damage"]},"early":"At level 1 you are both feeling out range — Aatrox will try to prod with the edge of The Darkin Blade and establish priority, but he cannot all-in yet. Do not charge Decimating Smash in the open; the wind-up is readable and he will simply sidestep and punish the whiff with a full three-swing rotation. Farm with short auto-cancel trades and use Soul Furnace to eat his poke, not his combo. Level 2 opens your first real window: a brush-charged Q, hidden behind minions until release, can catch him mid-swing and deliver a half-HP knockup he cannot answer. Land it and trade; miss it and concede the wave.","mid":"Levels 3-5 are Aatrox's best stretch. His full The Darkin Blade rotation with Infernal Chains is online and you have no answer if he lands Q3 into the chain — it deals enough to drop you into zombie range without your full HP stacking yet established. Track The Darkin Blade religiously: it is 10 seconds at rank 3 and 6 when maxed, and his pattern dies without it. When it is down, run your combo — Roar of the Slayer slow into a charged Decimating Smash stun, Soul Furnace to eat the counter-trade. When it is up, give him nothing and let the wave reset. At 6 your Unstoppable Onslaught changes the math: World Ender gives him a combat-stat burst but your R closes from 7500 units, so he can no longer play passive after a Q cooldown — you close and force a brawl he must win on drain-healing alone.","late":"Heartsteel and Warmog's turn your zombie passive into a genuine second fight: a healthy Aatrox that drops you to zero still has to survive 4-6 seconds of unstoppable zombie damage while your HP is enormous. That said, his R heals scale with missing HP and his Q sweetspots deal percentage-of-your-HP-adjacent burst in a prolonged fight — never duel him at even-HP in a long brawl at 2+ items. Your lane win condition is to use Unstoppable Onslaught as a teamfight engage tool, zone with zombie threat, and deny him solo carries. If he gets two kills ahead, stop forcing and play for wave control: your side-lane pattern and R transit are still globally useful even when you are losing the 1v1.","whys":["At level 1 his Darkin Blade edges are active but his full rotation is not — keep a W-shield charge ready and do not stand still charging Q in his poke range.","Level 2 is even because a brush-charged Q stun is a genuine half-HP punish, but he can sidestep it and reply with his empowered Q2 auto chain if you miss.","Aatrox's full Q3 knockup plus W-pull lands at level 3 and your HP stacking is not yet large enough to tank the full combo — trade only on his Q cooldown.","Levels 4-5 Aatrox controls the trade pattern with Darkin Blade timing; without a Heartsteel spike yet, your W-shield value is moderate and he can out-trade extended brawls.","Unstoppable Onslaught at level 6 makes every trade a genuine all-in threat — if his R is on cooldown you can close, land the charged Q knockup, and brawl freely.","Heartsteel's HP stacking makes your W-shield absorb more and your zombie passive last longer — force trades the instant his Q is down and exploit the first-item window.","At 2+ items Aatrox's R healing and sustained Q3 patterns out-trade your HP stacking in drawn-out duels — keep fights to one rotation or bring your team."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"akali","win":["Skill","Akali","Akali","Skill","Skill","Akali","Akali"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"A fully charged Q knockup at level 1 is a genuine kill threat — Akali has no shroud yet and no escape from the stun."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Akali's R two-part dash comes online and she can now burst you between W procs; respect her all-in and keep W ready."},{"when":"1st item","text":"With Heartsteel ticking your HP passive begins compounding — W-shield value climbs with your max HP and she needs two items to reliably one-shot you."},{"when":"Late","text":"At full build your zombie passive guarantees trade value even if she bursts you — she cannot contest you in a slow side-lane brawl."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup before Akali can activate Twilight Shroud to dodge it","Pop W-shield to absorb her burst window between R casts","Stack max HP via kills and Heartsteel so your zombie passive zones her off objectives even in death"],"foe":["Drop Twilight Shroud so you lose sight of her mid-charge and swing the area blind","Burst you between W-shield charges using Q poke into R1-E-R2 sequence","Scale to two items where her burst reliably outpaces your W regeneration between fights"]},"early":"At level 1 Akali has no shroud and no escape — she is uniquely vulnerable to a cold-opened fully charged Q that most melee champions would just walk out of. Respect that window: hold behind your wave, bait her forward with apparent passive farming, and release the charge the instant she steps into knockup range. If you land it, Roar of the Slayer to shred armour and detonate Soul Furnace while she is stunned. If you miss, back off — Five Point Strike pokes magic damage and Soul Furnace only answers it every 15s, so two or three uncontested Qs flip the trade math by level 3. The 3-second detonation on short trades is your equalizer: she trades one Q proc, you trade the blast, and your passive means she never gets free value even if she wins the all-in.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are the flattest stretch — you have your full basic kit but no Unstoppable Onslaught, and Akali can fish Five Point Strike on a 1.5-second cooldown, gated only by Energy. Her Assassin's Mark ring proc is the commit tell, not her cast. Do not charge Q in her line of sight; use brush or a minion wall to hide the glow, then step and release. At level 6 the dynamic flips sharply: R1 closes the gap, her E repositions, and R2 executes you below the threshold. Do not fight her post-6 unless Soul Furnace is up and you have the stun ready to interrupt between her two R casts — that gap is roughly 1.5 seconds, just enough to land a charged Q and deny the execute.","late":"Two items into late game your max-HP total via Heartsteel stacks and your kill-passive HP bonuses push your effective durability past what a single Akali rotation can delete. She needs both R casts plus a full Q-E-Q chain — Soul Furnace absorbs one burst window and your zombie passive spends 6-8 seconds as a frontline threat even if she completes the combo. Push side lanes with Unstoppable Onslaught as a transit tool, force her to match you or concede towers, and make her burn R just to disengage from a zombie. You own the pre-6 stun threat and the very late brawl, she owns the level-6 burst window — and remember Twilight Shroud only hides her from your aim, so swing where she stood, not where you can see.","whys":["Akali has no Twilight Shroud at level 1 — a fully charged Q knockup lands clean and she has no escape tool to avoid it.","Both champions have meaningful tools but no dominant spike — you have W-shield, she has Q poke, and neither can force a clean kill.","Your E armor-shred plus W detonation makes short trades even on her Q poke, but she can disengage with passive movement speed before you land a full combo.","At levels 4-5 her Q cooldown is shorter than your charge time in the open — neither side can force a reliable kill window.","Her R two-part dash and shroud come online together — she can burst between your W charges and disengage before your Q resolves.","Heartsteel ticking makes your W-shield and zombie passive increasingly hard to punish — neither side can cleanly end the lane.","At full HP stack your zombie passive is a 6-8 second frontline threat she cannot delete cleanly — she cannot match you in a slow side-lane brawl."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"akshan","win":["Akshan","Akshan","Akshan","Akshan","Akshan","Akshan","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-5","text":"Heroic Swing anchors on any wall so he can swing in, land Avengerang and a Dirty Fighting shot, and leave before your Q charges — punish needs the stun first."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Akshan can use his E to stay mobile and dodge your R charge; his revive passive means even a kill can be reversed if a nearby ally dies."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Once you complete Heartsteel your HP stack begins compounding and your W-shield value grows — sustain his poke and look for a charged Q knockup to open a real trade."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"At full tank with 5,000+ HP your W-shield absorbs his burst and your zombie passive forces him off you even when dying — the lane flips decisively."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup to break his kite loop and open a W-detonate E-armor-shred combo","Stack HP passively through Heartsteel procs and W-shield trades until your bulk exceeds his damage","Use R to close distance when he overextends or to transit to a side lane where he cannot follow safely"],"foe":["Swing in on Heroic Swing, land Dirty Fighting's extra shot, and exit before your Q charges","Poke your W-shield off cooldown so you cannot detonated it in a follow-up trade","Mark you a Scoundrel by trading kills, then kill you to resurrect the ally you took down"]},"early":"Levels 1-3 are Akshan's best window and he knows it. Heroic Swing anchors on a wall, so he swings across, fires Avengerang plus a Dirty Fighting shot, and is back behind minions before your Q finishes charging. Do not stand still trying to full-charge Q — he reads the animation and disengages the instant your feet leave the ground. Use Soul Furnace proactively to absorb the poke rather than saving it for an all-in, because he will never let you all-in him at this stage. Farm and accept lane pressure; your passive means he gains nothing from trading kills.","mid":"Levels 4-6 stay uncomfortable. Heroic Swing is back every 15s at rank 3 and your Q is still a snail's telegraph on a mobile ranged target. At 6 your R gives you a threat vector — if he is standing far from a wall anchor your R can close the gap faster than his swing exits — but he will respect this and hug terrain. Your best mid-game play is to bait a greedy grapple onto the wave near you, flash or R the remaining gap, and land the charged Q. One knockup is a full trade won; he has no real defensive cooldown once the swing is down, and Roar of the Slayer's armour shred into a Soul Furnace detonation chunks him hard. Track that 15s before committing.","late":"This is why the matchup is FAVOURED for you despite the painful lane. At 2+ items your HP total is so large that his burst does not end trades — he chips, Soul Furnace eats the window, and your zombie passive threatens him even as you die. He cannot splitpush against you because your R covers 7500 units and one knockup in a side lane is a kill. Going Rogue runs the opposite way to how it reads: he resurrects a fallen ally by killing the Scoundrel who took them, so after you claim a kill, do not hand him yours. Force objectives after every skirmish you win, where the HP advantage is decisive and his mobility loses value.","whys":["Akshan pokes with Avengerang out and back while Dirty Fighting adds a second shot — you have no answer at level 1 and take the chip.","Heroic Swing is up every 18s off any wall, and Avengerang out-ranges your charge at 850; his poke outpaces you.","His full E kit is online and your Q telegraph is fully readable by an experienced Akshan — he disengages every time your feet stop.","With no ult yet your only gap-close is walking, and he can stay out of your range indefinitely using E to reposition off any wall or minion.","Comeuppance stores bullets that scale with your missing health, and Heroic Swing angles him off your charge line; commit only with the stun.","He can still kite your first-item powerspike and his Q poke peels your W-shield off cooldown before you can detonate it for value.","At 2+ items your HP pool and W-shield absorb his burst, your E armor-shred punishes any close-range mistake, and your zombie passive makes trading into you a losing proposition even in his winning moments."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"ambessa","win":["Skill","Ambessa","Ambessa","Skill","Ambessa","Ambessa","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Ambessa's dash-combo comes fully online — she weaves around your charged-Q telegraph and trades on her terms every time you wind up."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Public Execution blinks to the farthest target, suppresses and stuns; she owns the all-in unless Soul Furnace eats the rotation."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Ambessa's Sundered Sky or Black Cleaver spike compounds her armor-shred window — keep W ready and do not let her open a trade freely."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your Heartsteel HP passive peaks and W-shield value becomes enormous; Ambessa cannot burst through your bulk and your zombie passive punishes any reckless all-in."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a full-charge Q stun while Ambessa's dash is on cooldown to flip the trade","Hold the charged swing until Repudiation's brace ends, or you feed its bonus damage","Stack HP through Heartsteel kills and farm to reach the 2+ item window where bulk wins"],"foe":["Dash your charged-Q telegraph every time and punish your immobile body mid-charge","Weave her R chain CC into a full burst combo while your W is already burned","Slow you with Lacerate and chain Drakehound's Step so you never land a stun"]},"early":"At level 1 you both feel each other out — Drakehound's Step only fires off an ability cast, so she has one dash in her, and your Q charge is the only real threat. Farm safely under the Q tip and do not commit a full wind-up unless she walks straight at you with no room to dash. By level 2 she has enough casts to dash around your telegraph on demand, so she starts poking freely; pre-pop Soul Furnace before walking up to last-hit under pressure. By level 3 she has her full basic rotation and is winning every short trade — accept the chip, keep W for her combo, and stay healthy for level 6.","mid":"At levels 4-5 you have your full kit but no power spike, and she is still dashing off your charged Q while taking favorable short trades; this is her window to build a lead, so play at max charged-Q range and spend Roar of the Slayer's armour shred only to set up a stun you are confident will land. Level 6 is still hers — Public Execution suppresses you out of every answer before your zombie passive matters, and your R is better as an escape or ganksync tool than a 1v1 engage. Buy Grievous Wounds before the fights get long, stop bleeding gold, and keep the wave managed.","late":"At two items your Heartsteel HP stack starts making you genuinely unkillable in a sustained brawl — Ambessa's damage does not scale with your HP the way Vayne's W or Gwen's scissors do, so the pure bulk wall actually works here. Force long fights, let your W absorb her opening rotation, then lock her down with a charged Q the instant her dash is on cooldown. Your zombie passive also flips the psychology: she cannot all-in recklessly without feeding your team a punish window, so she is forced to respect you in sideline 1v1s that you now own. Play for the prolonged teamfight and deny her a clean burst window.","whys":["Neither has a clear level-1 edge — Drakehound's Step only dashes off an ability cast, so she has one, and your Q tip keeps her honest.","With two abilities she chains Drakehound's Step dashes around your charge telegraph and trades freely while you wind up.","Her full combo lands at 3: Lacerate slows, Sundering Slam follows Cunning Sweep, and she dashes your Q — take chip and bank Soul Furnace.","Both champions have their full basic kits but no item spikes; the lane is skill-dependent — whoever lands their CC first wins the trade.","Public Execution suppresses, slams and stuns on a 130s cooldown and heals her through the burst; play your R as a gank anchor, not a solo kill.","Her Black Cleaver or Sundered Sky spike compounds her armor-shred trades and she still dodges your Q with her dash — W first, then look for a stun.","Your Heartsteel HP wall peaks and she lacks percent-HP damage to punish it; charged Q through a burned dash into a sustained brawl is the kill condition."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"aurora","win":["Sion","Skill","Sion","Sion","Aurora","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"A brush-charged Decimating Smash at level 1 knocks Aurora up before her E blink exists — the only window she literally cannot sidestep."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Bank Twofold Hex cooldowns into W-shield short trades: pop Soul Furnace, absorb her Q poke, detonate for magic damage, and back out with the HP lead."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Between Worlds leaps her out of Unstoppable Onslaught's path and leaves a rift she teleports across — every R commit must account for that hop."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel completion is Sion's clearest spike: HP passive compounding makes every Q-knockup trade a losing exchange for Aurora who must burst through a giant shield."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup while The Weirding is on cooldown so Aurora cannot hop out of the stun","Keep the wave frozen near your turret so Aurora must walk into your brush-charge range to farm","Stack Heartsteel safely early and force sustained brawls after first item when W-shield value eclipses her burst"],"foe":["Hop The Weirding backward out of every charged-Q path and deny the knockup entirely","Curse you with Twofold Hex and recast it back through you between Soul Furnace windows","Teleport across the Between Worlds rift to break your charge line and re-establish her range"]},"early":"Level 1 is Sion's clearest window in this lane — Aurora has no E blink yet, so a charged Decimating Smash from brush is a half-HP knockup she cannot escape. The moment she hits level 2, every subsequent charge must come from fog or behind minion cover because she will simply E sideways through the telegraph if she sees it coming. Use Soul Furnace liberally to absorb her Q poke rather than trading HP directly; her burst beats you in short trades until you land a stun. Hold the wave near your side of the river so she is always walking toward brush you can charge from, not the other direction.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 belong to Sion on cooldown math alone — The Weirding is 15s at rank 1, and every time it is down you run the full pattern: Roar of the Slayer's armour shred, charged Q stun, brawl with Soul Furnace detonating mid-trade. When the hop is up, give nothing: back to the wave, freeze, and make her walk to you. At level 6 the dynamic shifts temporarily — Between Worlds leaps her sideways and leaves a rift she teleports across, so she slips your R path and punishes the 140s cooldown. Treat level 6 as a mutual standoff and wait for it to be spent elsewhere before re-engaging.","late":"First item Heartsteel is where Sion reasserts dominance for the remainder of the game — her HP passive begins compounding in earnest, Soul Furnace shield value climbs with every stack, and Aurora's burst stops keeping pace with her bulk. Force extended brawls on The Weirding's cooldown with Grievous Wounds already bought, or Spirit Abjuration's spirits refund everything you chip; and let the zombie passive threaten her even if she kills you. She stays a burst threat, so never commit Unstoppable Onslaught into an uncast Between Worlds — bait it first, then close.","whys":["Aurora has no E blink at level 1 — a charged Decimating Smash from brush lands uncontested and sets the HP lead before she can contest.","With The Weirding online she hops out of most charged-Q attempts; trade inside Soul Furnace windows and do not chase her hop across the lane.","Full kit online with E armor-shred setting up the charged Q — keep the wave on your side so she must walk into brush-charge range and cannot kite the telegraph.","Track The Weirding's 15s and force short Soul Furnace detonation trades when it is down; when it is up, freeze and starve her of CS rather than engaging into the hop.","Between Worlds leaps her sideways and leaves a rift she teleports across, breaking your charge line — treat 6 as a standoff and bait it before you commit.","Heartsteel completion is the turning point: HP passive compounding means your W-shield absorbs her burst and every Q-knockup trade tilts in your favor for the rest of the game.","Two items in, your HP total and zombie passive make you nearly unkillable in a sustained fight — force side-lane brawls on Twofold Hex's cooldown and let the passive threaten her even when she lands the kill."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"camille","win":["Camille","Camille","Camille","Skill","Camille","Skill","Camille"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Charged Q from fog-of-war drops Camille before her E hookshot can answer — this is your earliest kill window if she stands still charging her own Q."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"The Hextech Ultimatum anchors you into a 1v1 arena where Tactical Sweep's outer edge heals her off your HP — never fight 6 without Soul Furnace."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel completion is your re-entry: your HP base is now punishing Camille's Q true-damage per auto, and W-shield value jumps enough to survive her full combo."},{"when":"Late","text":"At two items your zombie passive turns every all-in threat into a coin-flip for Camille — she cannot afford to dive you without a guaranteed burst window."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup on Camille before she can E hookshot into you","Stand inside Tactical Sweep's inner half so the outer edge cannot heal her, then shred armour","Keep the wave near your side of the lane so Camille must walk into charged-Q range to contest"],"foe":["Hookshot a wall to knock you up, then land Tactical Sweep's outer edge while you are locked","Bully you level 1 before your W-shield is online to build a lead she takes into her item spike","Anchor you with The Hextech Ultimatum while Soul Furnace is down and burn you with Precision Protocol"]},"early":"Level 1 is the one stage where neither kit has a decisive edge — Precision Protocol procs true damage off her autos, but Hookshot is 16 seconds at rank 1, so she cannot chain-knock you up freely. The moment you hit level 2, Soul Furnace changes the math: bait her into auto range, pop W to eat her first Q proc, then charge your Q while Hookshot is on cooldown. She cannot safely E into you mid-charge without eating the full knockup. At level 3 Tactical Sweep becomes the real threat — never let her land both Q autos plus the outer edge of that cone in one trade, because the outer half also heals her back up.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are her farm window — her E hookshot cooldown drops with rank and she starts threading wall-to-wall dashes to stay unpredictable. Respect the hookshot completely in this window: if you see her walking toward a wall, back off your Q charge immediately because a hooked stun into her W cone is a half-HP trade at minimum. At level 6 she gains the greatest threat in the matchup — her R drops a hex-arena that turns the lane into a pure 1v1 with no escape. She will use it specifically when your W-shield is on cooldown. Track that cooldown obsessively; if she walks up aggressively and your W is down, disengage with R Unstoppable Onslaught rather than eating the isolated combo.","late":"First item Heartsteel flips the math back in your favor — your HP total outgrows her burst budget (your zombie threat compounds with HP). Buy Grievous Wounds before you commit to any long trade, or Tactical Sweep's outer edge refunds what you chip. Force trades on Hookshot's cooldown the moment Heartsteel completes: Roar of the Slayer's armour shred first, then the charged Q knockup. At two items Soul Furnace absorbs most of her damage in a single trade, and your zombie form means any dive attempt ends with her taking damage from a corpse she cannot ignore. She still wins if she catches you isolated with W and R down, so never over-extend in the side lane without those available.","whys":["Neither kit has a decisive level-1 threat — her E hookshot is on a long cooldown and your W-shield is not yet online, so both sides auto-trade and farm.","W-shield online means her first Q true-damage proc gets absorbed; charge Q while her hookshot is down and she has no safe answer to the knockup.","Her full kit is online and Tactical Sweep's outer half tears your HP stack and heals her — she wins extended trades landing Q-auto, the far edge of W, then Q-auto.","Her hookshot cooldown drops with rank and she dashes wall-to-wall unpredictably — respect the E animation and never charge Q while she has a wall in hookshot range.","Her R drops a hex-arena that nullifies your R escape and isolates the 1v1 — she wins this window if your W-shield is on cooldown when she engages.","Heartsteel completion spikes your HP high enough that W-shield absorbs her burst budget; force trades on hookshot's cooldown with E-shred into charged Q.","Two-item HP total makes your zombie passive a genuine deterrent — she cannot dive you without risking the undead retaliation, and your W-shield value now exceeds her combo damage."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"cassiopeia","win":["Skill","Cassiopeia","Cassiopeia","Cassiopeia","Cassiopeia","Cassiopeia","Cassiopeia"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Before Miasma exists her poke is Noxious Blast on 3.5s and Twin Fang — charge from brush at level 1, because nothing can ground you yet."},{"when":"Miasma DOWN","text":"Miasma is 24s at rank 1 and 16s maxed — the instant the zone expires, Roar of the Slayer into a fully charged knockup is your whole kill window."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel completion means your HP passive starts compounding; Cassiopeia can no longer burst you in a single poison rotation and has to respect your zombie threat."},{"when":"Late","text":"Full-AP Cassiopeia's Twin Fang spam melts your HP pool faster than it regenerates — she owns sustained 1v1 DPS, so never solo-duel her at 2+ items; force her into your frontline."}],"wants":{"you":["Charge Q behind the wave or in brush — Cassiopeia cannot prevent the knockup if she cannot see the channel starting","Bait Miasma out to waste its 24s cooldown, then E-slow straight into the full-charge stun","Stack HP with passive kills and Heartsteel so the zombie reanimate is a genuine punish even when she wins trades"],"foe":["Drop Miasma under your feet so you are grounded out of Unstoppable Onslaught and stranded in poke range","Fire Twin Fang every 0.75s into poisoned targets, healing off the damage, so Soul Furnace never survives to its 3-second detonation","Sustain poison DPS from max range and deny ever giving you a single grounded window to charge Q safely"]},"early":"You cannot charge Q openly against Cassiopeia at level 1 — she has no Miasma yet, but her Twin Fang (E) poke punishes slow channels hard. Use the brush at the side of the lane or the wave itself as cover; if she steps up to last-hit a pushed wave she briefly leaves poke range and that is your only free charge window. W-shield absorbs one or two Twin Fang hits without meaningful HP loss, so do not panic-detonate it — hold it until you are ready to walk forward and she cannot poison through it fast enough to break even. Doran's Shield and a Magic Resist shard are mandatory; take Mercs if you expect Miasma to dominate the mid game.","mid":"Miasma (W) is the whole matchup from level 2 onward — Cassiopeia drops it at your feet the moment you step forward, and grounded means you cannot start Unstoppable Onslaught at all while you stand in it. The only correct trade is to charge Q from brush or fog, shred her step-up with Roar of the Slayer, and commit the instant the zone expires — it is 24s at rank 1 and 16s maxed, so that gap is real. Do not walk into a live cloud expecting to charge out of it. At level 6 your R is primarily a roam tool here: Petrifying Gaze stuns only enemies facing her, so a straight-line charge is the one approach she can always punish. Use it to reach another lane, not to all-in her top.","late":"At two items Twin Fang fires every 0.75s and heals her off each hit, so never solo duel her without Grievous Wounds already bought — anti-heal is the difference between a long trade and a free one for her. Your role is to force her into teamfights where your zombie passive and enormous HP pool zone her team while your carries clean up; she cannot kite five people. Your reanimated form also baits Petrifying Gaze away from your team's engage. Accept that she won the lane, but your frontline scaling wins the game if you play teamfight angles instead of trying to outright kill her.","whys":["Neither champion has their full kit — Cassiopeia lacks Miasma at level 1, so brush-angled Q charges are briefly possible, but she can still poke through Twin Fang spam without committing to melee range.","Miasma lands at 2 and grounds you, so you cannot start Unstoppable Onslaught inside it; every step forward is punished by poison, and it is only up every 24s at rank 1.","Her full three-spell combo (Q poison cloud, W Miasma zone, E Twin Fang) is now active; she pokes you through the wave and the grounding effect means your W-shield is your only real trading tool.","Levels 4-5 are a pure kite war — Cassiopeia has no need to step in range, and your Q charge is telegraphed enough that she simply repositions; farm under your tower and do not chase.","Petrifying Gaze only stuns enemies facing her — charging straight in is the one thing that guarantees it, so use Unstoppable Onslaught to roam, or approach with your back turned and eat the slow.","Heartsteel spikes your HP total but Cassiopeia's Twin Fang damage scales with her AP items simultaneously; the first-item duel still favours her sustained DPS and she can kite Heartsteel's proc range indefinitely.","Full-AP Cassiopeia's Twin Fang melts your HP pool in seconds at 2+ items and her Miasma denies any melee retaliation; never solo-duel her — force teamfights where your zombie passive and HP bulk zone her team."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"chogath","win":["Skill","Sion","Skill","Skill","Cho'Gath","Skill","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your W-shield absorbs Cho's Q-knockup follow-up and your charged Q stun punishes his locked animation — land the stun when Rupture is on cooldown and you win every trade at this window."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Feast lands on an 80s cooldown: flat true damage that armour and MR cannot touch, and each devoured minion makes him bigger — do not all-in at 6 without a clear health lead already banked."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel completed: your HP passive is compounding and W-shield value grows with your max HP, but Cho's first Heartsteel/Sunfire spike matches your bulk — the lane stays genuinely even in sustained fights."},{"when":"Late","text":"Fully grown, his Rupture-into-Feral Scream chain locks you out long enough for Feast to finish you — avoid low-HP skirmishes and play front-line rather than solo kills."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup when Rupture is on cooldown, then E-shred his armor and brawl with W-shield absorbing his return damage","Stack Heartsteel HP early so your W-shield detonation scales into a meaningful threat and Cho cannot poke you below all-in threshold","Force short trades around Rupture's cooldown window — never fight when the knockup is available because his silence into Q combo chunks harder than his stats suggest"],"foe":["Land Rupture (Q) to knockup you mid-charge so your Q whiffs harmlessly and he follows with W-silence to lock you out of the retaliate window","Devour a big minion or a scuttle with Feast every 80 seconds so his own max health keeps growing while yours stands still each cycle","Survive laning phase even at a CS deficit, knowing two Feast stacks and Sunfire start outpacing your bulk the moment team fights begin"]},"early":"Cho'Gath's Rupture (Q) is the only ability you genuinely fear in this lane — the knockup interrupts your charged Q mid-channel and Feral Scream immediately after silences you out of any answer. At level 1, farm at Q-tip range and watch his feet; Rupture has a visible telegraph delay and you can step out of the circle if you are not channeling. At level 2 Soul Furnace comes online and changes the math: let him walk forward for a last-hit, pop W, take the trade, and detonate it after the 3 seconds. Level 3 is your window — charged Q from brush or fog, land the stun in the gap after a spent Rupture, shred his armour with Roar of the Slayer, and brawl; Feral Scream is 11s at rank 1, so his lockout is not always ready.","mid":"Levels 4 through 5 the lane flattens — you both have your full basic kits, Rupture is the matchup-defining cast, and neither of you has a decisive item spike yet. Respect the circle religiously, but know it is a flat 6 seconds: you are trading inside the gap after a spent cast, not waiting on a long timer. At level 6 Cho gains Feast and the threat profile changes: it is flat true damage on 80 seconds, so neither armour nor magic resist reduces a point of it, while your raw health pool still does. Do not let him devour big minions for free — every one he eats grows his own health bar and raises his ceiling for the rest of the game. Your R is an engage tool here, not a kill button — close the gap once Rupture is spent, then land the full Q-E-W combo before Feral Scream silences you out of it.","late":"Two items in, both of you are enormous HP tanks. Feast hits for a flat chunk of true damage, so armour and magic resist buy you nothing against it — but the health you stack does, which is why the bulk plan still holds up here. Play for front-line engage value and your zombie passive rather than solo dueling: walk at his team, absorb CC with Soul Furnace, and let the passive deal damage after death. In splits you out-push him with R and a shove, but avoid over-extending while Feast is up — a chunked Sion is the one target it actually finishes. Win condition at this stage is team-fighting and siege, not 1v1 brawling.","whys":["Both champions are weak Level 1 with no meaningful all-in — farm safely and map where Rupture lands so you know its exact telegraph before you ever channel a Q.","W-shield comes online and absorbs Cho's return damage after your engage; step up when Rupture is on cooldown and force a short trade he cannot punish.","Full kit live — charged stun into Roar of the Slayer into a Soul Furnace brawl is your combo, but Rupture returns every 6s, so the clean window is genuinely rare.","Neither champion has a decisive spike yet and Rupture is the matchup-defining cooldown; both sides farm and trade strictly around its availability — even is the accurate read.","Feast comes online: flat true damage on 80s that armour and MR cannot reduce, and every minion he devours grows his own health bar — he owns this window while your first item is still unfinished.","Heartsteel completed on both sides: your W-shield and HP compound but so do his Feast stacks — neither side has a decisive edge and the lane stays split in extended fights.","Feast is flat true damage, so your HP pool still answers it even though armour does not; play for front-line and zombie-passive value rather than trying to solo-kill a fully grown Cho'Gath."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"darius","win":["Darius","Darius","Darius","Darius","Darius","Darius","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Hemorrhage stacks from any touch — at 5 he enrages, and Decimate's blade edge heals him, so hug him close and make the handle land instead. Apprehend is 26s at rank 1."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Darius's R becomes a true-damage execute at full Hemorrhage — do not fight into 5 stacks at low health even once you have Unstoppable Onslaught online."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel or Sunfire starts compounding your HP passive; your W-shield value is now large enough that a single blocked trade gives you a meaningful surplus against his bleed DoT."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your zombie passive makes killing you an active cost even for Darius — if he blows his R to execute you and your Passive zombie clears him or forces his flash, the 5-stack window he needed is gone."}],"wants":{"you":["Stay inside Decimate's handle radius so he heals nothing, with Soul Furnace up before Apprehend lands","Land a full charged-Q knockup to peel his pull cooldown and reset the trade on your terms","Reach first item with a meaningful CS lead so your HP passive out-bulks his bleed scaling"],"foe":["Pull you with Apprehend, then land Decimate's outer blade for the heal that wins the trade outright","Reset his autos with Crippling Strike to slow you out of range before he commits the pull","Execute you with R before your zombie Passive can contest and negate the kill value"]},"early":"Darius wins levels 1 through 3 cleanly — his passive bleed stacks from any touch, and his E pull at 5 stacks into a blade-edge Decimate is a kill threat Soul Furnace alone cannot eat at low HP totals. Your job is to keep stacks below 3: do not extend autos when Apprehend is up (26 seconds at rank 1), farm with Q tip from max range, and if he does close, stand inside him so Decimate catches you with the handle rather than the healing blade. Pop W the instant his pull animation fires. If you land a full charged-Q knockup before he closes, that stun buys time for Hemorrhage to fall off — but the telegraph is slow enough that he can step back out of the cone. One free blade-edge Decimate snowballs into an unkillable bleed window.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 remain Darius-favored if he built an HP lead, but his all-in damage no longer grows at the same rate and your W-shield starts absorbing a meaningful fraction of a 5-stack trade. At level 6 you gain Unstoppable Onslaught — the charge lets you gap-close from outside his E range, and if you R into him when his E is on cooldown you dictate the fight rather than reacting to his pull. His R execute still ends the fight if he stacks to 5 before you threaten the knockup, so the key habit is charging Q BEFORE his bleed reaches 3 stacks, not after — use E armor-shred to set up the stun rather than trading autos building his passive. This is when the lane becomes genuinely even: neither of you can freely win the all-in without hitting your setup first.","late":"By 2 items your Heartsteel HP total is large enough that Darius's Hemorrhage DoT becomes a small fraction of your effective health, and your W-shield can absorb the empowered Q on its own. More importantly, your zombie Passive turns every attempt to execute you into a contested window — he burns his Noxian Guillotine R to finish a kill, then a zombie Sion with E armor-shred and residual Q charges back at him while he is stationary and healing. In teamfights, Darius needs to stack 5 bleeds on a single target to get his R; your frontline HP wall forces him to commit to you specifically, burning his Apprehend pull while the rest of your team punishes his immobility. Splitpush with your R transit tool: Darius cannot answer a Sion push across the map at 2 items without burning TP, and you only need to be alive to threaten — your Passive zombie clears the wave even if he catches you.","whys":["Darius's passive bleed procs on any hit and starts stacking immediately — at level 1 he only needs a few autos to threaten the empowered Q heal window, so walk away from any trade he starts.","At level 2 he adds his E pull to the rotation; a full 5-stack pull into empowered Q at this stage chunks HP your W-shield cannot fully cover yet — dodge his pull animation rather than tanking it.","His full E-Q-auto bleed into empowered Q at level 3 wins a sustained trade before your W-shield value is large enough to absorb it; pop W on the pull animation and back out before 5 stacks.","Levels 4-5 still belong to Darius — Apprehend dictates every fight, and Decimate's blade edge heals him back through whatever Soul Furnace absorbs, so trade only at handle range.","Level 6 opens Unstoppable Onslaught as your gap-close tool and your zombie Passive gives every death trade value, making his R execute less clean — neither of you auto-wins the all-in here.","Heartsteel compounds your HP, but his first item lands too and Apprehend's passive armour penetration means the stat does not answer him yet — buy Grievous Wounds before you commit.","At full build your zombie Passive means Darius spends his Noxian Guillotine R to end you and still faces 8 seconds of zombie combat; your HP total makes his Hemorrhage a small damage fraction and you win the war of attrition."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"drmundo","win":["Dr. Mundo","Dr. Mundo","Dr. Mundo","Dr. Mundo","Dr. Mundo","Dr. Mundo","Dr. Mundo"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught closes the gap, but his passive absorbs the first immobilise — land a stun first, then the R knockup sticks."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Morellos or Thornmail online means your Q-E-W combo actually sticks through his passive regen; without anti-heal you're running water into a bucket."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Mundo's Q cleavers chunk you for percent-current-HP from outside charged-Q range — he can freely dictate the poke war while you can't retaliate."},{"when":"Late","text":"Once Mundo completes two tank items and presses R, his Maximum Dosage heals back more than your entire trade in seconds — you have no percent-HP damage to punish the stack."}],"wants":{"you":["Burn his passive cannister with one stun first, because the second immobilise is the one that actually holds him long enough for the kill","Rush anti-heal (Morellos or Thornmail) so his passive regen and heart-zapper sustain don't negate every trade you win","Use Unstoppable Onslaught at Level 6 to commit an all-in before he reaches his late-game healing threshold"],"foe":["Throw Q cleavers at max range to chunk your HP below the safe-trade threshold — percent-current-HP poke compounds every wave","Stack tank items until his R Maximum Dosage heals so much that no amount of trading pressure can kill him","Stall the game out to two-plus items where his regen flatly outpaces any damage you can output without percent-HP tools"]},"early":"At Level 1 Mundo's Q cleaver does percent-current-HP damage from a range your charged Q cannot answer without telegraphing the channel — last-hit at Q-tip range instead of walking into free cleavers. His passive also resists the first immobilising effect and drops a cannister he can pick up, so assume your first stun does nothing. Pop Soul Furnace when he throws a cleaver during your charge; it absorbs the burst and lets you finish the knockup. By Level 3 he owns the poke war: Infected Bonesaw is on 4 seconds, so there is one waiting every time you walk up, and an aware Mundo simply steps back from the charge. Do not force a brawl you are not landing first.","mid":"Level 6 is your best window in this lane and you should use it aggressively: R into him from off-screen, spend a stun on his passive cannister, then land the real knockup, shred armour with Roar of the Slayer, and dump Soul Furnace into the brawl. Blunt Force Trauma resets his auto, so his wind-up is the cue to step back, not the cast. If Maximum Dosage is down you have a real kill shot; if he presses it, disengage and bank the trade. That cooldown is a flat 120 seconds at every rank, so you get a genuine window every two minutes. Anti-heal must be in your build before any extended trade; without it his regen heals back the whole charged-Q trade.","late":"Two-plus items Mundo with his R available is genuinely difficult for Sion to kill — Maximum Dosage heals for a massive chunk of max HP and you have zero percent-HP damage in your kit to punish his stacking. Your job in teamfights is to land R-engage into charged-Q knockups on their carries, not to duel Mundo; let your team deal his damage while you zone with your zombie passive. In a prolonged side-lane 1v1 against a full-build Mundo who has already activated R, you will not kill him — shove the wave, back out, and take the tower trade. The matchup is winnable in draft if you pick up anti-heal early and snowball the Level 6 window; it is a slow bleed if you let him free-farm into his second tank item without ever converting the Unstoppable Onslaught.","whys":["Both champions are weak at Level 1 — Mundo's Q cleaver hits for percent-current-HP from range but your W-shield absorbs the trade; stay at Q-tip distance and neither side gets an edge.","Level 2 is still a poke skirmish — his Q comes out faster than your charge time, so W-shield a cleaver and look for a short trade rather than committing a full channel.","By Level 3 Mundo's Q rotation lets him throw a cleaver on nearly every approach — your charged Q is too slow to answer safely, and he freely wins the health war from range.","Levels 4-5 are his lane: without your ultimate you have no reliable way to close the distance before he throws another cleaver, so you are CS-ing into a slow poke-down with no retaliation tool.","Level 6 is your best window, but Goes Where He Pleases resists your first immobilise — spend the Q stun to pop the cannister, then knock him up with R before Maximum Dosage lands.","First-item Morellos or Thornmail means his Heart Zapper and passive regen no longer fully undo each Q-E trade — both champions spike here, making sustained trades a genuine coin-flip.","Two-plus items Mundo pressing R heals back a massive portion of his HP in seconds and you carry zero percent-HP damage; teamfight as an engage anchor and leave the Mundo-kill to your teammates."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"fiora","win":["Fiora","Fiora","Fiora","Skill","Fiora","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Fiora's passive vitals proc percent-max-HP true damage from the moment minions spawn — every trade she tags a vital and your HP pool works against you."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Grand Challenge unlocks her full vital rotation, giving her a heal aura over your body if she completes all four — her lethality ceiling jumps sharply here."},{"when":"1st item","text":"With Heartsteel or Sunfire compounding your HP passive, your W-shield value and zombie threat reach the point where she can no longer safely duel you after her Riposte is burned."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your max-HP pool becomes so large that even vital true damage is slow to kill you, and your zombie passive denies her the clean exit she needs — you decisively out-sustain her attrition damage."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait her Riposte (W) with a fake Q charge, then immediately recast the real charged Q to land the knockup while she is locked in the parry animation","Use E to shred her armor before every extended trade — her base resistances are low and the shred compounds your Q burst","Keep rotating your position between trades so her passive vitals spawn on difficult-to-reach sides, denying her the damage that makes short trades punishing"],"foe":["Tag all four vitals in a single trade window using Q dashes to reposition and proc whichever vital Sion is not covering","Parry the charged Decimating Smash so Riposte upgrades to a stun and his CC window becomes her kill window","Complete Grand Challenge (R) by hitting all four vitals to trigger the healing aura, negating Sion's zombie passive and turning kills into her net gain"]},"early":"At level 1 her passive immediately spawns a vital — usually on your back or side — and her Q dash lets her arc around your body to collect it for percent-true-damage before you can land any meaningful damage in return. Respect the vital indicator: rotate your position constantly so she cannot simply Q straight to the glowing spot. Do not attempt a full charged-Q at levels 1 and 2; the wind-up telegraphs your intent and she will Riposte (W) the moment your Q flashes charged, stunning you and reversing the trade entirely. Use short W-shield pops to absorb her auto-vital burst and farm safely, accepting that she wins these exchanges on paper.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are a genuine skill check: your kit is complete but so is hers, and the vital true damage is now hitting a larger HP pool — which means each proc hurts more in absolute terms. Focus on learning her Riposte timing. The tell is her W animation starting as your Q begins glowing; if you see it, release Q early for a short-range non-charged slam to bait it, then immediately re-charge for the real knockup. At level 6 her Grand Challenge gives her a heal aura if she completes all four vitals over your body — if she pops R on you, W-shield immediately, back off, and do not let her finish the sequence. Deny the heal or you give her a free kill convert.","late":"This is where the matchup flips. Your Heartsteel stacks and max-HP passive compound into a pool so large that Fiora's vital damage becomes a slow grind — she cannot burst you in a single rotation. Use Roar of the Slayer's armour shred in extended fights, and have Grievous Wounds bought before you start one, because her vitals and Grand Challenge both heal her back. Glory in Death forces her to keep fighting after your death or surrender the wave, draining her even on trades she wins. At 2+ items you are nearly unkillable in a straight brawl and can R to close if she disengages once Riposte is spent. The lane is decided by whose execution is cleaner.","whys":["Her passive spawns a vital immediately, and even though Lunge is 13s at rank 1 she only needs one to reach it — charged Q is far too slow to punish that clean walk-up.","Two vitals and her Q repositioning mean every short trade deals true damage to your growing HP pool; W-shield absorbs some burst but she still wins on paper.","With all three basics online, Riposte turns your own stun against you — it only becomes a stun when it blocks CC, and Bladework's second hit crits. She out-trades you here.","Neither of you has a clean advantage: your W-shield blunts her burst and you can fake-charge Q to probe her Riposte, but she can still collect vitals off spacing mistakes — it reads as a coin-flip.","Grand Challenge is 110s and reveals all four vitals; complete the set over you and it heals her and every ally nearby, which erases your zombie value — her ceiling jumps sharply at 6.","Heartsteel and your HP passive begin compounding; your W-shield value scales with your growing pool and her vitals become a slower grind — you survive trades she previously won decisively.","Your max-HP total is now so large that vital true damage is outpaced by your W-shield and zombie regen; with her Riposte burned you can Q-stun, E-shred, and out-sustain her attrition in the final brawl."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"galio","win":["Sion","Sion","Skill","Sion","Galio","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"Your W-shield comes online — Galio's Q poke and passive magic auto deal magic damage, so the shield soaks the trade and lets you charge Q back."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Galio is front-loaded — with Winds of War (9s) and Shield of Durand (16s) spent he has only Justice Punch left; charge Q to full inside that window for a free knockup."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hero's Entrance (180s at rank 1) makes him a map threat, not a duel threat — the lane danger stays Shield of Durand: taunted, you auto-walk into his Q, so pre-shield first."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel pushes your HP so high that Galio's magic-damage kit can barely dent your W-shield value — every trade from this point on costs him more than you."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup when Galio's Justice Punch (E) is on cooldown","Pre-pop W-shield before stepping into Galio's taunt (W) range so he can't force you to auto into his Q","Use E armor-shred right after the knockup to maximize your follow-up damage before he can disengage"],"foe":["Land his W taunt to force you to auto-walk into his Q poke and passive magic auto","Use Justice Punch (E) to interrupt your Q charge animation and knock you up before yours lands","Shove the wave under his tower so your slow Q charge has no minion cover to hide behind"]},"early":"Level 1 is yours on paper — Galio has only Q poke and his passive magic auto, neither of which threatens a Sion who stands behind his minions. Step up to last-hit with W ready, not committed; his Q has a clear animation so you can sidestep it. The moment you hit Level 2, the dynamic shifts in your favor: his Q poke deals magic damage and your W-shield soaks it directly, letting you absorb his trade and immediately charge Q back from brush angles. Never channel Q in the open on this lane — Galio's Justice Punch will interrupt your charge and flip the CC onto you. Play toward the river brush, fake a step-up, and release the knockup when he commits his E.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are the cleanest window in this matchup. Galio's kit has no reliable follow-up damage once Q and W are both spent — hold a nearly-full Q charge near the wave, force him to use Justice Punch to peel or engage, then step in for the stun and immediately E to shred his armor before he re-positions. His W taunt has a tight range: stay just outside it during Q charge so he can not force the auto-walk. At Level 6, Galio's Hero's Entrance is global and nearly irrelevant in the 1v1, but his W taunt becomes more dangerous if he is tanky enough to survive your combo — pre-shield with W before any Level 6 trade and do not hard-engage unless his W is visibly on cooldown.","late":"With Heartsteel and a second tank item your HP total makes Galio's magic-damage kit feel like poke — his Q poke and passive auto scale with AP he is not building, and your W-shield scales with your rising max HP. Force side-lane skirmishes and use Unstoppable Onslaught to engage teamfights from the flank: if Galio wastes his W taunt on a frontliner, he has nothing left to peel your charge. The one thing to respect all game is the Q-into-W taunt string — if Galio catches you mid-Q-charge with his taunt, you walk forward auto-attacking through his full burst window. Track his W before every commit, keep your W-shield banked, and the matchup stays firmly in your column.","whys":["Galio has only Winds of War and Colossal Smash at 1 — the passive is an auto reset, so its wind-up is your cue to step back; behind minions, out-trade him on base HP.","Your W-shield soaks Galio's magic Q poke directly, so taking a trade at Level 2 costs him more than you — angle for a charged Q from brush before he can Justice Punch your channel.","Both kits are fully online but Galio's Q and W are telegraphed and slow — the stun race is genuinely even here, so trade only when you have wave cover for the Q charge.","Bait Justice Punch (E) — at 8-9s down it is his only exit from your charge; the moment it is gone, hold Q to full for the knockup and follow with Roar of the Slayer's armour shred.","Hero's Entrance is 180s at rank 1 and buys map pressure, not lane pressure — the duel threat stays Shield of Durand, so pre-shield with W before stepping in or you auto-walk into his Q.","Heartsteel's HP spike pushes your W-shield value past Galio's magic damage output — every trade from first item onward is a resource trade you are winning.","Two items in, your zombie passive and raw HP make every trade, including death, a net positive — run the standard knockup-E-shred pattern and out-scale his static magic-damage kit."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"gangplank","win":["Sion","Sion","Sion","Skill","Gangplank","Gangplank","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Your charged Q knockup is Gangplank's nightmare early — he has no dash to dodge it and your W-shield eats his barrel-chain poke damage."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Gangplank's Cannon Barrage goes global and his W (Remove Scurvy) purges your E slow, letting him disengage or back safely — respect his escape tools."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP compounding tips fights decisively in Gangplank's favour: his barrels crit harder and his W buys him poke windows you can't punish at range."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your W-shield scales with your massive HP stack and your zombie passive becomes a genuine kill threat — re-engage and force fights he cannot barrel-kite away from."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup while Remove Scurvy (W) is down — it is his only cleanse, and at rank 1 it is gone for 22s","Buy Grievous Wounds early — Remove Scurvy heals back every trade you win, and it is up every 14-22s","Use R to close distance when he kites backward or to roam before he can Cannon Barrage a fight open"],"foe":["Chain barrel pokes to chip your HP below W-shield value before committing to a real trade","Save W (Remove Scurvy) to cleanse your E slow the instant you try to lock him down with a charged Q","Scale into crit builds and pressure you through global Cannon Barrage so you cannot trade safely mid-lane"]},"early":"Your W-shield eats his entire barrel-chain combo early — barrel damage at levels 1-3 is low enough that one detonation barely dents a fresh Sion shield. Walk forward when he places a barrel, take the poke on the shield, and start charging Q while he repositions. He has no dash, so a fully charged Q at level 1 is a free knockup-into-auto if he misreads your wind-up. At level 2 your E slows him through his W cooldown window — if his Remove Scurvy is on cooldown from a recent heal, walk in, E to shred armor and apply slow, and follow with charged Q. The pattern is: bait his W first, then commit the combo.","mid":"At levels 4-5 the lane drifts toward Skill because his barrels start to genuinely hurt and his W lets him shrug off your E slow just as you try to lock him down — if Remove Scurvy is up, your combo simply does not connect. At level 6 the dynamic flips further in his favor: Cannon Barrage gives him global pressure so he backs safely, pokes you at barrel range, and never has to stand still for your charged Q. Use R to collapse on him if he over-extends while his barrels are on cooldown, but respect that W removes your E slow the moment you need it most.","late":"Past two items your W-shield is enormous — thousands of HP mean the detonation trades favorably even in a sustained poke-fest, and your zombie passive forces him to respect every dive you make. He cannot simply kite a Sion with this much max HP without taking a charged Q knockup eventually. Force close-range fights by using R to engage across his barrels rather than walking into them; once in melee range his kit has no real answer to your bulk. Win the 1v1 split and convert your HP advantage into tower pressure before his barrel crits and global ult create too many angles.","whys":["His barrel poke is weak before he stacks gold and your W-shield blankets the damage — walk up, take a hit on the shield, and start charging Q while he has no escape tool to dodge it.","W-shield absorbs his follow-up barrel chain and your E comes online — bait his Remove Scurvy heal, then E slow into charged Q for the first real trade win.","All three abilities are live: E shreds armor and slows, charged Q knocks him up with no dash to dodge, and W-shield eats his counter-poke — he cannot win this window without his W up.","His barrels crit harder now and Remove Scurvy purges your E slow on demand, making your full combo unreliable — farm safely and only commit when you see his W go on cooldown.","Cannon Barrage goes global: he backs safely, pokes from barrel range, and uses W to cleanse any attempted lockdown — he dictates the pace and you cannot R-engage without walking into barrels.","His first crit item means barrels genuinely threaten your HP even through the shield, and Remove Scurvy keeps him safe from every E-slow attempt — respect his range and wait for his W cooldown.","Your Heartsteel HP stack makes your W-shield a massive burst of magic damage and your zombie passive guarantees trading value even if he kills you — force melee range with R and brawl where his barrels cannot help him."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"gnar","win":["Skill","Gnar","Gnar","Skill","Skill","Gnar","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Mini-Gnar's boomerang hits hardest before you have W-shield stacked — he hammers your HP with %max-HP damage every time it returns through you."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"GNAR! (90s at rank 1) is his kill window — transform while you are low and one wall-bounce stun buys a full combo, so fight away from walls."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Your Heartsteel passive begins compounding — but Gnar's boomerang scales with your growing HP, so build Bami's Cinder first for the armor and engage threat."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"At two tank items your HP passive creates a zombie threat even when dying, and his percent-HP damage loses potency relative to your regeneration and shield bulk."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup on mini-Gnar before his Rage bar fills to force him out of transform","Use W-shield to eat the returning boomerang hit and immediately trade back while the CD is up","All-in him during his Mega-Gnar cooldown window when his transform bar is empty and he's melee range"],"foe":["Kite in mini form and let the boomerang pass through you twice for double percent-HP damage","Fill his Rage bar by taking small trades and then transform mid-fight for the GNAR! AOE stun","Hop (E) over your charged Q when you telegraph the channel, then counter-trade from safety"]},"early":"Level 1 is a coin flip — if you can get an early charged Q on him before he learns your telegraph it hurts, but mini-Gnar's boomerang (Q) returns through you on the way back, dealing percent-max-HP physical damage twice per throw. At level 2 he gets his hop (E) and can jump your charge entirely. Level 3 is his strongest window: boomerang poke plus the ability to build Rage quickly on short trades, setting up a transform all-in. Your W-shield is online but his percent-HP damage punches through it harder than a flat-damage opponent would — absorb one hit with W, then look to retaliate on the boomerang's cooldown rather than eating the return.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are a positioning game: mini-Gnar wants to keep you at boomerang range and fill his Rage bar, you want him in charged-Q range with no hop available. Watch his Rage meter — a full or near-full bar means Mega is seconds away and you must either disengage or have a charged Q ready to use his landing stun as your engage. At level 6 you gain R for all-in and escape, but he gains reliable Mega-Gnar access. Trade into him only when his transform bar is near-empty; if he goes Mega while you're extended and mid-channel on Q, his GNAR! stun interrupts everything.","late":"Two-item Sion is the lane's power shift: your HP passive reaches the point where his boomerang's percent damage is outpaced by your shield bulk and zombie threat, and a landed charged Q into E-shred into R all-in is a genuine kill threat even on a kite-focused champion. However, if you are behind in HP items or he has two full items of his own, his ranged poke and hop mobility keep the lane EVEN rather than flipped — you still need to land the stun to win the trade. Use R as a gap-closer through minions to close the distance he kites behind, not as an engage from far range where he can hop clear.","whys":["Level 1 Sion charged-Q damage is real but mini-Gnar can simply walk out of the telegraph — trade only if you catch him standing still at the boomerang's return path.","Boomerang Throw hits going out and coming back — 16s at rank 1 unless he catches it, and he wins Level 2 anyway because W cannot shield both halves.","At level 3 he can combo boomerang into hop (E) into follow-up auto, filling his Rage bar fast; his transform all-in outpowers your W-shield before it stacks up.","Both of you wait: he fills Rage, you want Hop gone — it is down 22s at rank 1, and that gap is the only time your charged Q is not simply jumped.","Your R is online but his Mega-Gnar GNAR! AOE stun is equally live; all-ins are mutual kill threats and whoever mis-times the transform window loses the fight.","First item Gnar still has percent-HP boomerang slamming into a now-larger HP pool — the ratio doesn't flip yet, and his hop keeps him out of your charged-Q range.","At two tank items your HP passive and zombie threat tilt the ratio: his boomerang damage doesn't scale fast enough to kill you before you land a charged Q knockup and brawl him down."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"gragas","win":["Skill","Gragas","Skill","Gragas","Gragas","Skill","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Gragas's Body Slam (E) into Barrel Roll (Q) combo comes online — his CC chain and AP burst easily win short trades before your W-shield value is reliable."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Gragas's Explosive Cask (R) can knock you away from him or into his team at the worst moment — your all-in threat with Unstoppable Onslaught is countered by his displacement."},{"when":"1st item","text":"The matchup equalises around Heartsteel or Sunfire — your W-shield absorbs more of each trade and your HP passive starts compounding into a meaningful stat lead."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your HP pool and W-shield scaling dwarf Gragas's damage output — he cannot kill you in a sustained fight and your zombie passive punishes every attempt to finish you off."}],"wants":{"you":["W-shield his Body Slam (E) into Barrel Roll (Q) combo before retaliating with a charged Q stun","Bait his Explosive Cask (R) cooldown before committing your Unstoppable Onslaught (R) engage","Stack HP with Heartsteel and your passive to reach the 2+ items window where he cannot kill you"],"foe":["Land his Body Slam (E) to proc his Barrel Roll (Q) and win every short trade in the first six levels","Use Explosive Cask (R) to knock you away from tower or into his team to deny your all-in windows","Drink Drunken Rage (5s) for the damage reduction and heal off Happy Hour, grinding your W-shield down between your cooldowns"]},"early":"At Level 1 the lane is a coin flip — neither of you has meaningful kill pressure yet, but Gragas's Q puddle out-ranges your melee threat, so hug the lane boundary and farm without eating free Barrel Rolls. The danger window opens at Level 2 when he can chain Body Slam into Q: his E closes gap and sets up the puddle slow, turning a short trade into a one-sided burst. Pop your W-shield the moment he commits the Body Slam — it absorbs a significant chunk of his Q detonation and lets you answer with a charged Q stun while his E is on a 13-second cooldown. Never fight inside his Q puddle voluntarily; the slow ensures he wins every extended trade at this stage.","mid":"Levels 4 through 6 are Gragas's best window and you need to respect it fully. His Body Slam cooldown is low enough to fish for combo trades repeatedly, and at Level 6 his Explosive Cask (R) adds a displacement tool that either saves him from your charged-Q follow-up or pockets you into his team for a punish. Play for safe CS and use your E armor shred only when you've landed the charged-Q stun first — never lead with E because it takes you into his melee range without CC. If his R is down, that is your all-in window: R forward to cancel his escape, land charged Q, then E for the armor shred and brawl. Without his displacement he cannot disengage from your zombie passive.","late":"Past two items this matchup belongs to you entirely. Your W-shield absorbs a meaningful fraction of his AP combo, your HP passive has compounded into a stat lead he cannot match with bruiser items, and your zombie form after death deals chip damage that forces him to disengage rather than clean up. He has no percent-HP damage and no true damage, so every additional tank item you build shrinks his trade window further. Force extended brawls on your terms — walk at him with R or charged-Q threat, use E armor shred mid-fight, and accept that he cannot finish you before your zombie activates. He relies on displacement to stay relevant; deny him clean separation angles and the lane is yours to close.","whys":["Neither champion has reliable kill pressure at Level 1 — farm safely and avoid his Barrel Roll (Q) puddle at the outer range of your Q charge.","Body Slam (E) into Barrel Roll (Q) is live, and the Drunken Rage sip is the tell — step back on the drink, not on the cask.","You can W-shield the Body Slam combo now and answer with a charged Q stun inside the 14s his E is down — trades are finally two-sided.","His E-Q cycle is fast enough to fish repeatedly and your charged Q is predictable; respect his combo and trade only after absorbing his engage with W.","His Explosive Cask (R) displacement adds a third dimension — he can peel your all-in or pocket you into his team; only engage when his R is confirmed down.","Both spike items are coming online simultaneously — your W-shield absorbs more each trade and his burst starts falling short of your growing HP pool, making the lane genuinely even again.","Your HP passive, W-shield scaling, and zombie passive combine into a sustained-fight profile Gragas cannot overcome — he has no percent-HP or true damage to punish your stack."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"garen","win":["Garen","Garen","Garen","Garen","Garen","Garen","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Your full kit is online — E-shred into a fully charged Q knockup is your first real window to punish his passive regen before it ticks back in."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Demacian Justice is true damage on a 120s cooldown at rank 1 — stay above the execute line, and all-in only in the window after he has spent it."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP stacks begin compounding and your W-shield value scales with your max HP — force extended trades he cannot win in his tanky-but-limited kit."},{"when":"Late","text":"Both scale into late tanks, so the lane stays contested — your zombie passive guarantees trading value even in death, while Garen's R missing-HP true damage punishes you if you fight too low."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup when Garen steps up to trade and his Courage W is down","W-shield his empowered Q silence and E spin so he can't burst you out of your own combo","Force extended brawls where your HP passive and max-HP W-shield out-value his passive regen"],"foe":["Break your E slow with Decisive Strike (8s), silence you, then spin E through you","Pop Courage W to cut incoming damage and trade short before disengaging to passive-regen back","Threaten Demacian Justice execute when you're low to deny your zombie value and force you to back early"]},"early":"Level 1 is a respect game on both sides — Garen wants to slap you with an empowered Q silence and disengage, and you want to land a charged Q knockup. The problem is that his Q silence can cancel your Q charge mid-cast if he hits you during the hold, so you cannot telegraph the charge in his face. Use the wave and brush angles to hide the charge, then commit only when you see him step forward without his Courage W ready. Your W-shield absorbs his opening E spin cleanly if you pre-pop it the moment he Q-dashes toward you, which turns a trade he expects to win into a roughly even exchange. By level 3 you have all three tools: charge Q from fog, E-shred his armor as he's knocked up, then burst the W-shield for extra damage — that sequence punishes his immobility and is your first true window to take a kill if his Courage W is on cooldown.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are the quietest stretch for you — Garen can play passive-regen denial, stepping up only when his Q silence is ready, and you lack the mobility to force a fight he doesn't want. Track his Courage W cooldown (22 seconds at rank 1 — a very long gap) and only charge your Q when that window is open. At level 6 you gain Unstoppable Onslaught for all-in threat and the ability to deny his regen reset by chasing him to his turret, but his Demacian Justice now becomes a real deterrent — never give him a sub-40% HP target or he can execute through your W-shield. Trade when he's above 60% HP and your R is up so his execute math doesn't pencil out.","late":"Late game this lane produces two frontline tanks of similar bulk, and neither truly dominates. Your HP passive has been stacking all game off minion kills and your W-shield is enormous by this point, but Garen's passive regen, Courage W damage reduction, and tenacity make him surprisingly durable in drawn-out fights too. The decisive edge goes to whoever lands their CC first in a full-team engagement — your charged Q knockup into E-shred sets up your whole team, while Garen's Q silence can shut down an important cast at a critical moment. Your zombie passive is the hidden separator: even if Garen's R executes you at low HP, your reanimated corpse deals residual damage that can change a teamfight, giving you real value in death that his kit simply doesn't have.","whys":["Perseverance only regenerates while he is untouched and Courage is 22s at rank 1 — chip him with autos to switch the regen off and deny the free Q silence.","Your W-shield comes online and now absorbs his E spin, turning his favored short-trade pattern into a break-even or better exchange for you.","Full kit online: hide the Q charge, land the knockup, E-shred his armor while he's stunned — this three-ability sequence is your first clean kill window if his Courage W is down.","Garen pokes with silencing Qs and then disengages to regen; neither kit has a decisive spike here, so track Courage (22s at rank 1) and only engage inside that gap.","Garen's Demacian Justice execute threat goes live — never fight below half HP, and use your Unstoppable Onslaught to chase him to his turret and deny his passive reset.","Heartsteel HP stacks compound and your W-shield scales with max HP — force the long brawl here, where his kit has no sustain mechanic to match your bulk or your W-shield regeneration cycle.","Both are durable late-tank threats; the edge is positional — your zombie passive guarantees trade value even when his R executes you, a factor his kit cannot replicate in a teamfight."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"graves","win":["Sion","Skill","Graves","Graves","Skill","Graves","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Your Level 1 charged Q out-ranges Graves's E dash — land the knockup before his Quickdraw is online and you set the tone for the whole lane."},{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Graves stacks Quickdraw armor and kites your charge telegraph freely — respect the dash window or you eat a full shotgun burst for nothing."},{"when":"1st item","text":"His first item lands before Heartsteel stacks and Collateral Damage is 100s at rank 1 — his burst outpaces your HP pool."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Heartsteel compound interest and W-shield scaling make you genuinely unkillable; his shotgun autos stop mattering and your zombie passive denies him clean exits."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a charged Q knockup before Graves commits Quickdraw so his armor-stack window is wasted","Keep a minion between you — his four bullets stop on the first unit and never reach you","Reach 2+ items with a Heartsteel stack lead so your HP pool and W-shield outvalue his burst"],"foe":["Quickdraw (E) into your charge telegraph to dodge the knockup and punish you standing still","Stack Quickdraw armour and kite through the 3 seconds before your W detonates","Burst you below half HP with shotgun autos so your Passive zombie is the only fight value you get"]},"early":"At Level 1 you are the aggressor — Graves has no Quickdraw yet and your charged Q telegraph is the scariest thing on the map. Walk up along the wave edge, start charging Q as he last-hits, and release the full knockup the moment he steps in range; a Level 1 knockup into E armor-shred sets a lead he cannot erase. Level 2 becomes a coin flip: he now has Quickdraw and can dodge your charge by dashing through you, so only commit to a trade if he wastes the dash on a creep or tries to poke with his W smoke. By Level 3 his Quickdraw cooldown is short enough and his armor is stacking — the window where you freely charge your Q has closed, and you need to respect that he will dodge it and return a full shotgun burst while you are standing still.","mid":"Levels 4 through 6 are his best stretch: every time you start channeling Q, Graves can Quickdraw sideways and punish your standstill with buckshot autos that shred through your W-shield faster than you detonate it. Keep the wave frozen near your tower so he must walk to you — don't give him a free angle to side-step in open lane. At Level 6 your R gives you an escape line and gank-setup tool, but Graves's Collateral Damage also opens up punishing overextensions; treat this as a genuinely even power-check stage and only use R Unstoppable Onslaught as an engage when his Quickdraw is down and the angle is clean. Avoid charging Q into open terrain where he can E freely — the brush between top-side scryer and the wave is your friend.","late":"At two items your Heartsteel stack count and W-shield value dwarf his burst output — he needs two full Quickdraw dashes and a Collateral Damage to threaten you, and your zombie passive means even a kill costs him half his HP. Splitpush aggressively on a side lane and let your R Unstoppable Onslaught ferry you to objectives; Graves cannot duel the 2-item Sion in a straight fight. If the game goes long, his damage falls off against your raw HP wall faster than most ranged skirmishers — this is the payoff window the whole lane was building toward, so bank your Heartsteel stacks and force 1v1s on the side lane where his kite space is limited by terrain.","whys":["Graves has no Quickdraw at Level 1 — a clean charged Q knockup into E armor-shred is free and sets a lead he cannot reverse with a dash he doesn't have yet.","Quickdraw is up and dodges your charge — 16s at rank 1, but his autos cut it down, so trade only in the beat after he burns it.","Quickdraw armor stacking and free kite angles mean your charge telegraph is a liability; he punishes every slow channel with a full shotgun burst.","He kites your W-shield detonation freely in the open lane and his Quickdraw CD is short — freeze the wave and stop giving him open-terrain commit opportunities.","Your R Unstoppable Onslaught opens an engage and escape threat, but Collateral Damage matches it as a punish — only all-in when Quickdraw is explicitly down.","His first item burst outpaces your unfinished Heartsteel stack pool; defer extended trades until the item gap closes on your side.","Two-item Heartsteel Sion: your HP total and W-shield value outscale his buckshot output entirely — splitpush, force 1v1s, and your Passive zombie denies him clean exits."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"gwen","win":["Skill","Gwen","Gwen","Skill","Gwen","Gwen","Gwen"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"You can threaten a fully charged Q knockup at level 1, but Gwen's Q true damage procs and E dash mean she can punish you mid-charge if she reads the telegraph."},{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Snip Snip!'s centre is true damage and Skip 'n Slash (13s, partly refunded when it connects) arrives — she out-trades you here, and from outside her mist you cannot target her at all."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Needlework (120s at rank 1) fires up to three times for ranged poke; Unstoppable Onslaught still catches her, but Skip 'n Slash sidesteps it if she reads the angle."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"At Riftmaker and Nashor's Tooth, Gwen's scissors proc enough percent-max-HP true damage per trade to erase your entire HP passive investment — your win condition is never this late."}],"wants":{"you":["Step inside Hallowed Mist — from within it she is targetable again and your charged Q lands","Use E armor-shred before every extended trade so your raw HP bulk actually converts into kill pressure","Buy Grievous Wounds — A Thousand Cuts heals her off every auto, straight through your HP pool"],"foe":["Pop Hallowed Mist (22s) so your Q cannot touch her while you stand outside it","Stack percent-max-HP true damage from her Q scissors against your HP-stacking build so your bulk actively hurts you","Kite with E dash and needle pokes from safe range, never letting you close into a sustained brawl"]},"early":"At level 1 you have genuine threat — a fully charged Q knockup can punish her if she greeds for CS, but Gwen's Q true damage ticks and E mobility mean she punishes you mid-charge the moment she reads the windup. Farm with the tip of Q and accept that she will win most trades if she plays correctly. Your W-shield is your lifeline: pop it to absorb her scissors burst, then disengage rather than extending into a losing brawl. Level 2 makes things harder — she gets her Hallowed Mist and can block your charged Q stun from inside it, stripping your only real engage tool in a straight trade.","mid":"Levels 4 through 6 are the most dangerous stretch. You have no power spike yet and she is comfortably poking with scissors while hiding in mist any time you threaten a charged Q. At level 6 your Unstoppable Onslaught gives you a legitimate all-in threat — the charge is hard to react to — but Gwen can sidestep with her E dash if she has distance or anticipates your angle. Use the R to catch her off Mist cooldown, not to chase into it. Prioritize W-shield trades to chip her health rather than going all-in, and look to stack HP kills in lane to bank your passive stacks for later.","late":"By first item Gwen's Riftmaker stacks percent-max-HP damage on every scissors proc, and your Heartsteel HP passive becomes a liability — the bigger you are, the more each tick hurts. The matchup flips decisively here: your W-shield value does not scale fast enough to offset her omnivamp-fuelled sustain plus true damage output. Your only realistic path is to have converted a lead earlier with roam kills off Unstoppable Onslaught, or to force a split scenario where your team can engage elsewhere. In a straight 1v1 at 2 items, respect her win condition and never let her scissors freely stack inside the mist.","whys":["Both kits are level 1 capable but neither dominates — Gwen's Q procs true damage on approach, your Q knockup punishes her CS greed; the outcome is coin-flip positioning.","Gwen's E dash is online and her scissors begin stacking percent-max-HP true damage — she wins the short trade and her mist arrives to block your charged Q stun.","Hallowed Mist is 22s at rank 1 and she is untargetable to you only from outside it — the way in is to walk into the mist yourself and swing from inside.","You have your full basic kit but no Onslaught threat yet; she has mist, scissors, and E mobility — neither can cleanly close, so the lane sits even if you play defensively.","Her Needlework poke adds long-range percent-HP damage from outside your charged Q range; your R gives you an engagement angle but she can sidestep with E if she reads the vector.","Riftmaker's percent-max-HP empowerment comes online here — your HP passive stacks begin working against you, and she sustains through your W-shield with omnivamp.","Full AP Gwen scissors erase your HP investment every trade; your passive zombie threat is irrelevant when she can safely poke from scissors range and re-enter mist on demand."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"heimerdinger","win":["Sion","Skill","Skill","Heimerdinger","Sion","Heimerdinger","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"A fully charged Q from brush at level 1 instantly removes Heimerdinger from his turret nest and chunked him before he can place a second one."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught closes the gap before Heimerdinger can kite or E-stun — the unstoppable tag means his grenade cannot interrupt the charge."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Three turrets plus Micro-Rockets at 1325 range is his peak, and Hextech Affinity speeds him up inside the nest you have to walk into."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heimerdinger's Luden's or Shadowflame spike gives his turrets enough AP to shred your W-shield in seconds, making every all-in attempt significantly riskier."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup to shatter his turret nest and punish him while he rebuilds it","Use W-shield to tank the turret beams long enough to close range and proc the E armor-shred","R into him from off-screen angle so his CH-2 Grenade stun cannot interrupt your engage"],"foe":["Fight beside his turrets, where Hextech Affinity's move speed lets him kite you","Land CH-2 Grenade stun to reset the trade and buy time to rebuild his nest","Kite back on Hextech Micro-Rockets (1325 range) whenever your Q charge telegraphs itself"]},"early":"At level 1 Heimerdinger cannot place his full nest yet — one turret deals modest damage and your Q charge from the brush is a genuine threat he must respect. Walk up, bait him into placing a single turret, then slow-charge Q behind the wave so the slam hits him, not his machine. If you land the knockup, his nest resets and you win the first sixty seconds of the lane outright. Don't take the poke war from open ground — the moment you telegraph a Q charge he'll E-stun you out of it and punish through his turret.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are his window, not yours — three turrets fully online means any straight walk-up eats so many beams that your W-shield evaporates before you close range. Farm conservatively, hold your Q charge, and do not commit to a full all-in unless his E grenade is on cooldown. At level 6 everything changes: R from brush or river angle and the charge is literally unstoppable — his grenade cannot cancel it mid-flight. Hit him with the knock-up, pop W to tank his turret beams on entry, then E to shred his armor and brawl. Your zombie passive also makes diving his turrets much less risky — he has to respect that even a dead Sion fights back.","late":"Two items in, Heimerdinger's AP makes his turrets genuinely dangerous to tank and his R UPGRADE!! grenade can stunlock you long enough for the nest to delete your W-shield. Don't walk into a full three-turret spread — instead shove with R from distance to clear his setup, or splitpush where your HP passive and W-shield dominate the 1v1 and he is forced to match you without his nest already built. In teamfights your job is to engage with R, tank the beams on your massive HP pool, and let your team collapse — Heimerdinger's turrets need a stationary fight to shine and your unstoppable engage denies him that.","whys":["At level 1 he has one turret at most — a fully charged Q from brush hits before his nest is established and forces an early recall or burnt summoners.","His nest is half-built and you both have two to three abilities — neither side can force a decisive trade, so respect the turret poke and farm even.","His H-28 G turret nest covers the wave now, and CH-2 Electron Storm Grenade stuns on a flat 11s at every rank — charge your Q only inside that gap.","Three turrets plus W rocket poke from safety is his peak — you cannot charge Q through beam fire without your W-shield melting before you reach him.","Unstoppable Onslaught is literally unstoppable — his CH-2 Grenade cannot interrupt the charge, and you arrive already in melee range with E armor-shred ready.","His Luden's or Shadowflame spike means turrets now shred your W-shield in seconds — you need a perfect angle and grenade tracked before you can dive safely.","Both of you have full builds — your HP passive and W-shield are enormous but his turret nest plus UPGRADE!! grenade can lock you down; splitpush and angle your R rather than straight-walk his setup."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"illaoi","win":["Sion","Sion","Illaoi","Illaoi","Illaoi","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your W-shield absorbs a tentacle slam and you follow with a charged Q knockup — her E whiffs or you bait it and punish with E armor-shred into the brawl."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Her E rips your spirit — R out of the vessel range instantly, then re-engage on your terms when the tentacle field is thinner."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel HP compound makes your W-shield enormous and your zombie passive a genuine threat — sustained brawls near her tentacles become survivable."},{"when":"Late","text":"Illaoi's R summons a tentacle per nearby enemy champion — never fight her 2v1 or 3v1 inside her field or a single R wipes your team."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a full-charged Q knockup while she has no E target to rip your spirit","Buy Grievous Wounds — her tentacles heal her off every champion they hit, all lane long","Bait her E by standing near a tentacle then sidestepping — leave her with no vessel to punish"],"foe":["Rip your spirit with E near a cluster of tentacles so you fight your own vessel","Harsh Lesson (4s) leaps to you and makes every nearby tentacle swing at once","Stall until her R is up and fight you with summon-on-hit tentacles multiplied by your team presence"]},"early":"Short trades favor you at level 2 once your W-shield is online — absorb a tentacle auto-attack with the shield, then immediately charge Q toward her. Her E (Test of Spirit) is the only thing that turns the lane: if she lands it, walk your spirit away from every tentacle until the vessel expires rather than fighting through it. At level 3 with E armor-shred in hand, you can full-combo her after a blocked tentacle slam — shred her armor, land the charged-Q knockup, and brawl. The window before she lands E is genuinely yours; the moment she hits it near two or more tentacles, walk away cleanly.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are the flattest stretch — her tentacles accumulate and her passive sustain keeps her healthy, while your charged Q is readable enough that she can pre-position away from choke points. Avoid charging Q in the middle of a tentacle cluster or you eat two slams during the channel. At level 6 you gain Unstoppable Onslaught as an escape valve: if she lands E and calls tentacles on your vessel, R away toward your tower, wait for the vessel to despawn, then R back in. Never sit inside her vessel zone trading — you are handing her the Prophet of an Elder God healing she needs to grind you down.","late":"Late game Sion is a genuine frontline threat in this matchup but Illaoi's R is the equalizer in any multi-person fight — each nearby ally she has adds a summoned tentacle on every hit, turning a 3v3 fight in the side lane into a tentacle apocalypse. Split-push with R as transit and force her to answer you alone; her immobility means she cannot simply follow you across the map. In a side-lane 1v1, your zombie passive (Glory in Death) after dying still deals damage and denies her an easy reset, which genuinely matters in close fights. Force the solo lane and make her play your game, not a teamfight inside her field.","whys":["Her tentacles need time to place — level 1 she only has a passive slam if one is nearby. Farm safely and don't stand adjacent to a tentacle.","W-shield absorbs a tentacle slam at level 2, letting you charge Q without eating full damage — her E is not yet reliably positioned to punish.","With E and W both up, you E-shred her armor, W-absorb a slam, and land charged Q knockup before she can reposition — the full combo wins the extended trade.","Tentacle density builds and Prophet of an Elder God heals her off every slam — brawl only in the 16s after Test of Spirit misses, and away from the field.","Test of Spirit at 6 forces you to run: R out of vessel range the moment it lands, and come back only once the tentacle field has thinned.","Heartsteel compounds your HP passive from kills and stacks — your W-shield value and zombie durability both scale here, making sustained brawls near her field winnable.","A full tentacle field plus her R makes late teamfights inside her zone catastrophic — split and force the 1v1 where your HP bulk and zombie passive deny her clean resets."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"irelia","win":["Sion","Skill","Irelia","Irelia","Skill","Irelia","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"A pre-charged Q at level 1 threatens a knockup before Irelia has her E stun — the only stage where you can punish her for stepping up."},{"when":"Lvl 4-6","text":"Irelia's Q resets off minions let her close the gap and proc her E stun freely — she chains dashes through a large wave to pin you before your charged Q lands."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Trinity Force Irelia spikes here, and Vanguard's Edge (125s) walls off your retreat — hold Flash for the wall, not for the damage."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Heartsteel Sion peaks: your HP total makes W-shield absorb enormous amounts, and a full-charged Q stun into E armor-shred one-shots her burst windows."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup when Irelia commits her E dash, interrupting her reset chain","Detonate Soul Furnace through Defiant Dance — its damage is magic, and her W only cuts physical","Build Heartsteel and force sustained brawls where your HP pool and Passive zombie threat outlast her combo cooldowns"],"foe":["Stack her passive (Ionian Fervor) to 5 by dashing through minions before engaging you, shredding your armor and attack speed","E-stun you at an angle or from behind so your charged Q telegraph is already committed and whiffs","Force fights on large minion waves where her Q resets let her kite inside your charge-up time"]},"early":"At level 1, a pre-charged Q is your only legitimate threat — hold it at the brush edge and bait her forward before she hits level 2. The moment she hits level 2 and gets her W (Defiant Dance), she can block the burst of your full-charged slam, removing your best trading tool entirely. Keep the wave even or slightly pushed so the minion count is never large enough to fuel her Q resets into you; deny her the lane geometry she needs to stack Ionian Fervor safely. If she tries to step up to auto you, fire the charged Q into her approach — the knockup duration is long enough that you can follow with E armor-shred before she lands.","mid":"Levels 4 through 5 are her strongest window: with Q resets, her passive at max stacks adds bonus on-hit damage, and her E sets a guaranteed stun that burns your W-shield on HER timing, not yours. Respect large minion waves entirely — do not try to fight on a 7-plus minion wave because she chains dashes and you can never complete a charge. At level 6 the lane rebalances slightly: your R can catch her mid-dash sequence and the sudden all-in threat forces her to play around your cooldowns. Hold your charged Q for the moment right after she uses E — Flawless Duet has a 16-second cooldown, and the gap between her E and its return is your only clean punish window.","late":"Once Heartsteel starts compounding your HP, your W-shield absorbs a meaningful portion of her Trinity Force burst, and your E armor-shred on a stunned Irelia hurts her back disproportionately given her typically lighter build. The zombie Passive is a legitimate deterrent in extended sieges — even a dying Sion under a tower forces her to back off her reset chain. Play the 2-plus items stage as a slow-push splitpush threat: R into her flank, Q-stun the engage, E-shred, and brawl; if she tries to Defiant Dance through your burst, the shield only blocks one exchange and your HP total wins the war of attrition. Never die to her at low HP with minions around — her Q resets off your Passive form still give her free gold.","whys":["A pre-charged Q threatens a knockup before she has her E stun — she has no reliable in-trade reset at level 1 and must respect the ground-slam telegraph.","Both kits come online simultaneously: your W-shield can block her E stun's burst, and her Defiant Dance can block yours — genuine parity in short trades.","With all three basic abilities she chains Q resets through a minion wave to stack Ionian Fervor before her E stun, winning sustained trades on her terms.","Ionian Fervor at max stacks is attack speed and bonus on-hit, not armour shred, and Bladesurge resets keep her on you through the wind-up — the big wave is hers.","Your R gives you all-in threat she must respect, but her E is back up and her Trinity Force timing approaches — neither side can force a clean kill.","Trinity Force Irelia's burst per reset cycle matches your W-shield cooldown; every E stun she lands goes unanswered while your Q is charging.","Heartsteel Sion's HP makes your W-shield absorb her burst, your E shreds her lightly-armored frame, and your Passive zombie forces her off every extended trade."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"jax","win":["Sion","Skill","Jax","Jax","Skill","Jax","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"A fully charged Q knockup before Jax can activate Counter Strike ends the trade before his stun fires — but he can simply dodge the telegraph, so it is a real spike only if he misreads the setup."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught closes the gap that Counter Strike buys Jax and opens a genuine all-in, but his R armor/MR aura still makes this a skill fight rather than a free kill."},{"when":"1st Item","text":"Trinity Force Jax is your hardest window — the Empower wind-up (3-7s cooldown) is the tell his burst is coming, and the stun locks you out before the shield detonates."},{"when":"2+ Items","text":"Heartsteel + second tank item means your W-shield absorbs the Sheen proc burst and your max-HP pool out-stats Jax's on-hit damage — you win the sustained brawl outright."}],"wants":{"you":["Keep landing Q and E while Counter Strike is active — it blanks basic attacks, not abilities, so only your autos are wasted","Stack HP passively with Heartsteel and let your W-shield value compound against his on-hit burst at 2 items","Use E armor-shred to drain his physical-damage output and win the late-game sustained brawl where your zombie passive threatens even on death"],"foe":["Bait your autos into Counter Strike (17s at rank 1) — it dodges basic attacks, so your basics do nothing while it is up","Spike with Trinity Force at first item and all-in before your HP passive compounds enough to absorb his Sheen procs","Sustain the lane to item power and outbrawl you through R armor aura, denying your HP advantage with consistent on-hit damage"]},"early":"At level 1 you have a genuine threat — a full-charge Q knockup deletes his early window before Counter Strike is even active — but Jax simply sidesteps the slow charge, so do not stand still telegraphing it. Farm with Q tip at long range and watch for him to step up without his E ready; that is the one moment to hard-commit. Your W-shield absorbs his first trade if he walks onto you at level 2, but do not detonate it into his active Counter Strike or the shield expires for zero damage while he stuns you out of the follow-up. At level 3 his full kit is online and he wins most coin-flip trades: E dodges your Q auto follow-up, then stuns you into a Q-W burst sequence that your shield cannot fully block. Respect the level-3 all-in and let the wave come to you.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are Jax's lane. He can activate Counter Strike pre-emptively when he sees your Q charge animation, though it dodges attacks rather than abilities. Farm safely, keep your W-shield for reactive use when he dashes in, and shove him under tower if the wave permits — his Counter Strike does not stop a tower shot. At level 6 the matchup opens up: Unstoppable Onslaught covers too much distance for Counter Strike to buy him a clean escape, and landing the follow-up E armor-shred then W-shield into a charged Q is a real kill threat. However his R grants armor and MR on activation, so wait for it to drop before committing the all-in if he is already low — fighting into active Grandmaster-at-Arms halves the damage your combo does.","late":"At two items the lane flips decisively. Your Heartsteel-stacked HP total means your W-shield absorbs his Sheen proc plus one full Counter Strike stun cycle before expiring, and your zombie passive forces him to either finish the kill or take free damage — he cannot just back off. Your E armor-shred punishes a stat-stick fighter harder than an AP tank because his entire late kit is on-hit physical; shredded armor turns your brawl into a stat rout. In true late game he has no percent-HP tool to punish your bulk, unlike Vayne or Gwen, so the HP snowball you built all lane is pure advantage. Force the 1v1 at tower or in splitpush: R in, E-shred, W-shield, charged-Q — the zombie followup means even a close fight costs him the wave.","whys":["Your charged Q knockup is a genuine level-1 kill threat before Counter Strike is online, but Jax can sidestep the charge animation — the window is real only if he steps into range carelessly.","Your W-shield soaks his first two-ability trade at level 2, but you cannot reliably convert without your E; Jax can simply back off and reset, keeping the lane coin-flip.","His full kit at level 3 — Counter Strike dodges your Q auto, stuns you, and his Q-W burst fires before your shield detonates — tips the trade firmly in his favor.","Counter Strike is 17s at rank 1 and dodges attacks, not abilities — your Q and E still land through it, but the stun eats your auto follow-up and your shield times out.","Unstoppable Onslaught closes the gap Counter Strike creates and opens a kill threat, but his R armor-and-MR aura means the all-in is still a skill read, not a free win.","Trinity Force Sheen procs spike sharply enough to burst through your W-shield before you detonate it and stun you before the follow-up Q, making first item his hardest power window.","Two tank items and a Heartsteel stack means your W-shield absorbs his full on-hit burst cycle, E armor-shred dismantles his physical damage, and your zombie passive makes even a close duel costly for him."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"jayce","win":["Jayce","Jayce","Jayce","Sion","Sion","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Shock Blast (16s at rank 1) fired through the gate chunks you before you can charge a Q — step up only inside that window, and farm under tower until it returns."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Your W-shield now absorbs a full Shock Blast gate combo; walk up behind shield and force him to switch to hammer stance or eat a charged Q stun."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel or Sunfire spike: your HP total balloons and your W-shield value skyrockets, making every charged-Q close-range brawl decisively Sion-favoured."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"With two tank items stacked your zombie passive alone zones Jayce off objectives — he cannot poke a Sion corpse to death before it deals meaningful damage."}],"wants":{"you":["Charge Q the moment he lays the gate — that slot is 20s and he cannot re-lay it","Use W-shield to absorb his Acceleration Gate poke and then commit to a short trade","Out-scale his early poke lead with Heartsteel HP stacks and brawl him at first item"],"foe":["Throw Shock Blast through Acceleration Gate from max range to whittle your HP before any trade","Use E knockback to interrupt your charged Q charge or shove you off a CS","Swap stance (6s) — his first hit after it shreds your armour and MR, and it is an auto reset"]},"early":"Levels 1 through 3 are purely Jayce's game and you have to accept that. His Shock Blast through an Acceleration Gate hits harder than your W-shield can absorb at this stage, and his E knockback shoves you away the moment you try to walk up and charge a Q. Do not attempt to charge Q into a Jayce who is standing at max gate range — he will knock you back mid-charge and you lose the trade. Farm with auto-attacks and short W-shield tank trades only; your goal is to hit level 4 with decent HP and not give him a kill that fuels his early lead.","mid":"At levels 4 and 5 the tide begins to turn because your W-shield now soaks a meaningful portion of his gate combo, and your charged Q range is long enough to threaten him if he stands still to aim. Walk up with W-shield already active — he has to choose between retreating out of gate range or eating your stun; either way you win the positioning war. At level 6 your Unstoppable Onslaught makes every bush and river gap irrelevant: if he over-extends even slightly, your R closes the distance instantly and his E knockback cannot interrupt the charge once it is active.","late":"Past first item you are the clear favourite in every straight exchange. Heartsteel or a tank mythic puts your HP total so far above his burst threshold that even a perfect Shock Blast gate plus hammer combo cannot drop you far enough to matter before your zombie passive threatens him back. Force short brawls at melee range where his ranged poke loses all value and your E armor-shred plus charged Q do enormous damage to his light bruiser frame. In teamfights your R engage targets him specifically — a Jayce who is running from Unstoppable Onslaught cannot Acceleration Gate from behind a wall and poke safely, which is his entire late-game plan.","whys":["Jayce's Shock Blast through an Acceleration Gate chunks you hard at level 1 before your W-shield has any real value — farm at range and do not charge Q into him.","He still out-ranges you at 2, and his E slot is 20s at rank 1 — Thundering Blow and Acceleration Gate share it, so walking up costs you either way.","Shock Blast is 16s at rank 1 and the gate doubles its reach — you cannot close on a Jayce who keeps his range, so accept the deficit and farm it out.","Your W-shield now absorbs enough of a gate combo to justify walking up, and your charged Q threatens his E knockback timing — Jayce must back off or take a stun.","Unstoppable Onslaught cannot be interrupted by his E knockback; you can R through his gate line and close the gap for a full charged Q brawl on demand.","Heartsteel's HP stack makes your W-shield enormous and his burst combo trivially absorbed; you out-trade every melee exchange and his poke no longer threatens a kill.","Two tank items plus your zombie passive mean Jayce cannot safely poke in lane, push objectives, or duel you — he is permanently on the back foot and you dictate every engagement."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"karma","win":["Karma","Skill","Sion","Sion","Karma","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Karma's Q poke hits freely before your W-shield comes online — she dictates the opening trade with zero cooldown pressure on your part."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Mantra ranks up here: RW extends her Focused Resolve root and heals her through your chip — her real spike, so dodge the tether, not the Q."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel or Sunfire means your HP snowball begins compounding; Karma literally cannot kill you in a trade and your charged Q threat forces her back."},{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your W-shield comes online and you can start angling charged Q from brush — the telegraph risk drops and Karma has no dash to escape a landed stun."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a charged Q knockup after baiting her Focused Resolve tether off cooldown","W-shield her Q poke burst so she bleeds Mantra charges for nothing","Force her to respect the charge-Q angle so she falls off the wave and loses CS"],"foe":["Root you with Mantra-W and chain Q poke while you stand still","Keep her E shield and move speed ready so she can step back out of Q charge range","Deny you CS with repeated Q poke so you fall behind on Heartsteel stacks"]},"early":"At level 1 you have no W-shield and Karma's Inner Flame reaches 950 units — she will walk up, Q-poke you, and step back behind the wave before you can begin charging. Accept it. Last-hit at Q range and do not start charging your Q in the open where she simply walks back. The moment you hit level 2 your W-shield changes the equation: pop it preemptively before she steps up to Q, soak the burst for free, and look for a charged Q angle through a minion gap or from brush. Her Focused Resolve tether is her only lockdown — sidestep it, because Mantra needs no skill point and is live from level 1, so a completed root buys her a free empowered follow-up.","mid":"Once you have your full basic kit, your charged Q threat is genuine: Karma has no dash, so a landed stun at levels 3-5 means a free E armor-shred follow-up and real damage. Bait her Focused Resolve tether first — it is a flat 12s cooldown, so the moment it is down you have a free charge window; dodge it by moving perpendicular, then charge. Mantra is live from level 1 and ranks up at 6: RW extends the root and heals her, RQ drops a delayed cataclysm, so early Grievous Wounds blunts her sustain. Hold W for whichever she commits and stay out of tether range while Mantra is up. Ping your jungler if Karma pushes — Inspire gives a speed burst that makes her hard to gank without setup.","late":"Past first item Karma simply cannot kill you — your HP pool and W-shield absorb her burst, and your zombie passive means she cannot rotate away from a deep push without losing the tower. Force long side-lane shoves: Karma's Q poke is powerful but her kill threat on a full Sion is basically zero, so she is stuck poking from range while you teleport a chunk of the map. In teamfights Mantra-E spreads a shield and move speed to her team, but your R travels 7500 units and she has no way to peel you off a back-line target once you connect. Mantra is a 40s cooldown she shortens with every damaging spell — if she spends it on Inspire or the healing tether, walk onto her with a charged Q.","whys":["Karma's Q poke is free and safe at level 1 — your W-shield isn't up yet and charging Q in the open just lets her walk back, so hold your ground and last-hit.","Both have their key tools: your W-shield soaks her Q but her Focused Resolve tether can break your charge — dodge the tether before committing.","Your full kit is online and Karma has no dash; a charged Q from brush or a minion angle stuns her in place for a free E armor-shred combo she cannot escape.","Your HP starts to compound and your W-shield makes her Q burst feel like nothing — pressure the wave and force her to spend Mantra reactively rather than aggressively.","Mantra ranks up here: RW extends the Focused Resolve root and heals her, RQ is a delayed cataclysm — dodge the tether, hold W for the blast, and buy Grievous Wounds.","Heartsteel begins stacking hard and Karma literally lacks the burst damage to threaten your HP pool; charge Q confidently and punish her every time she steps up to poke.","An 8,000-HP Sion with a zombie passive is a nightmare for a poke enchanter — keep shoving and force her to answer your R engages with utility alone."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"kassadin","win":["Sion","Skill","Kassadin","Kassadin","Kassadin","Kassadin","Kassadin"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Your charged Q is the only threat here — Void Stone reduces magic damage, so W is blunted but your physical Q and Roar of the Slayer armour shred land in full. Take the knockup early."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Kassadin's R (Riftwalk) comes online and he can blink away from every charged Q you attempt — your threat window closes sharply here."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Kassadin's Luden's or Shadowflame spike makes his Q silence + trade pattern sustainable; your W-shield starts losing the damage-absorption race against his AP scaling."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Full AP Kassadin blinks through your charged Q telegraphs freely and one-shots your zombie passive — you cannot pin him and his R cooldown is too short to punish."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup before Null Sphere lands and breaks the charge","Keep Kassadin in a long corridor so his blink distance is constrained","Use R (Unstoppable Onslaught) to close distance when his Riftwalk stack count is high and he is committed"],"foe":["Break your charge with Null Sphere, then trade with Nether Blade","Stack Riftwalk and blink away from every all-in attempt once he hits level 6","Reach two items and delete you from range before your zombie passive can threaten back"]},"early":"You are a genuine threat at level 1 and level 2: Void Stone only reduces magic damage, so your charged Q knockup and Roar of the Slayer armour shred land at full value. Walk him toward a wall or tight chokepoint, let him push and farm, then start charging Q when he steps up to last-hit. Bait Null Sphere by walking forward without charging, then charge once it is down — it is 9s at rank 1. Watch his auto wind-up rather than his casts: Nether Blade is a 7s empowered attack and that animation is the real tell. Void Stone also means he ignores unit collision, so the wave will not body-block him. Get a knockup before level 3; it sets the HP trade in your favour before the matchup inverts.","mid":"At level 3 Kassadin has his full base kit and the lane begins slipping away. Force Pulse is 21s at rank 1 and only fires once he has charged it off nearby spellcasts, so a whiffed cone is a long free window for you. Level 6 is the hard line — the moment Riftwalk is available he can blink out of any charged Q you attempt and your R (Unstoppable Onslaught) becomes his best friend: he blinks sideways while you travel in a straight line and you overshoot. The blink is only 500 units and each repeat costs extra mana, so press him toward a wall rather than in open ground. Otherwise hold farm and track his roams with pings — his level 6 trip down mid is a genuine threat and you have no sprint to match it.","late":"You are a nearly unkillable frontline with a menacing zombie passive, but none of that matters if you cannot touch Kassadin. He blinks through every charged Q, his AP burst burns through your W-shield, and your zombie passive (Glory in Death) is the one thing that keeps the lane respectable — he has to respect finishing you twice. Accept the lane loss and play for team utility: your R engage into his backline, your W-shield soaking AoE, and your Q knockup on grouped enemies are all still meaningful. Kassadin has to walk into a chokepoint with his team for you to contribute — ward river chokes and coordinate your R with your team rather than trying to 1v1 him again.","whys":["Your charged Q knockup is physical and unblockable by his passive magic shield — it is your only level-1 threat before he has Q to silence you.","He has Q silence but no mobility yet; a tight lane and patient charge timing can bait the silence cooldown and land the knockup.","Null Sphere is a 650-range orb on 8s that interrupts and shields him from magic — he does not need to fight you, just break your rhythm and walk away.","With Riftwalk online he blinks 500 units out when you start the charge, but at rank 1 it is a 5s cooldown — engage in the window right after he burns it.","His Riftwalk stacks and roam range compound every minute — he wins neutral farm and roams to impact mid while you are stuck splitpushing with no mobility to match.","His first AP item pushes Null Sphere's trade damage above what your W-shield can absorb, and rank-2 Riftwalk on 3.5s dodges nearly every engage attempt you make.","Full AP Kassadin deletes you before your zombie passive activates and blinks away from the retaliation — your HP stacking is irrelevant against his burst damage."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"kayle","win":["Sion","Sion","Skill","Skill","Kayle","Kayle","Kayle"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Your fully charged Q knockup is her worst nightmare early — she has no dash or invulnerability until level 6 and cannot dodge it without burning flash."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Divine Judgment arrives at 6 on a 160s cooldown — bait it with a W-pop, then commit the charged Q; if she spends it, the lane is yours again."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Kayle's Nashor's Tooth or Riftmaker spike hits around the same time as your Heartsteel — she starts dealing percent-max-HP damage that punishes the very HP stack you need."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Full-AP Kayle with Nashor's + second item shreds your HP bar from range while her R makes every kill attempt a coin flip — respect her and split or peel."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q stun before level 6 to punish her zero-mobility weak laning phase","Pop your W-shield to eat her auto-poke, then immediately counter-trade with E armor-shred before her next auto","Deny her CS and XP early so her item spikes arrive late, keeping your HP advantage relevant longer"],"foe":["Free-farm and dodge your charged Q with footwork, kiting back after every poke without over-committing","Reach level 6 and bank her Divine Judgment invulnerability so your charged Q all-in wastes itself on her R","Scale into Nashor's Tooth and a second AP item where her ranged percent-max-HP damage outpaces your HP stacking"]},"early":"Kayle is one of the weakest level-1s in the game and she has no dash, no escape, and no invulnerability yet — this is your best window. Step toward her to threaten a full charge, and the moment she backs up to dodge, you secure free CS with the outer tip. If she missteps or over-extends even slightly, a fully charged Q knockup into E armor-shred is a near-guaranteed ignite kill at level 2. Use your W-shield to absorb her auto poke and trade back immediately; she cannot sustain your HP pool damage in a short trade when you shred her armor with E. Every kill or CS denial here delays her item spikes by a full minute or more — that time is your lane.","mid":"At level 6 the dynamic shifts: Kayle now has Divine Judgment, and she will hold it as a get-out the instant you open a charged Q animation. It is 160s at rank 1 — bait it, then run Q-E while it is gone. Do not dump your all-in while her R is up or you hand her a free lane reset. Celestial Blessing heals her every 15s flat, so buy Grievous Wounds before your second item or your chip damage is refunded. Watch her attack wind-up, not her casts: Starfire Spellblade is a 6-8s auto reset that scales with your missing health, so it hits hardest exactly when you are low.","late":"Once Kayle completes Nashor's Tooth alongside a second AP item, her ranged on-hit percent-max-HP damage turns your Heartsteel stack against you — the bigger you are, the harder she melts you. Her Divine Judgment makes every clean kill attempt risky, and she can freely kite your R charge if she sees it coming. Play for split-pressure with your R-transit speed, zone objectives with your zombie passive threat, and understand that the 1v1 you won early is gone. Your team can still win through your frontline bulk, but you do not kill a well-itemized Kayle 1v1 unless she burns her R on something else first.","whys":["Kayle has no mobility and no R at level 1 — a fully charged Q stun here costs her a kill or burns her flash with zero counterplay available.","W-shield absorbs her auto poke, and your E armor-shred plus passive zombie threat tips the short level-2 trade decisively in your favor.","Her kiting footwork and your slow charge animation reach a stand-off — she can avoid your Q if she respects it, but you can zone her off CS freely.","Both kits are mostly online but neither has an item spike yet — she kites your charge at range while you zone her off farm, a genuine skill-dependent grind.","Divine Judgment answers your charged Q all-in outright — but it is 160s at rank 1, so a baited R hands you the whole lane until it comes back.","Her Heartsteel-era spike arrives alongside yours, but Nashor's Tooth on-hit percent-max-HP damage begins punishing your HP stack — she wins this trade.","Full-AP Kayle shreds your massive HP bar from outside your Q range, her R absorbs any all-in, and she scales harder than any HP juggernaut can match."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"kennen","win":["Skill","Kennen","Kennen","Sion","Kennen","Kennen","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Your full basic kit is online but Kennen has no meaningful power spike yet — W-shield his Q poke and charged Q the moment Lightning Rush is down to claim the wave-control window."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Heartsteel stacking plus a second tank item (Warmog's or Iceborn Gauntlet) makes you effectively unkillable in side-lane brawls; Kennen's Q poke and Lightning Rush burst can no longer chip you to a killable threshold."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Slicing Maelstrom is live on a flat 120s cooldown — it re-applies Mark of the Storm again and again, so never start a charge inside its 550 radius."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Kennen's Rocketbelt or Zhonya's spike lets him all-in then Lightning Rush out at 170 range — sit on the wave and do not start a charge he can simply walk out of."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a charged Q knockup on the Lightning Rush cooldown — E-shred his armor and brawl him when he can't escape","W-shield his Q and auto poke to deny his harass pattern and hold a HP lead into mid-game","Force side-lane 1v1s at 2+ items where your zombie passive and HP pool make every fight cost him more than it costs you"],"foe":["Stack three marks with Q auto and W to set up a Lightning Rush stun without ever entering your charged-Q range","Kite you around the wave with move speed from Lightning Rush so your charge telegraph is always visible before you release","Ult into a pre-stacked team fight where his AOE stun chains five marks and your HP pool works against you"]},"early":"Kennen controls levels 1 through 3 with his ranged Q and passive mark stacking — he can proc a Lightning Rush stun on you without ever stepping into your charged-Q range, so charging your Q in the open is a free mark for him. Take MR shard and Doran's Shield, stand close enough to CS behind minions, and use W-shield to eat any Q he lands. Don't chase his poke with an E-slow; save E for when you've landed the knockup. Your only real threat window early is an angle charge from river or bush — if he doesn't see the telegraph, a full-charge Q knockup into E-shred trades more than he bargained for, but it requires him to over-step.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are your first real window: Kennen has no spike here and Lightning Rush has a 10-second cooldown you can track. Once it's burned on a kite attempt, step forward, shield up with W, and charge Q — he can't escape and can't Mark-proc you without the Rush. At level 6 you gain Unstoppable Onslaught for engage and exit threat, but Kennen's R changes the math on every all-in: three marks already on you from the lane means his R can full-stun you mid-combo and end the fight instantly. Don't commit the knockup into a pre-marked setup — bait the R first, then run the full pattern on cooldown.","late":"At two-plus items your HP stacking makes Kennen's burst pattern irrelevant in a straight side-lane brawl — his Thundering Shuriken chips a fraction of your pool, and your zombie passive means killing you still costs him his own life if he miscalculates. Force repeated 1v1 splits and let your team react while he's stuck in side. In team fights respect Slicing Maelstrom: it is a 550-radius storm that keeps re-applying Mark of the Storm, so a group of five all get stunned — peel back, let your team follow the engage, and use Unstoppable Onslaught to arrive from outside that radius.","whys":["Neither champion has a real answer at level 1 — Kennen's Q poke sets marks but can't all-in, and your charged Q is too slow to land on a mobile ranged champion who hasn't over-committed yet.","Kennen's Q and autos stack Mark of the Storm freely while your charge is visible at range — the third mark stuns you before you release, making levels 2-3 his window.","Full kit online but his mark pattern is consistent now: Thundering Shuriken twice, then Lightning Rush through you for the third Mark of the Storm — the stun beats your charge.","Lightning Rush has a 10-second cooldown and no spike lands for Kennen here — W-shield his Q poke, then charged Q the moment Rush is burned and you own the wave-control window.","Kennen's R AOE stun comes online and his marks are already on you from lane — any all-in you force into a pre-stacked setup lets him chain-stun you in place and burst you with his team.","Kennen's first item (Rocketbelt or Zhonya's) lets him all-in and Lightning-Rush out cleanly, or bait your commit into a Zhonya's invulnerability — his power peak, your most dangerous split to force.","Heartsteel and a second tank item make your HP pool larger than Kennen's entire damage budget — his Q poke and Lightning Rush burst can't chip you to a killable threshold in a side-lane brawl."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"kled","win":["Skill","Kled","Kled","Kled","Kled","Kled","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Kled's Bear Trap on a Rope (Q grab) comes fully online — one successful hook before your W-shield is up and he can dismount-chain you into a losing extended brawl."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Kled's Chaaaaarge!!! lets him dictate every all-in angle; your W-shield absorbs one burst rotation but he can reset with remount and come back for a second fight."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Your Heartsteel spike is still outpaced by Kled's early item power — the HP you have accumulated is not enough to withstand his mounted-plus-dismounted combo burst yet."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"At two items your Heartsteel HP stack finally outweighs his burst ceiling — W-shield his Q grab, brawl through dismount, and your zombie passive punishes any overcommit."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Bear Trap on a Rope (Q grab) on your W-shield, then charge full Q into the knockup while he has no hook to escape","Hold the wave near your tower so Kled must walk into your charged-Q range and burn his Q to even approach","Stack Heartsteel through the rough early levels and reach 2+ items where your HP pool and zombie passive override his burst"],"foe":["Land Bear Trap on a Rope before your W-shield is deployed to lock you in place and run the mounted-plus-dismounted combo","Abuse the Level 2-5 window where his skirmish burst outpaces your HP scaling — force early all-ins while you are still fragile","Use Chaaaaarge!!! to dictate every fight angle and remount into a second brawl if his first rotation fails to kill"]},"early":"Kled comes off the Level 1 start even — but at Level 2 his Bear Trap on a Rope (Q) turns every step-up into a hook-to-death, and it is only 11s at rank 1. Hold your W-shield in reserve; do not blow it on harass before he throws the grab. Charge Q from behind your own minions so the telegraph is obscured — hooked mid-charge you eat the full mounted combo, lose Skaarl, and take the dismounted follow-up with nothing left. His commit tell is the attack-speed jump from Violent Tendencies, not a cast, so read his autos. Farm under tower until you have his Q timed.","mid":"Kled keeps the edge through Levels 4-6 because his skirmish burst still outpaces your HP accumulation and his Chaaaaarge!!! means he chooses when every fight starts. At Level 6 do not try to match his R with yours blindly — Unstoppable Onslaught travels far and straight, Kled can sidestep it, and a whiffed R into his full remount combo is a death. Instead use R as an escape or gank assist; let your tower and wave do the punishing if he dives recklessly. Look for the one window where his Q grab is on cooldown and his Skaarl bar is low — a landed charged-Q knockup into E armor-shred there can strip his mount and reset the tempo.","late":"Two items changes everything: your Heartsteel stack reaches a HP total where his full mounted-plus-dismounted combo cannot one-shot you through a W-shield, and your zombie passive means he must burn a second rotation to confirm the kill — which he usually cannot afford. Now you take the trades. Pre-deploy W before he lands Bear Trap on a Rope so the shield eats the hook burst, absorb the dismount chain, and charge Q the moment he is re-engaging on foot. Your E armor-shred compounds on his lower base armor in dismounted form. Splitpush with R transit, force him to match alone, and your late-game HP wall becomes a genuine 1v1 answer that his early window can no longer paper over.","whys":["Level 1 Kled has his bear-trap grab but so does every melee bully — both kits are incomplete and the first wave is genuinely contested; farm safely and read his aggression pattern before committing your W.","Bear Trap on a Rope comes online — an 800-range hook on 11s that yanks you in if the shackle holds, setting up a full mounted combo before your W-shield deploys.","Violent Tendencies gives four rapid attacks with a heavier fourth, and his Q grab is up — he out-trades you in any short exchange and W-shield alone will not swing it back.","His skirmish burst through Levels 4-5 still outpaces your HP curve, and there is no clean window to land a charged Q without him either hooking you first or simply walking out of the slow charge arc.","Chaaaaarge!!! lets Kled dictate the entire all-in angle; your Unstoppable Onslaught is a straight line he can sidestep, and a whiffed R into his remount-second-brawl cycle is a guaranteed death.","His first-item power spike outpaces Heartsteel's early HP returns — the raw stats he gains from core items mean his burst ceiling still clears your sustain before the HP passive compounds meaningfully.","At two-plus items your Heartsteel stack exceeds his burst ceiling through a W-shield; zombie passive forces a second rotation he cannot complete, and E armor-shred in his dismounted form flips the 1v1 decisively in your favour."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"ksante","win":["Sion","Sion","Skill","Skill","K'Sante","Skill","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"W-shield absorbs K'Sante's Q-knockup poke and lets you full-charge Q into his slow, resisty body for a guaranteed stun."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"K'Sante's All Out drops his own resists and isolates you — avoid all-ins at 6 unless you are substantially ahead and his R is down."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel completed: your HP passive starts snowballing and you win every sustained trade where K'Sante cannot proc his W-block dash efficiently."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Path Maker carries true damage, so armour buys you nothing against it — at two items he charges it up, takes reduced damage through your burst, and stuns you out of the fight."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a full-charge Q knockup into E armor-shred while K'Sante's W dash is on cooldown","Keep trades short with W-shield burst damage so he cannot proc his percent-HP passive repeatedly","Reach Heartsteel spike and freeze the wave to compound your HP passive safely"],"foe":["Land Ntofo Strikes twice to pull you in, then cash the marked auto for its empowered damage","Use Path Maker's charge-up to soak your full-charge Q, then dash out of the combo that kills him","Pop All Out on a favourable flanks to strip his own resists and isolate you for a prolonged duel"]},"early":"Level 1 and 2, K'Sante is deceptively weak — Ntofo Strikes is a 450-range line and his passive only pays off on the next attack after an ability, so step out of that follow-up and he gets nothing. Pop your W-shield the moment he steps in for an auto-trade and let the detonation chunk him. Level 3, his kit is online but your charged Q off a bush angle or post-E-shove hits him too slowly to dodge — land it and the stun pays for the whole sequence. Path Maker is 14s at rank 1, so every time he spends the dash you get a long clean window to charge.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are genuine skill-checks: K'Sante can Path Maker through your charged Q if he reads the wind-up, and you have no meaningful spike yet to force a decisive trade. Farm safely, watch for him shoving to roam, and do not waste your E armor-shred while his W is up — he dashes out and you lose the tempo. At Level 6 All Out becomes the dominant threat: it strips his resistances, gives him healing, and it opens by launching you back through any wall in the way — hold Flash for that wall, not for the damage. Buy Grievous Wounds before you contest the form, and respect it unless you are meaningfully ahead.","late":"Once Heartsteel is complete and your HP passive is compounding, sustained brawls skew your way against a K'Sante who has to land his marked follow-up attack through your W-shield cycles. Force fights while Path Maker is on cooldown — that takes away his Q-dodge and his stun at once. Watch the true damage on that same ability, though: armour buys you nothing against it, so keep stacking health and add Grievous Wounds rather than piling on resistances. Trade briefly, reset with zombie pressure if you die, and lean on Unstoppable Onslaught to re-engage on your terms.","whys":["K'Sante's passive marks you with an ability and cashes in on his NEXT attack — pop W-shield when Ntofo Strikes lands and step back so the follow-up hits a shield.","Your W-shield absorbs his Q-poke damage and his passive proc, then detonates magic damage he has no easy resist for at this rank — you win the short trade.","Both kits are fully online but neither has a decisive spike: he can W-dash your charged Q and you can W-shield his poke, making the exchange genuinely even.","Path Maker is 14s at rank 1 and 10s at rank 5, and he can now reliably read a telegraphed charge — do not overcommit a full-charge Q while that dash is available.","All Out strips his own resistances and turns him into a duelist that heals — buy Grievous Wounds before you contest it, because Path Maker stops stunning but hits far harder.","Heartsteel gives you enough HP that All Out's 120s cooldown at rank 1 leaves long windows where he is only a tank again — trade hard in those gaps.","Your compounded HP pool and zombie passive make late teamfights Sion-favoured, but trade briefly and reset: Path Maker's true damage ignores every point of armour you bought and punishes long brawls."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"lillia","win":["Sion","Lillia","Lillia","Lillia","Lillia","Skill","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Before Lillia builds Blooming Blows stacks you have a brief window — a fully charged Q knockup here is a genuine threat she cannot dodge with move-speed she does not yet have."},{"when":"Lvl 2-6","text":"Once Lillia's Blooming Blows (Q) gives her stacking move speed, she can orbit your charged-Q telegraph and kite your E slow — her passive DoT bleeds you without ever standing still."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel vs Lillia's first AP item is a coin-flip spike race — both of you gain scaling power, so whichever side got a lead from the kiting phase carries the item window."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Her passive burns max health and the edge of Blooming Blows is true damage, so armour and MR do nothing — win instead with Heartsteel bulk, W-shield cycles and the zombie passive in brawls she cannot kite out of."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup before she builds Blooming Blows move-speed stacks","W-shield her DoT and trade short when she over-commits inside your melee range","Force a sustained brawl at 2+ items where your max-HP pool and zombie passive negate her kite"],"foe":["Stack Blooming Blows move-speed to orbit your Q telegraph and dodge the knockup","Use her W (Watch Out! Eep!) AoE to poke from range and bleed you down without engaging","Land her R (Lilting Lullaby) sleep on you at level 6 and disengage before your zombie passive threatens her"]},"early":"At level 1 Lillia has not yet stacked Blooming Blows passive move speed, so a fully charged Q is a real threat — use brush angles to hide the channel and catch her before the first wave clears. The moment she hits level 2 and starts cycling Blooming Blows her orbit widens with every stack, making your Q telegraph progressively easier to sidestep. Accept that levels 2 and 3 belong to her poke: your W-shield eats her W (Watch Out! Eep!) AoE damage, so absorb the DoT without trading back, last-hit safely, and do not channel Q in the open where she simply moves off the impact zone.","mid":"Levels 4 through 6 are Lillia's control window — Blooming Blows cycles every 4-5 seconds, her Swirlseed (E) is a flat 12s slow that sets up repeated poke, and at 6 her R threatens any all-in. Use your E (Roar of the Slayer) armor shred only when she over-steps into your range; do not blow it chasing an accelerating target. At level 6 your Unstoppable Onslaught (R) can close her gap on paper, but Lilting Lullaby only reaches targets already carrying her Dream Dust — step in unpoked and it cannot touch you. Threaten the ult, then spend it on transit or objective engages with your jungler.","late":"At 2+ items you are a frontline she struggles to kill outright, but know what her damage actually is: Dream-Laden Bough burns a share of your MAX health over time and the outer edge of Blooming Blows is true damage, so armour and magic resist are the wrong buys — raw health and Heartsteel stacks are what hold up. Force brawls in tight corridors like Baron or Dragon pit where her kite range collapses; your zombie passive (Glory in Death) after a late death still threatens her team for several seconds. Lilting Lullaby needs Dream Dust on its target, so arriving unpoked blanks her 150s ult. If she is ahead, do not chase through open terrain — group and start fights with your R.","whys":["Lillia has no Blooming Blows stacks yet and no move-speed advantage, so a fully charged Q knockup from brush is unreadable and lands clean.","Blooming Blows stacks give her passive move speed that widens her orbit — your Q telegraph is now readable and she steps off the ground slam every time.","Full kit is live but her orbit is already established: E-slow into Q only if you catch her overstepping, otherwise accept poke and farm with W-shield soaking her DoT.","She loops Blooming Blows every 4-5 seconds and its outer edge adds true damage, while Swirlseed's flat 12s slow holds you inside it — you cannot charge Q without being dodged.","Lilting Lullaby only makes you drowsy if her passive Dream Dust is already on you — arrive unpoked and her 150s ult has nothing to land on, so spend yours on map pressure.","Heartsteel and Lillia's first AP item spike at near the same time, making this window genuinely contested — whoever executed the kiting phase better carries the item fight.","Her passive burns a share of your max health and her Q edge is true damage, so buy health over resistances; your W-shield and the zombie passive are what actually beat her."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"lucian","win":["Skill","Lucian","Lucian","Sion","Sion","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Lightslinger attacks shorten Relentless Pursuit but never reset it — at 16s he gets one dash, and Ardent Blaze's move speed does the rest of the kiting while your Q winds up."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught (R) closes the distance Lucian needs to kite you — one good R into a charged-Q knockup deletes his dash-based safety and forces a real fight."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel + Warmog's or a tank mythic means your W-shield value and HP-passive stacks compound enough that Lucian's double-shot burst stops killing you in two patterns."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"With two tank items your zombie passive guarantees trading value even on death — Lucian's damage cannot end a trade cleanly, and your size advantage wins every drawn-out brawl."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a full-charge Q knockup when Lucian's E dash is on cooldown to break his kite pattern","Use E armor-shred immediately after the knockup stun to maximize HP-passive brawl damage","Hit Level 6 or First Item before looking for extended trades — Lucian's early poke forces patience"],"foe":["Use Relentless Pursuit (E) to sidestep every charged-Q wind-up and keep you at range","Double-shot passive plus Q Piercing Light to burn your W-shield before it can detonate","Deny your HP-passive stacking by keeping trades short and dashing away before your W pops"]},"early":"Relentless Pursuit is a 16-second cooldown at rank 1 — its MANA cost falls to nothing with rank, but the dash never becomes spammable, and Lightslinger attacks only shave time off it. Charge Q once it is spent and he has to walk. Until then do not stand still in the open: he reads the telegraph, dashes back, and punishes with a double-shot and Piercing Light. Ardent Blaze is the other half of his kiting — a mark on you gives him move speed for attacking you. Pop W when he steps up, tank the poke, and last-hit at max range.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are a lull, but Level 6 changes everything. Your Unstoppable Onslaught (R) is the one tool in your kit that closes distance faster than Lucian's E can reposition. Commit with it while his 16s dash is down, then follow with a charged-Q ground-slam as the knockup locks him in place, and your E armor-shred lands for free. Two cautions: The Culling is a 1400-range torrent on a 110s cooldown, and Lightslinger gives him bonus magic damage on his next two attacks whenever a nearby enemy is immobilized — so a stun landing on a teammate arms him against you.","late":"Past First Item you are genuinely unkillable in a sustained exchange. Your W-shield scales with max HP, your HP-passive has been stacking every kill, and your zombie form guarantees you deal damage even through a bad trade — Lucian's double-shot cannot end a fight cleanly when the target refuses to stay dead. Force side-lane pressure: your R gives you the same transit speed Lucian uses to rotate, you win every extended 1v1 once your bulk is online, and his team will struggle to deal with a Sion who walks into their backline and dies twice before expiring. At 2+ items, catch one E cooldown, R into him, charged-Q, E-shred, and the lane is yours.","whys":["Relentless Pursuit is 16s at rank 1, so he gets one dash per trade — W-shield his double-shot, farm with the Q tip, and charge once that dash is spent, not before.","Lightslinger attacks only shave time off Relentless Pursuit, but he still owns short trades with Piercing Light — W-shield delays him and you cannot match his output at level 2.","His double-shot pattern plus Q poke out-trades your W-shield value at level 3 before you have enough HP stacking to absorb it — respect the range and charge Q only in fog.","Your full kit is online and his E is now on a real cooldown — angle around terrain and bait the dash before committing your Q wind-up; the stun window opens here.","Unstoppable Onslaught closes the gap Lucian relies on — R in while his 16s dash is down, land the charged-Q knockup, and E armor-shred makes the brawl entirely one-sided.","Heartsteel and your HP passive mean his double-shot burst no longer kills your W-shield in one pattern — you can force extended trades and your zombie passive guarantees value even in bad exchanges.","Two tank items and a full HP stack mean your W-shield absorbs his entire burst pattern and your zombie form deals real damage on death — Lucian has no percent-HP mechanic to punish your bulk, so you win every committed fight."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"malphite","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Skill","Skill","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"An early charged Q knockup on Malphite's slow immobile frame deals heavy damage before he has any meaningful ability to trade back — his only answer is his Q slow, which cannot stop a grounded slam."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Force comes online as his first all-in threat — 1000 range, a knockup you cannot dodge, 130s at rank 1. Track it and keep your own R for the counter-engage or the escape."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP compound begins here: every Malphite all-in you survive makes your max-HP passive larger, and your W-shield value scales directly with the growing HP pool."},{"when":"Late","text":"Malphite's armor-stacking makes him genuinely durable against physical damage, but his entire damage kit is magic — build Hollow Radiance or Force of Nature alongside your HP stack to negate his late relevance."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup on Malphite's immobile body, then follow with E armor-shred and a brawl he cannot win","Use W-shield to absorb his Q poke and detonate for a free trade whenever he steps up","Stack max HP with Heartsteel so your W-shield value and zombie passive threat dwarf his punishing window"],"foe":["Slow you with his Q so you can never close the gap to charge your Q","Stack armor until physical trades deal negligible damage and he body-blocks your entire pattern","Reach Unstoppable Force and R your team into an AoE knockup that wins a teamfight from across the map"]},"early":"At level 1, Malphite is one of the weakest duelists in the game — his only tool is a Q slow, and he has no burst, no engage, and no escape. Use brush or fog angles to charge your Q early; when it's full, he has no ability to sidestep a ground-slam that covers half a corridor. W-shield absorbs his Q poke for free, and detonating it back immediately makes every trade positive. The only thing to respect at level 1 is that his passive shield regenerates when he hasn't taken damage — try to deny it by threatening every few seconds rather than trading in long windows that let it regenerate.","mid":"Levels 4 through 6 are the matchup's gray zone. Seismic Shard is a flat 8s and Ground Slam a flat 7s, so his chip never really stops — but Ground Slam's damage is calculated from his ARMOR, which means your Roar of the Slayer shred cuts his damage at the same time as his durability. Shred first, then trade. Don't let him regenerate Granite Shield between poke intervals; keep constant Q-threat so he must choose between farming and refreshing it. His level 6 Unstoppable Force is the first real flip — a 1000-range charge into an unavoidable knockup on 130 seconds. Track it, play off the wall while it is up, and keep your own R for the re-engage or the peel.","late":"Your late game is a nightmare for Malphite's design: he stacks armour to reduce physical damage, but your W-shield's value scales with your max HP, your E shreds the armour his Ground Slam damage is calculated from, and your zombie passive forces him to disengage or absorb free damage even when you die. The live threat is that Seismic Shard, Ground Slam and Unstoppable Force are all magic damage that ignores how much HP you stacked, so build one MR item (Hollow Radiance works perfectly into him as a tank) to deny his scaling. With that coverage you are effectively unkillable here and he becomes a slow speedbump you zone for free.","whys":["Malphite has only a passive shield and a Q slow at level 1 — your charged Q is a grounded slam he cannot sidestep, and he has no burst to trade back before the knockup lands.","Neither side has a power spike at 2 — Seismic Shard steals your move speed for 3 seconds on a flat 8s cooldown, and a few untouched seconds regenerate Granite Shield, keeping it genuinely even.","Your W-shield absorbs his Q poke for near-zero net damage, and detonating it immediately after a charged Q stun means every trade cycle is positive — but his armor scaling is ticking up and he isn't far behind.","Malphite's armor itemization begins suppressing your Q damage noticeably here, and neither champion has a decisive kill window — the stage is a farm-and-wait corridor until his R comes up.","Unstoppable Force launches him 1000 units into a knockup you cannot sidestep, on 130s at rank 1 — from 6 the all-in is his whenever it is up, so hug the wall and count it down after every use.","Heartsteel makes your W-shield enormous and Roar of the Slayer strips the armour Ground Slam's damage is calculated from, cutting his output as well as his bulk — but his R still decides fights, so the stage stays contested.","Your E armor-shred negates his stacking, your W-shield scales with your HP pool, and your zombie passive forces him to disengage or absorb free damage — but his R remains an instant teamfight-winner so he retains map threat even if the 1v1 is yours."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"maokai","win":["Skill","Maokai","Maokai","Maokai","Maokai","Maokai","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Maokai's W root (Twisted Advance) locks you in place for his full sapling + Q knockback combo — these two levels are his sharpest window before you have W-shield practiced."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"His R (Nature's Grasp) gives him a lane-wide root wave he can stack during a trade; respect it or he chains CC into a full combo that chunks even your HP pool."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Your Heartsteel HP passive starts compounding here and your W-shield value scales with max HP — this is the first window you genuinely out-sustain his sapling damage in an extended fight."},{"when":"Late","text":"Both of you are immobile tanks who brawl well in teamfights, and the matchup returns to Skill — whoever lands their crowd control first (your charged Q stun vs his W root) decides the duel."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup when his W root (Twisted Advance) is on cooldown to prevent him from locking you in for his full sapling combo","W-shield his Q knockback (Bramble Smash) to eat the damage and immediately detonate for magic damage back","Use E armor-shred to reduce his tank stats and make every subsequent trade deal meaningful damage"],"foe":["Root you with W (Twisted Advance) to pin you inside a cluster of saplings and force you to eat the full AP burst","Speed up Sap Magic by trading spells with you, so his empowered attack heals back everything your chip damage takes","Use Q knockback (Bramble Smash) to interrupt your charged Q wind-up and punt you out of melee every time you try to hold a charge"]},"early":"Levels 1-3 are Maokai's window — his W root locks you in place before you have the feel for W-shield timing, and a rooted Sion standing inside three saplings takes a brutal burst of AP damage. The key habit here is pre-charging your Q from brush or fog-of-war so you arrive at close range with the stun already loaded; do NOT start a charge in the open or his Q knockback (Bramble Smash) will punt you out of it the moment the animation appears. If you get rooted, throw up W immediately to eat the sapling hits, then detonate it for return damage — you will not out-damage him without it.","mid":"Levels 4-5 give him zone control, but Sapling Toss is 18s at rank 1 — each one is a placed decision, not a blanket, so punish the seconds after he throws and walk the caster side where he has less brush to aim at. At 6 Nature's Grasp is a 3000-range wall of roots on a 130s cooldown that advances at you; caught holding a charge, you eat the root and the follow-up. Trade while it is down. Save your own R for the engage rather than the poke — it answers Twisted Advance — and since you have no dash, keep Flash for his wall, not for the damage.","late":"Late game this resolves back into a genuine tank mirror — your zombie passive (Glory in Death) means even a losing fight costs him HP he cannot ignore, and your W-shield scales into a massive absorb on a full HP stack. The duel comes down to who lands lockdown first, and to healing: Sap Magic's cooldown falls every time he casts a spell or is hit by one, so a long brawl without Grievous Wounds refunds him. Respect the root, do not start a charge with Twisted Advance up, and the matchup evens at two items where your HP accumulation matches his sustain.","whys":["Both of you arrive at level 1 with one ability — neither has a clear advantage without landing a skillshot first. Charged Q in the open is telegraph-able and his Q knockback can interrupt it.","Twisted Advance locks on from 525 and he is untargetable while it travels, so burst spent into it lands on nothing and you end up rooted — that is the whole short trade, on a 14s cooldown.","With all three basic abilities online, Maokai can root you, reposition with saplings, and knock you back out of melee whenever you try to hold a charge — he dictates every trade direction.","Sapling Toss is 18s at rank 1, so it is placement rather than spam — but Bramble Smash punts you out of a charge every 7s, and Sap Magic's cooldown drops each time he casts or is struck by a spell, so your poke feeds his regen.","His R (Nature's Grasp) adds a lane-width root wave on top of his existing lockdown toolkit; engaging into that root wave mid-charge Q is a death sentence at this stage.","His sapling AP sustain, root, and knock-back have been consistent all game and his item spike keeps that trade pattern intact — your Heartsteel HP is growing but not yet dominant enough to brawl through it.","At full HP stacking your W-shield absorbs most of a sapling burst and your zombie passive still costs him HP after the kill — both of you are equally dangerous in a sustained brawl, returning this to a genuine skill matchup."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"mel","win":["Skill","Mel","Skill","Sion","Sion","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Radiant Volley lands freely while your Decimating Smash charge is telegraphed and Soul Furnace is still thin — her best window to build a lead."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught closes the gap on a champion with no dash — bait Rebuttal (38s at rank 1) first, then charge from fog and the all-in is clean."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel’s HP stack makes your W-shield enormous and your zombie passive dangerous; Mel’s W-zone and execute lose punch against a 3,500-HP wall."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your full HP-stack and W-shield size are overwhelming; corner her with charged-Q and she has no reliable escape from the zombie passive either."}],"wants":{"you":["Corner her with a fully charged Q knockup — she has no dash to escape the telegraph once she’s near a wall","Detonate W-shield into her after landing E armor-shred to turn every trade into a net HP gain","Use R to close from fog of war after Rebuttal is spent — 38 seconds of it down at rank 1"],"foe":["Poke with Q from max range and force you to charge your Q into empty lane instead of trading","Use Rebuttal to reflect Roar of the Slayer back at you and block your damage while she resets the trade","Zone you off CS with Solar Snare's root and Radiant Volley so you fall behind on HP stacks"]},"early":"At level 1 neither of you has a clear advantage, but Radiant Volley starts to chip as soon as minions arrive — keep Soul Furnace available to eat one volley and immediately counter-charge your Q. At levels 2 and 3 she denies your charged-Q setup by holding max range and dropping Solar Snare to root you off the wave; her poke rhythm beats your charge time during this window. Focus on last-hitting with the tip of your Q, build Soul Furnace value by absorbing one poke and detonating it on the nearest minion, and do not hard-commit to a charged Q unless she over-extends past a wall where the knockup is guaranteed.","mid":"Level 4 onward your short-trade pattern shifts in your favor: pop Soul Furnace to eat Radiant Volley, then E to shred her armor and chase with a partially charged Q that at least slows and damages her even if not fully held. At level 6 your R is the equalizer — she has no dash, so an Unstoppable Onslaught from out of vision or around a terrain angle is nearly guaranteed to connect; once she is knocked up, your E-W-Q combo finishes the all-in before Rebuttal comes back up. Track Rebuttal (38s at rank 1) and all-in in the window when it is down.","late":"Two items in, Soul Furnace scales off an enormous HP total and Glory in Death forces her to burn spells just to finish the kill, letting your team follow up for free. Corner her in the side lane with terrain and she cannot outrun your R approach or kite your charged Q indefinitely. Respect Rebuttal — it reflects Roar of the Slayer straight back, so never open with it; walk in with Soul Furnace active, eat her retaliatory poke, then close with a charged Q once E has shredded her armor and she has nowhere to go.","whys":["Neither kit has a decisive level-1 edge — Mel’s Q poke is already online but your charged-Q hits hard if she stands still; farm safely and feel out her range.","Radiant Volley and Solar Snare land freely at levels 2-3, and each cast loads Searing Brilliance — the wind-up on her next attack is your cue to step back.","Your full kit is online but Radiant Volley poke and Rebuttal's reflect punish charging in open lane; bait it, then Soul Furnace eats the follow-up.","E armor-shred into a partially charged Q starts winning short trades, and her lack of a dash means one successful stun is a guaranteed combo.","Unstoppable Onslaught erases the range gap on a champion with no mobility — land it off a wall or from fog of war and your post-knockup burst is clean.","Heartsteel’s HP stack makes your W-shield absorb most of her burst pattern and your zombie passive forces extra spells even when you die, swinging fights in your favor.","Full HP-stack and giant W-shield make you nearly unkillable in side-lane; corner her with terrain and she has no escape from charged-Q or your zombie passive threat."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"mordekaiser","win":["Skill","Mordekaiser","Mordekaiser","Mordekaiser","Mordekaiser","Mordekaiser","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Mordekaiser's Realm of Death goes live — he drags you into a 1v1 and steals your stats; your W-shield and a charged Q stun are your only answers inside the realm."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Mordekaiser's first AP item (typically Riftmaker) fully unlocks his W shield sustain and Q isolated-bonus damage, making his Realm duel reliably lethal if you enter it low."},{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Neither champion has reliable engage at level 1 — Sion's charged Q is telegraphed and Morde has no dash, so the very first level is a genuine skill-matchup farm."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items your Heartsteel HP stack and zombie passive give you irreplaceable frontline presence, offsetting Morde's scaling with sheer bulk and an after-death damage threat he cannot ignore."}],"wants":{"you":["W-shield his E pull and Q combo to survive inside the Realm, then charged Q stun to break his follow-up","Use Unstoppable Onslaught (R) to engage or disengage before the Realm is up so you dictate when the 1v1 happens","Stack Heartsteel HP passively so your zombie passive and late-game bulk offset losing every lane trade"],"foe":["Pull you with his E and slam isolated Q bonus damage on a Sion he can hit repeatedly","Drag you into Realm of Death at low HP and steal your stats to snowball a lead he can't lose in isolation","Bank your chip damage into Indestructible and consume it as a heal, so a long grind refills him instead of you"]},"early":"Levels 1-3 are the one window where neither of you has an automatic advantage — Mordekaiser's E pull is short range and he has no dash, so he can only hurt you if you stand directly in his Q slam hitbox. Keep max distance, last-hit with the tip of your Q, and only commit to a charged Q knockup if he over-extends to zone you. W-shield his Q hit the instant his E lands, because the isolated bonus damage is the real chunk — absorbing even half of that with your shield completely neutralizes the trade. His level 3 all-in (E pull into Q into W shield pop) is his first real threat; back off at low health before you gift him a kill he can snowball into Realm dominance.","mid":"Levels 4-6 and particularly the moment Realm of Death unlocks are entirely his — he controls the pace of every duel and his Realm removes your team from the equation. Stay outside the 700-range Death's Grasp so he never gets the isolated Obliterate bonus. At level 6 respect the Realm hard: if he presses R, your only winning moves inside are to immediately W-shield, land a charged Q stun to interrupt his Q follow-up, and survive long enough that your zombie form eats his remaining HP. Start Doran's Shield, rush Heartsteel, and get Grievous Wounds on the way — Indestructible turns every long grind into a heal, so anti-heal is what makes the Realm duel contestable. Re-engage ONLY when his Realm is down (140s at rank 1) — that is your actual window.","late":"At 2+ items the matchup evens out to its 49.97% coin-flip reality: your Heartsteel HP total is enormous, your W-shield blocks a meaningful chunk of his combo, and your zombie passive forces him to keep attacking a corpse that is still dealing damage — he cannot simply walk away after killing you. He never gets true percent-HP damage or range, so your bulk genuinely matters against him where it doesn't against Vayne or Gwen. Focus on engaging as a frontline with Unstoppable Onslaught when his Realm is down so your team can collapse; deny him isolated 1v1 opportunities in the side lane where his Realm would be decisive.","whys":["Neither champion has a reliable level-1 engage — Morde has no dash and your charged Q is too slow to land on a patient opponent, so the opener is a farm-or-die skill check.","Death's Grasp pull into an isolated Obliterate is online at level 2, and Obliterate is a 8s cooldown; trade only with Soul Furnace already up.","At level 3 he has E-Q-W available and can combo you for half your HP if you let him step in — respect the range, last-hit safely, charged Q from outside his pull distance.","Levels 4-5 he roams for a zone pick or just bullies you off CS; your charged Q has no target to punish and his W sustains through your poking, so accept the farm loss and stack HP.","Realm of Death goes live and it is his best window all game — low-HP Sion dragged into an isolated stat-steal duel usually dies; only fight the Realm with W-shield ready and a charged Q stun prepared.","His first AP item makes his Realm duel near-certain-kill if you enter below 60% HP; Heartsteel or Hollow Radiance must be rushing so your HP total tilts the 1v1 back toward a contest.","Your stacked HP total and zombie passive make you a legitimate frontline threat he cannot burst or ignore — the matchup converges to a coin flip at 2+ items, exactly what the 49.97% WR reflects."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"nasus","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Skill","Skill","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"A fully charged Q knockup before Wither is online turns the first wave into half Nasus's HP — he has no answer if he can't move."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught launches from 7500 range, well outside Wither's 700 — bait the slow (15s at rank 1), then charge and he has no escape."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Nasus hits Sheen or Divine Sunderer first and his empowered Q suddenly one-shots your W-shield, marking the moment his stacks become a real threat."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 400+ stacks Siphoning Strike deletes Soul Furnace in one proc, and Fury of the Sands burns max HP — your HP stack is not armour against it."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup on Nasus before he can activate Wither, punishing his immobile stance","Pop W-shield during his empowered Q hits to survive the trade and brawl back with E armor-shred","Freeze the wave near your tower at 100-200 stacks to starve his Q farm and force him into your engage range"],"foe":["Land Wither before your Q finishes charging to root your engage pattern entirely","Stack Q safely on frozen waves behind tower where your charge time telegraphs every approach","Pop Fury of the Sands to burn a share of your max HP and out-armour your stacked bulk"]},"early":"At level 1 Nasus has no movement tool and his only defense is blocking your approach — walk toward him in the minion wave and start charging Q as he inevitably steps up to last-hit. A full charge knockup before Wither is online (level 2) is essentially free because he cannot move away during your windup. Level 2 is the key inflection: the moment he hits level 2 he has Wither, which cuts your movement and attack speed so severely that a channeling Q gets completely nullified. Your window before he levels W is your clearest aggression in the whole lane. From level 3 onward every charged Q attempt must account for Wither's 15-second cooldown — track it like your life depends on it, because it does.","mid":"Levels 4 through 6 are a patience game where whoever wastes a cooldown first loses the trade. Your W-shield eats one empowered Q proc but not two consecutive ones, so never let him land Wither and then walk into a brawl — that combination will delete your shield and leave you slowed in melee range. When Wither is on cooldown, run the full pattern: E-slow to close distance, then let the Q channel complete for the knockup; his zero-mobility kit has no answer once you're on top of him. At level 6 Unstoppable Onslaught answers Wither by range, not by speed: you launch from 7500 units and Wither only reaches 700, so the charge is committed before he can age you. Bait the 15-second cooldown first, then immediately E into a charged Q for the kill attempt.","late":"Two items in, Nasus becomes a genuine problem: 400+ Q stacks delete your W-shield in one proc, his R's lifesteal sustains through your zombie passive, and his size makes your charged Q easier to interrupt by stepping behind minions. Your win condition from here is not to duel him straight — it is to use your zombie passive to zone him off objectives while your team collapses, and to force the fight before his R is up. Your Unstoppable Onslaught still threatens him in the open but he simply outruns you in sustained damage at high stacks. Respect the math: a fed Nasus with 500 stacks and Sheen deletes Sion, but a Sion who banked a lead in the stack-denial window and is roaming with R has already won the game before that scaling matters.","whys":["Nasus has no Wither at level 1 and cannot move away from a charging Q — hit him before he hits 2.","W-shield plus a charged Q knockup before Wither is online gives you the cleanest damage trade in the lane.","Wither is up and Nasus has full kit, so both patterns cancel each other out — trade only around Wither's cooldown from here.","He is stacking safely and your Q channel is visible from long range — freeze the wave and deny CS rather than force a telegraphed all-in.","Unstoppable Onslaught launches from 7500 range, far outside his 700-range Wither — fire it once he has spent the slow and he cannot answer.","Nasus completes Sheen or Divine Sunderer and his empowered Q now one-shots your W-shield — respect his damage curve and stop short trading.","Both kits scale into the late game and Nasus's stacks are high enough to threaten — win conditions split; avoid the straight duel, play for objectives."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"nautilus","win":["Skill","Skill","Sion","Sion","Sion","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Full Q-E-W kit online — E-shred his armor, then walk him into a charged Q knockup; Nautilus has no mobility to escape the combo once the stun lands."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught turns every Dredge Line whiff into a death sentence — R through him and follow with the full Q stun before he recovers."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel completion compounds your Passive's max-HP stacks — your W-shield value spikes and you become too tanky for Nautilus's trading pattern to dent you."},{"when":"Nautilus Hook","text":"Dredge Line is his only real threat: hooked, you have no dash out, so his Staggering Blow root chain lands free — hold Flash for the hook, not the damage."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup when Dredge Line is on cooldown and Nautilus has walked into your range","Use E to shred his armor mid-trade so your W-shield detonation and follow-up autos delete his HP pool","Hold the wave near your side of the lane so Nautilus must overextend into your charged-Q range to farm"],"foe":["Hook you with Dredge Line and chain Staggering Blow roots — you have no dash to answer","Force you to push the wave so you are overextended and exposed to a roaming jungler assist","Avoid your fully charged Q by staying at max auto range and never walking into brush-side angles"]},"early":"Nautilus level 1 has nothing but auto-attack poke — his passive roots on every auto but you have the HP to eat it and retaliate. Do not charge a Q into his face when he can root you mid-charge; instead last-hit with tip of Q and let him commit first. At level 2 the moment his Dredge Line is on cooldown you can walk up and begin charging from brush: he has zero mobility to escape a full-charge knockup and the stun duration lets you land E armor-shred plus W detonation for a massive first-blood-level trade.","mid":"From level 3 onward you run the lane. Every trade cycle is Q-telegraph into E-shred into W-shield burst — Roar of the Slayer's armour shred plus your shield volume beat his rotation. The only check is Dredge Line (14s at rank 1, 1150 range): once it whiffs into terrain he has nothing left but Riptide's slow, so commit the moment he is inside charged-Q range. At level 6 Unstoppable Onslaught makes every Dredge Line whiff a kill attempt — R in, land the full charged Q, and his HP pool cannot survive the armour-shredded brawl once Titan's Wrath expires.","late":"Your late game against Nautilus is a free side-lane stomp: he cannot 1v1 a two-item Sion whose W-shield alone absorbs his entire trading rotation, and your zombie passive means he risks losing a fight even after killing you. Play the wave to force him into a 1v1 or let your team engage while you absorb Dredge Line as a frontline target. The only thing that can go wrong is a roam-heavy Nautilus buying time for his carries — track Depth Charge (120s at rank 1) and treat his missing-from-lane timer as a call to match the roam with your own R transit or take the tower.","whys":["Nautilus level 1 is pure auto-poke with no engage — trade minimally, track his passive-root range, and deny CS at Q tip so he gets nothing for free.","Dredge Line is up (14s at rank 1) so respect the 1150-range hook angle, but Soul Furnace already eats his auto damage; probe with a short Q to set up level 3.","Full Q-E-W kit: E-shred his armor first, then charge Q from brush — he has no dash or mobility to escape the knockup and you detonate W for massive trade damage.","His Dredge Line controls whether you all-in; use this window to freeze near your tower and deny him CS, banking the HP lead for a clean Heartsteel spike.","Unstoppable Onslaught closes any gap Nautilus tries to create — R into range then charge Q; your zombie passive means he cannot safely trade kills even at 6.","Heartsteel stacks meet your Passive's max-HP bonus — your W-shield is enormous and your zombie is a genuine threat; force fights immediately on his Dredge Line cooldown.","Two-item Sion cannot be outdamaged by Nautilus's kit; side-lane, absorb his R in teamfights, and let your zombie passive punish any overzealous engage he attempts."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"neeko","win":["Sion","Skill","Skill","Skill","Neeko","Neeko","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Your zombie passive (Glory in Death) means Neeko cannot bully you off the wave for free at level 1 — a partial Q charge punishes any overstep."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Inherent Glamour hides Pop Blossom's wind-up entirely — a disguised Neeko landing it on a rooted Sion is a near-certain kill; charge Unstoppable Onslaught out of the ring."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Luden's or Shadowflame gives Neeko a burst spike that outpaces your Heartsteel timing — her one combo can chunk you even through W-shield until you hit 2 items."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Full Heartsteel and Warmog's stacking walls her entire burst combo; one landed full-charge Q into E-shred and W-detonate kills her, and your zombie passive means she cannot trade your death for free."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait or dodge Tangle-Barbs (E) root, then immediately commit the full Q-charge knockup into E-shred and W-shield detonate","Angle Q charges behind minion bodies so Neeko walks into the arc and cannot sidestep","Stack Heartsteel HP to reach 2 items where her burst is swallowed and your W-shield value dwarfs her damage"],"foe":["Land Tangle-Barbs (E) root from range and unload Q pops plus her burst combo before you can W-shield","Use Inherent Glamour's disguise to hide the Pop Blossom wind-up and stun you out of a landed root","Kite you off the wave with Q poke and deny your charged Q pattern by respecting no CC windows"]},"early":"Blooming Burst reaches 800 and Tangle-Barbs 1000, so she roots you from outside your own E range — a poke lane, not a walk-up one. Take Doran's Shield and absorb Q ticks while looking for a partial Q charge when she steps forward. Hold charges behind minion bodies on the side she approaches from; do not chase her into open ground where she roots you mid-channel. At level 1 your zombie passive means she cannot freely burst you off the lane — even your death costs her.","mid":"At 6 the dynamic shifts — a disguised Neeko standing still near the wave is Pop Blossom winding up, and Inherent Glamour hides that wind-up completely. Charge Unstoppable Onslaught out of the ring or walk clear before it lands. Between level 6 and first item farm safely and punish only on a missed Tangle-Barbs (10-12s); never all-in while her root or her 120-second ult is up. This is the one window she can kill you, so wait for your Heartsteel timing.","late":"Full Heartsteel plus Warmog's stacking makes you nearly unkillable by a single AP mage — her burst combo barely dents your HP wall and your W-shield absorbs the follow-up. Focus her in teamfights; she melts to physical damage and your Unstoppable Onslaught catches her from across the map. In a sidelane 1v1 she can only kill you if she lands Tangle-Barbs into Pop Blossom; otherwise your HP wall and W-shield outlast her burst and your charged Q into E-shred ends her.","whys":["Blooming Burst reaches 800 and Tangle-Barbs 1000, so she out-ranges you — but Glory in Death means her kill attempt is never free, and a partial Q charge punishes any overstep.","Tangle-Barbs (E) comes online and immediately threatens to root you into burst; your W-shield also unlocks — pop it the instant her root hits to absorb the Q follow-up pops.","Full kits on both sides; angle Q charges along the wave so she cannot sidestep, she wants to root you and burst — neither wins cleanly unless the other misses their CC.","Farm window with no decisive power spike for either side; respect Tangle-Barbs completely and punish only when it is down or wasted.","Pop Blossom's wind-up is hidden while Inherent Glamour has her disguised — landed on a rooted Sion it is a near-certain kill, and Unstoppable Onslaught is your only clean exit.","Luden's or Shadowflame gives her burst that outpaces Heartsteel timing — her one combo chunks you even through W-shield until you reach 2 items.","Full Heartsteel and Warmog's stacking walls her entire burst; one landed full-charge Q into E-shred and W-detonate kills her, and your zombie passive means she cannot trade your death for free."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"olaf","win":["Olaf","Olaf","Olaf","Olaf","Olaf","Olaf","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Undertow slows and shreds armour, and Reckless Swing's true damage punishes your slow Q charge — he wins every short trade before Soul Furnace is a real deterrent."},{"when":"Ragnarok","text":"The moment Olaf presses Ragnarok he is immune to disables for as long as he keeps attacking — never burn a charged Decimating Smash stun into that window."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Once Heartsteel starts compounding, your HP pool grows fast enough that Olaf's true damage per auto becomes a lower percentage of your total — the lane tightens considerably."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"With a full tank mythic and Heartsteel stacks banked, your zombie passive threatens genuine kill pressure even when you die, forcing Olaf to respect trades he would otherwise accept freely."}],"wants":{"you":["Charge Q before Ragnarok is available so your stun has real CC value and is not wasted on an immune target","Use Soul Furnace on his step-up so the shield eats his autos — Reckless Swing's true damage it cannot stop","Build anti-heal second item and mix in armor so his lifesteal sustain does not let him freely extend every winning trade"],"foe":["Land Undertow to slow and shred you, then walk up for a 325-range Reckless Swing","Press Ragnarok before your charge lands so the stun is wasted for as long as he keeps attacking you, then brawl","Deny you the wave so your Heartsteel stacking stalls and each Reckless Swing stays a big slice of your total HP bar"]},"early":"At level 1 Olaf throws Undertow to slow and shred your armour, then steps in for Reckless Swing — 325 range, true damage, and no resist of yours reduces it. Do not charge a full Q while slowed; his attack speed under Tough It Out beats your charge time. Farm with partial Q taps from max range, keep the wave slightly frozen toward your tower, and never walk over a thrown axe: picking it up resets Undertow instantly. Pop W defensively to stay healthy, not offensively to trade back.","mid":"At level 4-5 Olaf has his full combo and Ragnarok is fresh every fight, so every trade you start risks burning your charged-Q stun into a champion who ignores disables while he keeps attacking. Save the fully charged Q for the gap between Ragnarok casts (100s at rank 1) — when the icon is grey, that is your all-in window. Roar of the Slayer shreds his armour and sets up the stun, but only commit that sequence with Ragnarok down, and never chase over a thrown axe: picking it up resets Undertow. Level 6 gives you Unstoppable Onslaught to disengage or chase, but it does not flip the lane.","late":"Once Heartsteel has 200+ stacks and you have a tank mythic, your HP pool grows large enough that a Reckless Swing hit is a smaller fraction of your total and your zombie passive becomes a genuine zone threat. The 49% win rate reflects this near-parity — neither of you dominates the other cleanly at full build. Play for teamfight engage with Unstoppable Onslaught, target the squishies behind Olaf rather than trying to out-duel him 1v1, and your W-shield plus zombie form give you enough staying power to anchor the frontline even if he wins the isolated skirmish.","whys":["Undertow slows and shreds armour at level 1, then Reckless Swing hits for true damage at 325 range — he wins the first skirmish before Soul Furnace exists to blunt any of it.","Undertow is online and Reckless Swing's true damage (11s at rank 1) beats your short-charge Q tap — he wins every trade where you lack a fully charged knockup.","A fully charged Q knockup is your one strong tool, but Undertow slows and shreds armour during the charge and Reckless Swing punishes the windup — he still edges this stage.","Olaf peaks pre-R at levels 4-5: Undertow resets its 9s cooldown when he picks the axe back up, Reckless Swing's true damage ignores your resists, and Berserker Rage heals it back.","At level 6 Ragnarok makes him immune to disables while he keeps attacking, so your charged-Q stun does nothing — the 100-second cooldown is the only window your all-in exists in.","Ragnarok is up every 100 seconds and Berserker Rage gives him more lifesteal the lower he drops, outpacing your Heartsteel stacking — he still edges the 1v1 into your first big item.","Your Heartsteel HP pool is finally large enough that his per-auto true damage is diluted, your zombie passive forces respect even on losing trades, and the matchup reaches genuine statistical parity — neither side dominates cleanly."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"ornn","win":["Skill","Skill","Ornn","Ornn","Ornn","Ornn","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Volcanic Rupture's pillar plus a Searing Charge knockup is his best window — he charges into terrain, knocks you up, and Bellows Breath makes you Brittle."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Call of the Forge God (140s at rank 1) slows and Brittles anyone it runs over, and he can recast to redirect it — respect it and play at the wave, not under the tower."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Ornn self-upgrades Forgefire Cape into Ornn's Upgraded version, widening his durability lead — this is his peak spike; W-shield every trade and wait for him to waste Searing Charge."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your Heartsteel stacks compound: by the time both players have 2+ items your raw HP total dwarfs Ornn's and your W-shield value scales with it, letting you absorb his Brittle combos and brawl him down."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup when Searing Charge (E) is on cooldown so he cannot wall-shove you during your setup","W-shield his Brittle-proc combo to deny the amplified burst, then retaliate with E armor-shred into a follow-up Q","Stack HP through Heartsteel kill stacks and W-shield farming so your 2+ item bulk overwhelms his Brittle output"],"foe":["Plant a Volcanic Rupture pillar, then Searing Charge into it so the knockup lands where you cannot walk out","Catch you in the final gout of Bellows Breath so you are Brittle for the follow-up burst","Run Call of the Forge God through you for the slow and Brittle, then recast it to cut off any clean escape"]},"early":"At Level 1 and 2 neither of you has a meaningful combo — Ornn has his Q pillar poke but no pillar-and-charge delivery yet, so short W-shield trades are even. Play at max auto range, use your W-shield to eat one Bellows Breath gout and shove it back, and never charge Q in the open at this stage because his Q slow will stagger your approach and you will net nothing. At Level 3 his kit is fully online: Volcanic Rupture plants the pillar, Searing Charge slams into it for the knockup, and the final gout of Bellows Breath makes you Brittle for the burst. Your charged Q is too slow to preempt this — he initiates it, not you. Stay near the centre of the lane away from both walls and any fresh pillar, and save your W-shield for the Searing Charge landing.","mid":"Levels 4 through 6 are Ornn's best stretch. His pillar-into-charge combo is on short cooldowns, he self-upgrades his first item into an enhanced version that outpaces your own durability climb, and his Level 6 Call of the Forge God adds a displacement that can confirm any all-in. Your job here is survival and HP stacking: W-shield every Brittle sequence, keep the wave near river so you have backing room, and never brawl him at half HP once his R is available. Your charged Q can land if you bait out Searing Charge first — it is 14 seconds at rank 1, and without it he has no delivery into his own pillar, so punish with Q-stun into E armour-shred. But this window is short; take the small trades, not the all-in.","late":"Once both of you reach 2+ items your Heartsteel HP stack total starts to dwarf Ornn's base durability, and your W-shield now absorbs a meaningful chunk of his Brittle proc burst — the brawl shifts in your favor. He retains his CC tools, so never charge Q near a wall, but in open ground your HP advantage combined with your zombie passive means trading into his Brittle combos costs him more than it costs you. In teamfights your Unstoppable Onslaught R engages over walls and his knockups confirm for your team even if he peels with his own R — your kits stack rather than cancel. This is why the matchup ends in your favor overall despite his dominant early windows: you scale harder, survive his peak, and brawl him into the ground with your HP mass.","whys":["Neither kit has a clean engage at Level 1 — Ornn has no wall-shove yet and your charge time is too long to force; auto-trade into W-shield and identify where the walls are before committing anything.","Bellows Breath is online — its final gout is what makes you Brittle, not his autos — but without a pillar his Searing Charge has nothing to slam you into; hold Q until E is committed.","Volcanic Rupture's pillar into a Searing Charge knockup into a Brittle gout is live — he picks when the trade starts, so fight centre-lane away from terrain.","Volcanic Rupture is a 9s cooldown and Searing Charge 14s, so he still picks the trades — play for Soul Furnace value and CS, and never charge Q near a wall or a fresh pillar.","Call of the Forge God (140s at rank 1) makes you Brittle itself and he can recast to slam the elemental back through you — play at the wave, keep your HP up, and only all-in once it has been spent.","His self-upgraded first item spikes his durability above your own at this stage; W-shield every Brittle combo and wait for a Searing Charge misstep before you commit your charged Q all-in.","Your Heartsteel HP total exceeds his durability at 2+ items and your W-shield absorbs a punishing share of his Brittle proc burst — sustain into his CC and brawl him down with your raw mass."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"pantheon","win":["Skill","Pantheon","Pantheon","Skill","Skill","Pantheon","Pantheon"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Empowered Comet Spear and a point-click Shield Vault dictate early trades — Aegis Assault (his E) then blocks damage from the front while he harasses freely."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Pantheon peaks here: Comet Spear at 9s plus Mortal Will means constant empowered poke — stay high on HP or he chunks you under your own tower."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Both ultimates arrive simultaneously — Pantheon's R is a global roam tool while yours is an engage; whoever uses theirs to create an advantage first swings the map."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your Heartsteel stacks compound into a max-HP pool so large that Pantheon's pre-6 dueling kit cannot dent it, and your zombie passive guarantees damage return even if he somehow kills you."}],"wants":{"you":["Hold W-shield for his point-click W stun all-in and detonate it during his combo to deny his burst window","Land the fully charged Q knockup once Shield Vault is spent — 13 seconds at rank 1 with no dash to answer","Stack HP through Heartsteel into a pool large enough that his falling-off item-scaling cannot threaten you"],"foe":["Stack empowered Q poke charges by trading short and retreating before you can charge your Q","Open with the Shield Vault auto reset and empowered Comet Spear while your shield is down","Abandon lane pressure and use Grand Starfall globally on a collapsing fight your lagging HP cannot answer"]},"early":"Pantheon comes out of the gates hitting hard: Mortal Will empowers his next spell every few actions, Shield Vault (W) is a point-click dash-stun on 13 seconds at rank 1 that also resets his auto-attack, and Comet Spear (Q) pokes every 11. Do not charge your Q in the open — he reads the animation and walks out, then Shield Vaults you mid-channel for a free trade. Stay at wave-tip range, take Doran's Shield, and pop W the instant his auto winds up rather than when the ability lights up, because that reset is the real start of his burst. Trade back only once Shield Vault is spent.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are Pantheon's sustained pressure window: Comet Spear returns every 9-11 seconds and Mortal Will keeps empowering it, so he chips you for free. Keep your HP above 40%, burn W-shield proactively to stay healthy, and be patient — your charged Q outranges his 600-unit Shield Vault whenever he steps up to last-hit. Never dump a combo into Aegis Assault: it blocks damage from the front for the whole channel, so walk around it or wait it out. At level 6 his Grand Starfall (180s at rank 1) threatens the map but costs him lane priority, so track his recall timer, shove on his rotation, and match the roam with Unstoppable Onslaught if your jungler can convert.","late":"Once Heartsteel and a tank mythic are complete, this matchup reverses. Pantheon's kit is front-loaded: his Q damage, W stun, and E Aegis Assault were designed to win the first 15 minutes, not trade into 5,000 HP. Your W-shield value scales with your max HP, your zombie passive makes killing you cost him full combo plus passive-follow-up damage, and your E armor shred opens him up for jungle follow-up. Land the charged Q knockup in a side-lane fight and your full Q-E combo plus passive reanimate will out-trade anything he has in his kit at this point in the game.","whys":["Mortal Will empowers his next spell every few actions, and Shield Vault is a dash-stun — your Q charge animation is visible and he punishes it freely at level 1.","His empowered Q poke depletes between exchanges; he banks passive stacks and controls spacing before you have your W-shield value online.","With all three basics he chains Shield Vault's stun into an empowered Comet Spear and hides behind Aegis Assault — Soul Furnace eats the burst but nothing answers the stun startup.","Levels 4-5 are his bullying peak: Comet Spear is down to 9s and Mortal Will keeps arriving, so repeated empowered pokes keep you reactive with no spike to trade back.","Both ultimates come online simultaneously; neither has a decisive lane edge — his R is a global tool, yours is an engage, and the outcome depends on macro reads more than direct combat.","At first item your HP stack is not yet dominant and his burst kit still threatens you; the ledge is genuine but slim — fight only when his W is down and your W-shield is up.","Your Heartsteel HP pool dwarfs his scaling damage output; W-shield alone absorbs his full combo, your charged Q knockup into E armor-shred wins every extended fight, and your zombie passive guarantees trade value even in a worst-case death scenario."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"poppy","win":["Skill","Poppy","Poppy","Sion","Poppy","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Heroic Charge into a wall comes fully online — keep wall distance so she cannot stun you into a Hammer Shock zone."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Keeper's Verdict (140s at rank 1) is her anti-engage — she is untargetable in the channel and the knockback resets your all-in, so bait it before committing."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP stack starts compounding: your W-shield value scales with max HP and you become noticeably harder to kill in the short trades Poppy needs."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items your zombie passive alone zones Poppy off objectives — she has no execute or percent-HP tool to punish your HP mountain."}],"wants":{"you":["Land the fully charged Q knockup — Steadfast Presence stops dashes, and a ground slam is not one","Keep wall distance so her Heroic Charge (E) can never stun you into a free Q knockup","Bait her Keeper's Verdict (R) before using Unstoppable Onslaught so she cannot interrupt and reverse your engage"],"foe":["Pin you to a wall with Heroic Charge (E) for a free Q knockup combo before you can raise your W-shield","Use Keeper's Verdict (R) to knock you airborne and interrupt Unstoppable Onslaught mid-charge","Force short auto-trades where Steadfast Presence's armour and MR blunt you and Hammer Shock's zone chips your HP before every commit"]},"early":"At level 1 this is genuinely even — neither of you has meaningful CC online yet and both are melee with short-trade kits. The moment she hits level 2 the dynamic shifts: her Heroic Charge (E) wall-stun is live and she will angle every trade to pin you against the lane wall for a free Q knockup. Stay in the middle of the lane, never hug the side brush walls, and raise your W-shield before she closes to absorb the Q hit. If she hits the wall stun you eat a full knockup with no response — that is the one combo she wins the whole lane on.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are your relative breathing room — she has all her tools but no ultimate and her damage without the wall stun is modest. You can start holding a charged Q in the brush angle to stun her when she walks up to CS; the long charge time is less punishing now because she has no disengage dash to escape the telegraph. Steadfast Presence only stops dashes and you have none, so the charged knockup always threatens. At 6 she gets Keeper's Verdict and the equation shifts back — she is untargetable while she channels it and the knockback throws you clean out of the fight. Never start Unstoppable Onslaught into a Poppy holding R; bait the Verdict by walking at her, disengage when the channel flashes, and re-engage while the 140-second cooldown burns.","late":"Once Heartsteel is stacking and your HP passive has compounded through several kills or assists, the math flips decisively in your favour. Poppy has no percent-HP damage and no execute — she simply cannot kill you efficiently through a 4,000+ HP bar, and your W-shield grows proportionally with every item. Use your Unstoppable Onslaught for splitpush transit or team engages after confirming her R is down; she cannot peel your zombie passive off an objective and the chip damage alone wins vision contests. The one trap to avoid: never use R into a Poppy standing next to terrain with R available — she will knock you into a wall mid-flight and the charge fizzles.","whys":["Neither kit has CC online at level 1 — it is a pure auto-trade that Poppy neither wins nor loses cleanly before her E is available.","Heroic Charge (E, 14s at rank 1) pins you to a wall for the stun, and Hammer Shock's zone explodes on top of you — hugging the side walls loses this lane outright.","With Heroic Charge and Hammer Shock both up her stun-into-zone pattern is assembled, and you have no answer to the wall angle unless Soul Furnace is already raised.","She has all her tools but no ultimate, and your charged Q (a ground slam her W passive does not block) threatens a stun every time she walks up to CS — the lane stabilises in your favour.","Keeper's Verdict (R) comes online and interrupts Unstoppable Onslaught mid-charge; she retakes the threat window because your primary engage tool is now countered until you bait the R out.","Heartsteel HP begins compounding and your W-shield value scales with max HP — short trades now cost Poppy more than they cost you and she has no percent-HP tool to punish the bulk.","At full item she has no execute or true damage to cut through your HP mountain, and your zombie passive threatens objectives even when you are dying — the scaling race is decisively yours."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"quinn","win":["Quinn","Quinn","Quinn","Quinn","Quinn","Quinn","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"Vault comes online (12s at rank 1) and it interrupts your charge, landing her at max attack range — she dictates trades through her first item."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Her ultimate lets her leave the lane entirely, punish your immobility on the map, and return before you can accomplish anything in her absence."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Kraken Slayer or Galeforce completion is her power peak — her kite pattern is near-perfect and your HP wall is not online yet to absorb it."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Heartsteel plus a second tank item crosses the threshold where Quinn cannot kill you in a straight fight — your W-shield absorbs her burst and your zombie passive wins any engage she takes."}],"wants":{"you":["Commit the moment Vault is spent — 12 seconds at rank 1 with no second escape","W-shield her Harrier poke on cooldown and detonate for chip damage back","Track her R roams and push the wave every time she leaves to deny plates"],"foe":["Vault your charge to interrupt it, then land back at max attack range and poke","Hinder your vision with Blinding Assault so you lose sight of her mid-trade","Roam with her ultimate and punish your immobility while denying you a wave to push"]},"early":"Quinn owns this stretch entirely. Her Harrier passive gives her a speed burst on every auto, letting her poke and backpedal before your Q charge completes. Do not attempt a charged Q in the open — she reads the telegraph, presses Vault (12s at rank 1), interrupts the charge, and lands back at max attack range with Harrier ready again. Farm with the tip of your Q, W-shield her trades and detonate the shield for chip damage back, and accept that your only objective here is zero deaths and maximum HP passive stacks from last-hits.","mid":"At levels 4 through 6 Quinn roams with her R — call it to your team and shove the wave to deny plates every time she leaves. Behind Enemy Lines is a channel: a landed Decimating Smash or an Unstoppable Onslaught collision cancels it outright, so a Quinn taking off beside you is a free interrupt. If she stays, W-shield absorbs her poke and you charge Q only from brush cover. The window you own is a spent Vault, 12 seconds at rank 1 — land the full knockup, E armour-shred, and brawl.","late":"Two items changes the matchup completely. Your Heartsteel HP wall combined with a second tank item puts you past the threshold Quinn cannot burst through in a straight fight — her Vault resets your zombie passive threat rather than saving her. W-shield absorbs her burst and your E armor-shred punishes any fight she has to take. Force sidelane pressure where she must duel you or lose the structure, and let your passive zombie threat deter any peel she tries to set up for her carries.","whys":["Her Harrier passive procs on the first auto and her Q-poke is free damage you cannot answer — farm at Q range and do not step up to charge anything.","Vault is online (12s at rank 1) and it interrupts you mid-charge, then drops her back at max attack range — every open commit just feeds it.","Soul Furnace is up, but Vault interrupts your charge outright and Blinding Assault hinders your vision — she controls every engagement.","Her Harrier kite speed keeps her permanently outside your charge range; she only enters when Vault is ready to bail her out.","Her ultimate lets her roam and punish your immobility on the map — she can leave, fight elsewhere, and return before you accomplish anything.","She completes Kraken Slayer or Galeforce and her kite pattern becomes near-perfect — your HP stack is not yet online to absorb it.","Heartsteel plus a second tank item puts you past the burst threshold she needs — your zombie passive wins any engage she takes, and E armor-shred punishes the fight she is forced into."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"renekton","win":["Renekton","Renekton","Renekton","Renekton","Renekton","Skill","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Empowered Ruthless Predator at 50 Fury stuns 1.5 seconds and DESTROYS Soul Furnace outright — do not open a trade by shielding, because the shield is the first thing he eats."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Renekton's R (Dominus) gives him bonus HP and AOE damage, making his all-in lethal if you're already below half health — this is his last meaningful power window before he starts fading."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Your Heartsteel passive begins compounding kills and assists into permanent max HP stacks; by the time it fully procs you become noticeably harder to kill than Renekton in sustained brawls."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"With two tank items and hundreds of bonus HP stacked, your W shield absorbs his entire combo and your zombie passive guarantees you deal damage even if he somehow kills you — Renekton has no answer for this."}],"wants":{"you":["Hold Soul Furnace until after his empowered stun lands — cast early and Ruthless Predator simply destroys it","Survive to first item by staying out of his empowered-W range when he has 50+ fury, then force trades once your HP passive kicks in","Land a fully charged Q (Decimating Smash) knockup when he overextends — it sets up your E armor shred and turns the first real brawl in your favor"],"foe":["Build Fury with Cull the Meek on the wave, then Slice and Dice in for an empowered Ruthless Predator every 16 seconds at rank 1","Punish any charged-Q telegraph with a Slice and Dice through you, then answer with the Ruthless Predator stun","End the lane decisively before your HP passive and tank items come online, because Renekton has almost no damage contribution once you scale"]},"early":"Renekton's levels 1-3 are the strongest point of this lane, and you need to respect that honestly. Cull the Meek (Q, 7s) swings around him for damage and healing while building Fury, and Slice and Dice (E) is the dash that closes the gap. Once he reaches 50 Fury his empowered Ruthless Predator stuns for 1.5 seconds and destroys Soul Furnace outright — shielding into it hands him the shield for free. Do NOT charge your Q in the open at level 1; the telegraph is too long and he dashes straight through it. Start W, absorb one trade, and accept the lane is his until you have levels. Watch his auto-attack wind-up rather than the ability icon, because Ruthless Predator resets his attack and that wind-up is the real start of the combo. At level 3 you can fight back: charge from brush, and re-engage the moment his Fury is spent.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are still his window but the all-in threat diminishes — his kit is fully online but his damage doesn't scale with items the way yours does. Hold Soul Furnace for after his stun resolves rather than before it, and never fight into a Fury bar above 50; Ruthless Predator is 16 seconds at rank 1. Buy Grievous Wounds early, because Cull the Meek heals him through every extended trade. At level 6 Renekton hits his final spike with Dominus (R) — bonus HP and an AOE aura make his all-in lethal if you are low, so back off below 40%. Your Unstoppable Onslaught is a threat tool here: roam pressure or a gap-close if he overextends, not a direct engage into his R. Survive to the first Heartsteel proc and the lane begins to tip.","late":"Past the first item, Renekton has no meaningful power growth while your HP passive continues to compound every minion and champion kill into permanent max HP. Your Soul Furnace shield now absorbs most of his all-in combo, and the E (Roar of the Slayer) armor shred makes each of your fully charged Q knockups punishing. Two items in, a charged Q into E shred into R engage is a nearly unkillable sequence — and even if Renekton somehow kills you, your zombie passive will chase him down and deal chip damage. Force the 1v1 on him in sidelane after you complete two items; he has no percent-HP damage or percent-true damage to punish your HP stack, so this matchup becomes entirely yours.","whys":["Empowered Ruthless Predator at 50 Fury is a 1.5-second stun that also destroys your shield — charge Q behind the wave only, never in his face, or the telegraph is free for him.","He builds Fury with Cull the Meek and dashes in with Slice and Dice, so empowered W arrives fast — shield every approach and fight only under 50 Fury.","Cull the Meek is a 7-second AoE swing that heals him through your charged-Q trade — bring Grievous Wounds, absorb with Soul Furnace, and counter-engage on his Fury downtime.","No new tools but his empowered-W stun is still brutal — stay at the tip of your Q range and punish him only when his fury bar is empty and Ruthless Predator is on cooldown.","His Dominus (R) gives him bonus HP and AOE aura — if he Rs into you while your HP is low, retreat; your Unstoppable Onslaught (R) is better used as a roam threat or gap-closer than a direct answer to his all-in.","Your Heartsteel passive has been generating permanent HP stacks all lane — the first item power spike starts to even the brawls, though Renekton's burst still bites if he catches you with fury, so respect the empowered-W until you have clear HP cushion.","Two items of HP stacking and your Soul Furnace shield absorbing his combo means Renekton can no longer trade evenly — your zombie passive punishes even a trade he wins, and a fully charged Q knockup into E armor shred ends the 1v1 decisively."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"riven","win":["Skill","Riven","Riven","Riven","Riven","Riven","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Broken Wings into Ki Burst (11s at rank 1) comes online and bursts you before Soul Furnace matters — respect the window or die for free."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Blade of the Exile (120s at rank 1) gives her bonus attack damage, extra range and one Wind Slash — never charge Unstoppable Onslaught into a Riven holding it."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Black Cleaver on Riven shreds your armor and compounds every trade; your Heartsteel only catches up if you've kept the HP deficit manageable through lane."},{"when":"Late","text":"Your W-shield value scales with max HP and your zombie passive guarantees you deal damage even dying — this is the only window where your bulk can dictate the 1v1."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup on Riven after baiting her E (Valor) dash so she has no escape from the stun","Keep the wave frozen near your tower so Riven must walk into your brush Q-charge range rather than harassing freely","Use E armor-shred immediately after the Q knockup connects to compound the trade window before she can re-engage"],"foe":["Steer Broken Wings around your charge arc to close inside your swing where you cannot re-aim it again","Land Ki Burst (11s at rank 1) mid-combo to stun you out of the Soul Furnace detonation window entirely","Stack Black Cleaver armor reduction through repeated short trades to dismantle the HP bulk that defines your win condition"]},"early":"Level 1 is a coin flip — she can poke and step out before your W-shield stacks, but a full-charge Q from brush punishes her if she stands still. The problem is she rarely stands still: Broken Wings at level 2 chains three strikes she can steer around your telegraph and burst you inside your charge time. Respect the level 2 spike and do not commit a charged Q into a Riven with Broken Wings unspent — she moves through it and stuns you with Ki Burst before your shield detonates. Farm safely, hold your W for incoming burst rather than using it aggressively, and only trade once Ki Burst is on cooldown, which is 11 seconds at rank 1.","mid":"Levels 4 through 6 she owns the lane rhythm entirely. Broken Wings chains three strikes on a 13-second cooldown, and she follows with Ki Burst's stun and Valor's dash-block faster than your charged Q resolves — and at level 6 Blade of the Exile adds attack damage, range and a single Wind Slash that punishes your Unstoppable Onslaught commit hard. Do not R into a Riven who has her ultimate up. The correct mid-game pattern is wave management: keep your HP topped, use Unstoppable Onslaught only as a positional escape or to reach a clearly winning angle, and farm toward your first item without gifting her a gold lead she can snowball.","late":"Your late-game is the only realistic equalizer. At 2+ items your W-shield absorbs a meaningful chunk of her combo damage, your HP pool means she cannot one-shot you even with Black Cleaver stacks, and your zombie passive (Glory in Death) guarantees you deal damage and can land a stun even as you are dying — making every kill on you costly. Force engages on your terms: charge Q from a bush or around a wall where she cannot dash sideways to dodge it, then E-shred and brawl. She still out-trades you in a clean face-to-face exchange, so the goal is a controlled engage where you absorb her burst in one W-shield detonation and answer with the full stun-into-brawl pattern.","whys":["Neither kit has a clean edge at level 1: your charged Q is slow to wind up and she can auto-poke back, but she has no full rotation yet either — play neutral and do not overcommit.","At level 2 Broken Wings gives her three chained strikes she can steer around your telegraph, closing inside your charge and bursting through a half-stacked Soul Furnace.","Broken Wings into Ki Burst into Valor is online and resolves faster than your Q charge — she wins the extended trade and steps out before your shield detonates.","She stacks Black Cleaver through repeated short trades and maintains mobility you cannot match; your E armor-shred helps but you cannot land the stun reliably into three dashes.","Blade of the Exile (120s at rank 1) adds attack damage and range plus one Wind Slash — charging Unstoppable Onslaught into a Riven holding it is death.","Black Cleaver is likely complete; your Heartsteel HP is accumulating but her armor-shred compounds faster than your bulk compounds, keeping her ahead in direct trades.","Your W-shield and zombie passive create genuine friction even for an ahead Riven — if you can land a charged Q knockup from an unexpected angle you can force a costly trade, but she still out-duels you cleanly without that stun landing."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"rumble","win":["Sion","Rumble","Skill","Sion","Rumble","Skill","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"A full-charge Q at level 1 can catch Rumble before he unlocks Flamespitter — land it and he burns his W on the way out."},{"when":"Lvls 4-5","text":"Rumble's Flamespitter-into-overheat window is his strongest dueling phase — he shreds your HP passively; give him this window and step back."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's bulk starts compounding your HP passive stacks — your W-shield value and zombie threat both spike and Rumble can no longer outsustain your trades."},{"when":"Late","text":"At full HP stacking your W-shield absorbs Flamespitter ticks that would have chunked you early, and a charged-Q knockup into E armor-shred wins the isolated 1v1 cleanly."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a full-charge Q knockup before Rumble can press Flamespitter and walk into you","Use W-shield to eat one Flamespitter rotation and then trade back with E armor-shred","Build MR early (Null-Magic Mantle or MR shard) so his magic DoT does not snowball the HP deficit"],"foe":["Activate Flamespitter early and walk into you so the sustained %HP magic damage compounds before you can W-shield","Overheat mid-trade to proc bonus-damage autos while you are slowed by his E harpoon","Drop The Equalizer across your retreat path to zone you off the wave — you have no dash out"]},"early":"Level 1 is yours if you can set up the charge — Flamespitter only reaches 600 units, so he has to walk into the Decimating Smash arc to use it, and a max-charge knockup as he steps up sends him back to tower with no way to punish you. Respect the walk-up angle though: if he gets his nose under yours before you finish charging, the cone burns through your body and he wins the damage race at that range. Take W at level 2 so his first real Flamespitter hits Soul Furnace rather than your health bar, then detonate the shield into him after absorbing the bulk of the burn — you trade magic for physical and come out ahead.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are the danger window. Rumble reaches his full basic kit with Flamespitter, Scrap Shield, and E harpoon in a kit that out-DPSes you in a stand-still brawl: he E-slows you, walks into your face, and cycles Flamespitter twice before you can charge Q through the slow. Give him this corridor — farm near tower, hold W to eat the Flamespitter burst, and save your Q charge for the moment his overheat falls off so you can stun him while he has no cooldowns. Level 6 is the correction: Unstoppable Onslaught threatens a charge-in that he cannot dodge without burning Flash, so he is forced to respect your all-in for the first time.","late":"At Heartsteel you out-stat him cleanly. Soul Furnace now absorbs an entire Flamespitter rotation with HP to spare, your HP passive has been compounding since the laning kills, and his magic DoT — which was scary at 3,000 HP — barely dents 6,000. Land Roar of the Slayer first to strip his armour ahead of the Decimating Smash knockup, then chase; your passive zombie stays a genuine kill threat even if he gets you low, which keeps him honest about walking forward. The Equalizer is still the biggest swing: it is a slowing line laid across your escape on an 80-second cooldown at rank 3, and you have no dash — hold Flash for crossing it, not for the damage.","whys":["You are slow to charge Q at level 1, but Flamespitter is a 600-range cone — he has to walk into the arc, and a full knockup as he steps up wins the trade and burns his Scrap Shield.","With Flamespitter and a harpoon both up he out-DPSes you in a sustained trade; Soul Furnace is online, but his cone keeps burning through the three seconds before you can detonate it.","Both kits are fully operational but the lane is contested: your W-shield neutralises one Flamespitter cycle, but his E-slow and overheat damage cancel your charge-Q threat at close range.","Rumble's E-slow into Flamespitter sustain wins duels in the 4-5 lull — your Q takes too long to charge through the slow and his overheat autos out-damage your W-shield.","Unstoppable Onslaught at 6 gives you a close-gap tool he cannot dodge without Flash; the all-in threat forces him to play back and stops the free Flamespitter poke cycle.","Heartsteel's compounding stacks and your kill-HP passive mean Flamespitter ticks become negligible — you out-bulk his DoT and your W-shield absorbs enough to fight short trades freely.","Full HP-stacking wins the isolated 1v1: E shreds his armor, charged Q stuns him before Flamespitter cycles twice, and your zombie passive threatens follow-through damage even if he somehow trades even."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"ryze","win":["Sion","Sion","Ryze","Sion","Skill","Ryze","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Your W-shield absorbs Ryze's Q-E poke chain — he burns mana for free while you charge Q behind the wave."},{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Ryze's Q-E-W root combo comes fully online and can lock you in place for his entire damage rotation before you land a stun."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP passive starts compounding — every shrug of his poke makes you harder to kill, and one landed charged Q swing is now a kill threat."},{"when":"Late","text":"Full-build Ryze deletes you from range before your charged Q ever reaches him — play the map, not the 1v1."}],"wants":{"you":["Hide the Q charge in fog or behind the wave so Ryze cannot sidestep the telegraph","Sidestep Spell Flux so Rune Prison can only slow you, then run the full Q-E knockup-shred inside its 11s cooldown","Translate your W-shield sustain and HP stacking into Heartsteel as fast as possible to turn his poke into a losing trade"],"foe":["Tag you with Spell Flux so Rune Prison roots instead of slowing, then chain Overload twice","Spend his high mana pool poking your charge-up pattern from range before you can ever get in melee","Stall the lane long enough for his items to outscale your HP bulk and Realm Warp to avoid your R engage"]},"early":"At level 1 Overload reaches 1000 units and you have no answer at that range — farm on the back of the wave and do not walk into his face. The moment your W comes online at level 2, his poke math changes: you pop Soul Furnace before his Q lands and he is spending mana to damage a shield. At level 3 he is dangerous, but only through Spell Flux: that orb is 615 range, and without it on you Rune Prison slows instead of rooting. Hold the charge telegraph behind the wave and commit when the Flux whiffs or the W goes down.","mid":"From 4 onward your job is to force him to either spend Spell Flux and Rune Prison on you — and eat the charged Q once they expire — or back off the wave and give up farm. At level 6 Realm Warp is a wildcard: it lets him answer Unstoppable Onslaught by warping allies top, turning your engage into a 1v2, so talk to your jungler before you burn R. Inside the 11 seconds his W is down you step up, charge Q from fog or a minion angle, E-slow and chain the stun: that pattern wins the trade even through his poke lead.","late":"By first item your HP pool is large enough that his Q poke is a rounding error — land one charged Q per wave and you own the island. However, once he hits two items his burst output can delete you before the zombie phase contributes anything meaningful, and Realm Warp lets him dodge your R approach by teleporting away or warping his team to a better fight. Split the map: use Unstoppable Onslaught as a transit tool to force him to deal with you in a sidelane where Realm Warp has fewer targets to exploit.","whys":["Ryze's Q-E poke is online at level 1 and your only tool is W — absorb the first two hits and do not walk into root range.","W-shield hits level 2 and his poke suddenly costs him more mana than it costs you health — short trades are yours once you can pop the shield on his Q.","Full kit Ryze lands Rune Prison and chains Q-E-W-Q into you before your knockup can land — this is his cleanest kill window all lane.","Once his root is on cooldown you can freely charge Q behind the wave and run the full E-Q knockup-shred combo — he cannot interrupt a committed pattern with no CC up.","Unstoppable Onslaught threatens the kill but Realm Warp lets him warp allies to the fight or escape the charge — neither of you has a clean decisive advantage here.","Ryze's first completed AP item (Seraph's into Shadowflame) spikes his burst past your W-shield capacity and his root-into-rotation can now kill you before Heartsteel HP saves you.","At two-plus items your HP stack is the highest in the game and your zombie Passive guarantees trade value even in losing fights — Ryze can win the 1v1 at range but he cannot stop you from winning the island."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"sejuani","win":["Skill","Skill","Sejuani","Skill","Skill","Sejuani","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2","text":"Your W-shield comes online and short trades start costing Sejuani more — bait her Permafrost stun (E) before committing a charged Q so she can't answer in kind."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Sejuani's Arctic Assault (Q) and Glacial Prison (R) give her a full engage combo — respect both and don't give her a clean all-in with your HP low."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP compound begins in earnest here, but her first tank item on top of Frost Armor's free armour and MR blunts your swing too — the window is contested, not clean."},{"when":"Late","text":"In a long teamfight your zombie passive guarantees one last charged-Q knockup even through death, which can peel or zone Sejuani's Glacial Prison R off your backline."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Permafrost (E stun after Frost stacks) before charging Q — commit the full knockup only once her stun is burned","Charge Q from brush or behind the wave so the slow wind-up is hidden, then E-shred her armor into the knockup","Keep the wave on your side so Sejuani must walk into your range rather than you walking into her Winter's Wrath W tick"],"foe":["Stack Frost on you with autos and W ticks before landing Permafrost E to lock you in place during your Q channel","Use Arctic Assault Q to gap-close and trigger Permafrost early so your charged Q knockup connects with nothing","Win the all-in at level 6 with Glacial Prison R before your HP passive and Heartsteel can compound into an unkillable HP pool"]},"early":"Level 1 is a genuine coin flip — Sejuani's passive Frost stacks build on autos and her W Winter's Wrath tick, so she can set up a Permafrost stun without spending a single extra ability. Don't auto-trade freely; last-hit at Q range and let her show the Permafrost pattern before you commit. At level 2 your W-shield absorbs her Winter's Wrath damage and makes short trades cost her more, but she hits level 3 with the same Frost stacking threat. At level 3 she has her full kit too — Arctic Assault Q to close, W to stack Frost, E to stun — so her engage pattern is complete before yours. Respect that: charge Q from brush angles or behind a pushing wave where the telegraph is hidden, not in the open where she closes with Q and Permafrost fires before your knockup lands.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are a management window for both sides — neither of you has a decisive power spike yet, so play for wave state. Freeze near your tower to bait her forward into charged-Q range; if she doesn't walk up, shove for plates and reset. At level 6 Sejuani's Glacial Prison R gives her a hard all-in threat: she Arctic Assaults in, W-stacks you, Permafrost stuns, then R catches you in the AoE knockup. Don't be below two-thirds HP at 6, because your W-shield won't absorb a full combo and your Unstoppable Onslaught R is an escape as much as an engage here. Use R to reposition or bail if she's ahead — don't blow it on a straight-line charge into her full kit.","late":"Late game this matchup becomes about team value, not the 1v1. Your zombie passive makes every dive-in still punish — even dying into Glacial Prison, your reanimated form can charge a knockup through her team while she's committing. Heartsteel's HP compound makes you an enormous frontline sponge by two items, but Frost Armor hands her armour, magic resist and immunity to slows every time she is out of combat, so neither your E nor your Q slows her opener — you brawl as equals. The real win condition is landing Unstoppable Onslaught into their backline inside the 90 seconds Glacial Prison is down, so she cannot answer; ulting at the same time just trades AoE CC and settles nothing.","whys":["Level 1 is decided by who can auto-trade without getting Frost-stacked into Permafrost — neither side has a clean opener, so last-hit safely and map her stun pattern before committing.","Soul Furnace absorbs Winter's Wrath and makes short trades favourable, but Permafrost is already live — and Frost Armor makes her slow-immune, so your E only buys the armour shred.","Her full kit is online and her engage completes before yours: Arctic Assault closes, Winter's Wrath stacks Frost, Permafrost stuns. It costs an 18-second Q, so a whiffed charge is your window.","Both kits are online but neither has a meaningful item spike — the lane is a pattern-management window, trade around Permafrost's cooldown and freeze for the CS lead.","Sejuani's Glacial Prison R turns any all-in lethal: she Arctic Assaults, W-stacks, Permafrost stuns, then R catches you — don't be low HP at 6 and use Unstoppable Onslaught to bail if needed.","Her first tank item stacked on Frost Armor's free armour and magic resist blunts your Decimating Smash, while Winter's Wrath rebuilds Frost fast enough to re-stun — she edges the contested first-item window.","Two items each, both sides are enormous frontlines — your zombie passive guarantees one last charged-Q knockup even through death, keeping the brawl genuinely even in prolonged teamfights."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"sett","win":["Sett","Sett","Sett","Sett","Sett","Sett","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-2","text":"Sett's passive double-punch and Haymaker grit come online immediately — he wins every short trade you take before your W-shield is ready to absorb the true-damage center burst."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Both ultimates arrive at 6: Sett's R can dump you into his team; use your own R to disengage or engage on HIS terms, not his."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel or Sunfire spike: your HP passive starts compounding and W-shield value climbs — sustained brawls now favour you as long as you shield his Haymaker center."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your stacked HP total and zombie passive make you nearly unkillable in drawn-out fights; Sett's true-damage core still punishes if he lands a clean Haymaker, so keep shielding it."}],"wants":{"you":["Soul Furnace the Haymaker centre — it is true damage, so armour buys you nothing; shield it, then answer with a charged Q stun","Charge Q from beyond 490 units so Facebreaker cannot reach, then release the knockup as he steps in","Out-scale him into the first-item window where your stacked HP and W-shield value overtake his grit trades"],"foe":["Stack grit by taking short auto trades early and unload Haymaker's true-damage center into you","Catch you with minions on his far side so Facebreaker stuns you instead of only slowing","Force repeated short skirmishes pre-items so his passive double-punch chips you below W-shield threshold"]},"early":"Level 1, Sett has you beat. His passive double-punch accelerates autos and his Haymaker grit charges off your body — take short trades and you are feeding the exact damage he needs to crack you open. Hold your W and do not pop it early: the key trade is absorbing the outer Haymaker with W-shield active and blocking the true-damage center, because if that center lands on your HP bar directly you lose the exchange badly. At level 2 you get W online and the dynamic shifts slightly, but Sett still wants to brawl, so keep the wave near your tower and deny him free grit stacks on your face.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are the closest thing to neutral ground — both of you have your full basic kit and neither ult changes the equation yet. Look for your charged Q setup here: Facebreaker reaches 490 units on a 16-second cooldown at rank 1, so start the charge outside it, E-slow him if he steps up, and land the knockup into a Soul Furnace detonation. At level 6 The Show Stopper can carry you into his jungler's follow-up, so track the river and never fight at half HP near a collapsed rotation. Your own R is best used as a gap-close onto him when he is retreating, not as a blind peel tool.","late":"Once Heartsteel or your first tank mythic is complete, this lane is yours to hold. Your HP passive has been compounding off every wave kill and the W-shield now absorbs a meaningful chunk of Haymaker's outer ring, letting you take grit trades and come out ahead in sustained brawls. Keep shielding every Haymaker — even late, a clean center true-damage hit on a low-HP bar can reverse a fight. Your zombie passive on death also punishes any Sett who blows his R and Haymaker to kill you: he is left with his cooldowns spent and you are still dealing damage for three more seconds. Play the extended fights, use your R to maintain aggression, and let the HP gap close the matchup in your favour.","whys":["Sett's passive double-punch charges grit off your HP immediately and his Haymaker true-damage center punishes any early trade before your W-shield is ready — stay back and let him push.","W comes online but Sett still wins short auto trades by banking Grit; hold Soul Furnace for the Haymaker centre, an 18-second cooldown, not for poke damage.","The first genuine Skill window: Facebreaker only stuns enemies on opposite sides of him, so one-on-one it just slows — charge Q from outside its 490 range and the knockup lands.","Neither ult is a decisive factor yet; keep charged Q angles on him when his E grab is down and deny him grit stacks by avoiding auto trades in the open.","Both ults active — your R closes ground and his R threatens a lethal slam; fight near your side of the lane and track his jungler before committing to a charged Q all-in.","Heartsteel or tank mythic online: HP passive compounding plus scaled W-shield value overtakes his grit burst in sustained brawls — force extended fights and stop short trades.","Enormous HP total and zombie passive make you the stronger 1v1 threat in drawn-out late-game fights; keep shielding Haymaker's center and your passive guarantees trading value even on a kill."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"shen","win":["Skill","Shen","Shen","Skill","Skill","Shen","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Spirit's Refuge and Shadow Dash arrive together — the blade blocks attacks, not abilities, so your autos die inside it while Q and E still land. His dash is 18s at rank 1."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Stand United gives Shen a global save that turns any kill you force into a 2v1 — never commit without checking the minimap for his R channel."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel compounds your HP and your shield value rises, but Twilight Assault scales off your max health — raw health partly feeds his Q, so pair it with resistances."},{"when":"Late","text":"At full build you become an unstoppable frontline zombie — Passive guarantees fight contribution even when dying, and Shen loses his early lane edge in a full teamfight brawl."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup to break Shen's E-taunt timing and open a real brawl","Trade with Q and E inside Spirit's Refuge — it stops attacks, never your abilities","Use R to engage onto a sidelane target or flank where Shen's Stand United cannot arrive in time"],"foe":["E-taunt you onto his Q hit while his W-spirit blade is positioned to block your retaliating autos","Save Stand United for the exact moment you commit to a kill so a teammate arrives immune to follow-up","Out-trade short exchanges by blocking your auto-chain with W and resetting spacing before your Q fully charges"]},"early":"At level 1 this is genuinely even — neither kit punches through the other without setup. The moment Shen hits level 2 and pairs E-taunt with W-spirit-blade, the lane tilts to him: he can dash onto you, block the retaliation autos the spirit blade soaks, and Q you during the CC window. Do not try to out-trade that combo short. Play around the charge telegraph — start your Q wind-up to bait the E-taunt early, then cancel and walk out; if he dashes onto a partial charge, immediately detonate W-shield to eat as much of his auto DPS as you can before spacing away. At level 3 your E is live but his W will block whatever auto follow-up you add, so trade only when his spirit blade is on cooldown or poorly positioned.","mid":"From 4 through 5 you have your full basic kit and he has his, but his kit is more self-contained — W-block, E-taunt, Q empower, and the perpetual threat of Stand United mean he can trade, disengage, and save simultaneously. Farm safely; shove with your E armor-shred only when you have a full Q ready as the follow-up threat, otherwise he taunts you during the slow charge. At 6 your R opens roam options, but so does his — and his R costs him zero lane pressure since it channels him teleporting to a fight. Track Stand United: if the channel appears, immediately disengage the 1v1 because whoever he saves will 2v1 you. Your R is better used to join a fight where Shen is already committed in lane, denying him the map-impact moment his kit is designed around.","late":"Once both players have Heartsteel and a second item, the true EVEN resolution kicks in: your W-shield value scales with your enormous HP pool, your E armor-shred reduces Shen's tankiness in the brawl, and your Passive zombie guarantees fight contribution whether or not you die first. Shen is not a damage threat at full build — he is a CC platform for his team. In a pure side-by-side brawl your bulk and zombie passive outlast his taunt-trade pattern. The danger here is not the Shen lane — it is him abandoning the lane and winning fights globally with Stand United while you are split from your team. Force your ult engage when his R is on cooldown, so he cannot bail a teammate during your all-in window.","whys":["Level 1 neither kit has full trade combo — Q charges are slow and Shen has no W yet, so both players are respecting poke and last-hitting.","Shen's W-spirit-blade comes online at level 2 and blocks your retaliation autos during his taunt, giving him complete control of every short trade.","At level 3 he has E-taunt plus W plus Q empower — the full combo — and his spirit blade still negates your auto follow-up, so he dictates every engagement.","Levels 4-5 are a sustained Shen advantage: no power spike exists for Sion here, and Shen's E-taunt into W-block pattern punishes any poorly-timed Q wind-up he can bait.","At level 6 Stand United turns every all-in into a potential 2v1 — your Unstoppable Onslaught engage is strong but Shen's global answer immediately neutralizes any kill attempt the moment he R-channels.","Heartsteel compounds your HP pool, but Twilight Assault damages off your max health and Ki Barrier re-shields him every time he touches a champion — he still edges the sustained brawl he prefers here.","At 2+ items your zombie Passive guarantees fight contribution even if Shen out-trades you, and your raw bulk in a 5v5 brawl matches his taunt-peel utility — neither side closes the game alone from here."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"singed","win":["Skill","Singed","Singed","Singed","Singed","Singed","Singed"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Your charged Q is the only real threat — if you land a full-charge stun on his approach, it is the clearest punishment window of the whole lane."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught can finally catch a running Singed, but he will Fling you into your own team or into his poison — commit only if he is below half health with no path back."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Singed's Rylai/Chemtank spike lets him run at permanent chase speed while the slowed poison stacks — his Q trail becomes unavoidable and he wins every extended trade from here."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items Singed's poison out-damages your W-shield regeneration and his Ghost/Phase Rush means your R cannot reliably close the gap — he controls every fight's length."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a full-charge Q stun when Singed commits with Fling, then follow with E armor-shred and W-shield to punish the locked-down window","Force short trades at the edge of his poison trail — detonate W-shield after he flips you, not while running through his Q","Use R to reposition across the map rather than trying to run Singed down, since chasing his poison trail is a losing game"],"foe":["Activate Ghost or Phase Rush and drag the trade through his Q poison trail so every second you spend near him costs HP","Lay Mega Adhesive down first, then Fling you into it so the goo roots you where the poison is thickest","Proxy farm behind your tower to deny Sion CS and XP while Singed's HP regen makes him nearly impossible to punish for it"]},"early":"At level 1 Singed will walk at you with Ghost already anticipating the charged Q telegraph — he is counting on your slow windup to buy time for his poison trail to stack. You can threaten a full-charge Q stun, but the moment he sees the animation he walks out of the tip range; respect that he will not simply stand still for it. Farm with short Q taps and keep your W ready to absorb his Fling damage. Neither of you has a meaningful kill threat here — it is genuinely even on damage, but Singed wins by just not dying and out-sustaining your short-trade pressure with natural HP regen.","mid":"From level 2 onward Singed's poison trail goes live and the lane tips decisively his way. Mega Adhesive by itself only slows and grounds; the root arrives when Fling drops you into the goo, so the sequence to watch is adhesive down, then Fling on a 10-second cooldown at rank 1. Your W-shield covers the Fling burst but does not cancel poison duration — you eat damage every step you take back through his trail. At level 6 your R can catch a proxying Singed, but he Flings you the moment you connect while his regen restores. Play conservatively, track his proxy timings, and never chase more than two steps past the wave.","late":"Late game Singed has Rylai's and Chemtank online and his Q trail slows everything in it — your R charge is one of the few tools that ignores his run-down setup, but even a successful R engagement gets Flung into a bad angle. Your zombie passive (Glory in Death) does give Singed real incentive not to walk into melee with you freely, so use that threat to zone him off objectives. In teamfights you are the engage tool and he is the chaos agent — do not try to duel him, because his poison out-ticks your W-shield regeneration over any extended fight. Land your R onto their carries and let your team clean up while Singed runs a separate flank.","whys":["Neither of you has a kill button at level 1 — the charged Q telegraph lets Singed sidestep the tip range while his poison is not yet meaningful enough to punish through it.","Singed's Q poison goes live and he immediately starts the run-down pattern — your W-shield absorbs his Fling burst but you still eat the full poison tick retreating toward tower.","Mega Adhesive alone only slows and grounds — the root comes from Fling dropping you into it, so his order is goo down, Fling on top, poison ticking under you.","Phase Rush or Ghost keeps Singed moving faster than your charge-up allows — you cannot threaten a stun from outside poison range and every step you take toward him costs HP.","Your R Unstoppable Onslaught reaches a proxying Singed, but he Flings you the instant you arrive and his Rylai slow means you cannot disengage cleanly before poison stacks.","Singed's Rylai + Chemtank spike makes his Q trail a permanent slow zone — your W-shield value does not outpace the sustained poison tick in any fight he chooses to extend.","His poison out-ticks your shield regeneration at full build and his run speed makes him unkillable without landing a perfectly timed R-into-team setup — use your zombie passive to zone, not to duel."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"swain","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Skill","Sion","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your W-shield comes online and your charged Q stun completes the combo — Swain has no dash to escape the knockup, so this is your first real window to punish."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught closes on a dashless Swain; blow past his W positioning, then land the charged Q — the knockup drops him out of Demonic Ascension's drain entirely."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel starts compounding your HP passive — your W-shield absorbs Swain's Q bolts for free, and the sheer bulk makes his flat-damage drain look trivial."},{"when":"His R (Ascension)","text":"Demonic Ascension only persists while he keeps draining a champion, so a root into drain swings the trade hard — leave his 650 range or land the knockup and the form ends on its own."}],"wants":{"you":["Land charged Q knockup through his E root attempt — sidestep Nevermove, then punish with the full Q-E combo while he has no CC remaining","W-shield his Q bolt poke and detonate it for chip damage in trades, preventing him from winning short exchanges with flat AP","Buy Grievous Wounds before the extended fights — Ravenous Flock heals him through chip, and raw MR does nothing about health he simply regains"],"foe":["Root you with E Nevermove into a full W bomb slow so Demonic Ascension can drain you while you are locked down","Keep you at arm's length with Q poke and W Vision of Empire to chip your HP before an all-in so Ascension finishes the job","Activate Demonic Ascension when his soul stack is high enough that the sustained AOE drain outheals your damage and turns a full trade into his favor"]},"early":"At levels 1-2, Swain pokes with his Q Death's Hand bolts from short range — step sideways in the lane so only one or two bolts clip you rather than the full spread. His W Vision of Empire is a long-range bomb with a slow that telegraphs heavily; the moment you see the glow drop onto you, side-step it entirely. Your W-shield is your answer to his Q: pop it before walking into poke range, absorb his bolt cluster, then detonate it for a free chip of magic damage back. He has no dash, so once you reach level 3 and have Q charged, he cannot reliably escape — land E armor shred first to slow him, then release the fully charged knockup. His E Nevermove is a short-range boomerang line that roots on the pull-back; hug the wall side of the lane so the return arc misses you, and never walk directly toward him in a straight line.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are the genuine skill window. Swain's W has decent range and if he hits it, the slow lets him run down and land his E root for a Demonic Ascension trade — do not let him open with W into E. Walk unpredictably and keep minions between you and his E boomerang. At level 6 the matchup tips back hard: your Unstoppable Onslaught is an instant gap-closer that bypasses his W bomb positioning, and the follow-up charged Q knockup lands before his Ascension stacks enough to matter. If he activates Demonic Ascension during a trade, immediately use E to slow and keep distance or throw a charged Q to break his position — the drain requires him to stay in range and the knockup interrupts his cadence. He has no escape, so once you are on him at 6 the only way he survives is if Ascension is already stacked and you are already low.","late":"Late game Swain becomes a teamfight drain-tank, but his single-target power in a 1v1 with you is mediocre. Your HP wall from Heartsteel and your HP passive is too thick for flat AP drain to dent quickly, and your zombie passive guarantees he takes damage even if he somehow bursts you out. In side-lane 1v1s: bait his E Nevermove by walking at him on an angle so the boomerang misses the pull-back, then charge your Q and release it into the knockup — from there his R drain cannot save him since he is airborne and losing the trade. Respect his teamfight presence if he has high soul stacks and full Ascension stacks from earlier drains, but in a solo setting he is simply not a threat to a fully built Sion.","whys":["Swain's level-1 Q bolts spread wide — step sideways so only one clips you and he can't sustain a trade with chip poke alone.","Your W-shield absorbs his Q burst and detonates for magic damage in return — Swain has no escape from your knockup at level 2 once you commit.","Charged Q knockup plus E armor shred at level 3 is a full combo Swain cannot dodge without a dash; the stun lets you convert a full kill threat.","Swain's W bomb slow into E root can set up a dangerous Ascension window — walk unpredictably at levels 4-5 and never straight-line toward him.","Unstoppable Onslaught closes past his W positioning, and a knockup ends Demonic Ascension — the form lasts only while he drains a champion.","Heartsteel compounds faster than Ravenous Flock — but its fragments heal him and raise his max health for good, so Grievous Wounds decides this window.","At 2+ items your zombie passive deals damage during the few seconds you reanimate — even if Swain catches you low, he pays a tax for the kill."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"sylas","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Skill","Skill","Sion","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your W-shield goes online and blocks Sylas's Q/W magic damage, letting you win short trades that his chain-heal cannot sustain."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hijack takes your Unstoppable Onslaught and he can charge your own ult at your team — ping your jungler, hold your HP up, and note Hijack falls to 30 seconds by rank 3."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel and an MR shard blunt his chain, but Kingslayer still heals him off you — pair the health with Grievous Wounds or he just extends the trade until it is his."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items the lane is genuinely split — his stolen R is weaker because he lacks your max-HP passive bulk, but his heal and chain-dash let him disengage any fight he is losing."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a charged Q stun when his E (Abscond/Abduct) is on cooldown so he cannot dash away","Pop your W-shield before his Q/W chain hits to negate the magic burst and deny his heal","Get Grievous Wounds up before extended trades — Kingslayer is where all his sustain lives"],"foe":["Dash in with E to stun you, then chain Q into W-drain while your W is down","Steal your R at 6 and immediately use it to engage your team or open a lethal flank","Extend every trade past your W-shield cooldown where his sustained Q/W healing outpaces your regen"]},"early":"At level 1 Sylas has only Chain Lash — a slow with a delayed detonation at the intersection, no heal and no dash. Let him throw it, sidestep, and you can start a charged Q with nothing he can do about it. At level 2 he picks up the chain-dash and that is the true matchup start: he closes, then chains Q into Kingslayer for a burst-and-heal cycle while your W is down. Pre-activate Soul Furnace the moment he primes the dash, so the shield eats the magic burst and his heal comes off a protected health bar. Note where the sustain actually lives — Kingslayer is the only heal in his kit, 12 seconds at rank 1, so Grievous Wounds bought early turns every long trade around. At level 3 you out-trade him cleanly: a charged Q into E armour-shred settles it before his cooldowns reset.","mid":"At levels 4-5 the lane cools into a patience game: Sylas pokes with Chain Lash from outside stun range and disengages through E whenever you look threatening, so never charge Q in the open where he simply dashes away. Abscond is 13 seconds at rank 1 — charge from brush or behind the wave and commit only once you have seen it spent. At level 6 the dynamic shifts sharply: Hijack copies your Unstoppable Onslaught, and his version lacks your max-HP bulk behind it, so it hits softer than when you cast it, but the surprise angle is real. Warn your team the moment he hits 6, keep your HP above the line where a stolen R plus his own chain cannot kill you in one rotation, and bait the E out so he is dashless before you start a charged Q fight.","late":"In the 2+ items phase this is a genuine 50-50 brawl: your HP wall and shield value scale with Heartsteel while his chain scales with AP, so the fight turns on whether he lands the full rotation before your knockup. Build Gargoyle Stoneplate or Force of Nature second to blunt the magic, and carry Grievous Wounds — Kingslayer heals off champions, and the long trade is exactly what he is playing for. Fight on your terms: force the E dash early with a short trade, then reload the real charged Q engage once he is dashless. Your zombie passive guarantees you damage and slow him even when he kills you, punishing any 1v1 where he burns his chain for the kill and dies to your reanimated form.","whys":["At level 1 Chain Lash is his whole kit — a slow and a delayed detonation, no heal and no dash yet. Sidestep it and you can charge safely.","Your W-shield goes online and blocks his Q/W magic chain; short trades now favor you because his heal comes off a shielded health bar, not real HP.","A landed charged Q stun into E armor-shred wins the full trade before his chain resets — this is your cleanest kill window all game.","He kites with E whenever you look threatening at levels 4-5; charge from brush or behind the wave and only commit when E is on cooldown.","Sylas hits 6 and steals your Unstoppable Onslaught — warn your team and keep HP above 60% so his stolen R engage plus chain cannot one-rotation you.","Heartsteel and an MR shard turn his Q/W drain into near-zero damage per trade; your HP passive compounds and he cannot burst you through the W-shield window.","At 2+ items he can disengage any losing fight with E and heal back with his chain, so bait the dash first, then reload the charged Q for the kill commitment."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"tahmkench","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Tahm Kench","Skill","Sion","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Your full kit is online — charged Q knockup into E armor-shred lets you force a real trade that his grey-health passive cannot fully absorb before he has to back."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught is a free engage threat; if he spends Abyssal Dive dodging it, that is 21 seconds at rank 1 with no escape, and you follow with the full Q-E combo."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP proc compounds your passive kill stacks — your W-shield value scales with max HP, making sustained trades decisively yours against his drain-tank kit."},{"when":"Lvl 4-5","text":"Thick Skin absorbs your short trades and Tongue Lash slows every 7 seconds — but the stun only fires on his third An Acquired Taste stack, so deny the third and it stays a slow."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup and immediately follow with E armor-shred before his grey-health passive converts back to real HP","Buy Grievous Wounds before the extended trades — Tongue Lash and Thick Skin are both healing, and time is what he needs","Track his W (Abyssal Dive) cooldown and commit your R or all-in only when his escape-and-shield tool is down"],"foe":["Stack grey-health by walking into your auto range repeatedly then backing off before you can charge a full Q","Build three An Acquired Taste stacks on you so the next Tongue Lash stuns instead of only slowing, then brawl","Use W (Abyssal Dive) to dodge your Unstoppable Onslaught or a fully charged Q, wasting your biggest engagement tools"]},"early":"Level 1 is quiet — his Q tongue lash has about 900 range and a reliable slow into stun, so do not try to stand still and charge a Q in the open unless you are behind the wave. He cannot kill you at level 1, but he can interrupt a visible charge by simply walking into tongue range and pressing Q. Farm at Q-tip range and let him push. At level 2 your W-shield comes up: detonate it during his auto-trade to win the HP exchange, since his grey-health passive needs time to convert back and you are already dealing magic burst on top of your autos. Level 3 is your first real opening — the full Q-E combination shreds his armor and the knockup lands before he can use his W to dive underground, because Abyssal Dive has a 0.6-second animation you can interrupt mid-charge-up by committing when he steps forward. Hit that stun and detonate W immediately: the combined burst and shred outpaces his grey-health padding in a single trade window.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are where he gets comfortable. Thick Skin means every short trade leaves him a buffer that converts back once he is out of combat, and your charged Q asks you to stand still — which is exactly what Tongue Lash punishes. Do not charge Q with him in auto range; charge from fog angles or hold it and farm. Count his An Acquired Taste stacks while you do: at two, the next Tongue Lash stuns; at zero it is only a slow. At level 6 the dynamic shifts back — Unstoppable Onslaught covers the gap he cannot outrun and forces him to eat the knockup or burn Abyssal Dive. That dive is 21 seconds at rank 1, so if he spends it, the window is yours for a full Q-E; if he eats the R instead, chain straight into the charged Q. Either way your level 6 is an all-in he must spend his escape to answer.","late":"At first item and beyond this lane is decided by who builds more purposefully — your Heartsteel compounding and zombie passive make you very hard to kill in a straight brawl, while Thick Skin is sustain rather than a damage amplifier against you. He will not kill you in a side-lane duel once you have 4,000-plus HP; your shield alone absorbs most of what he throws, and your passive keeps trading after death. Buy Grievous Wounds anyway: Tongue Lash heals him on every champion it touches and Thick Skin converts stored damage straight back, so a long fight without anti-heal is one he can simply reset out of. He has no global — nothing he does happens without walking there — so punish every second he spends away from the side lane. The real late threat is Devour: he eats your carry mid-fight or shields an ally out of your engage, so be the one initiating the dive rather than reacting to his.","whys":["Tongue Lash hits the first unit in its 900 line and slows — the stun needs three An Acquired Taste stacks, so count them before charging in the open.","Your W-shield comes online and turns his auto-trade against him; detonate it during his walk-up and the magic burst outpaces his grey-health conversion rate.","Full kit at level 3 lets you combo Q knockup into E armor-shred before his grey-health can cover the burst — force this trade when he steps forward.","His grey-health passive absorbs short trades efficiently and his Q stun punishes obvious charge attempts — farm safely and charge Q only from fog angles.","Unstoppable Onslaught forces a binary choice: eat the knockup or spend Abyssal Dive; either outcome leaves him without his defensive CD for a full Q-E follow.","Heartsteel HP compounding makes your W-shield enormous and your zombie passive a genuine threat — sustained trades and brawls go decisively your way now.","Neither of you has a decisive damage advantage late; it splits on execution — your zombie passive and HP wall against Devour pulling a teammate out of the fight. Win the side lane."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"teemo","win":["Teemo","Teemo","Teemo","Skill","Sion","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Teemo blinds your autos with Q every 7 seconds — the entire early lane is him poking freely while you have no reliable gap-close."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught crosses the entire lane gap before Teemo can react — this is your first true kill window."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel + HP passive compound means your W-shield absorbs a larger and larger chunk of his poke, and your charged Q stun one-shots his escape plan."},{"when":"Late","text":"At 2+ items your zombie passive alone threatens Teemo's 800 HP — he cannot trade into you at any range where your charged Q can fire."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a full-charge Q knockup — it is spell damage, so Teemo's blind (Q) does not cancel it","Stack Heartsteel, but note Toxic Shot is on-hit magic damage — armour reads that trade wrong","Use Unstoppable Onslaught to collapse the gap instantly — he has zero dash and cannot escape it"],"foe":["Blind your autos with Q on every trade to negate your short-range threat","Kite your telegraph-heavy charged Q with his Move Quick passive and deny you ever reaching full charge","Stack Noxious Traps on chokes and jungle entrances to zone you off objectives with slow and poison"]},"early":"Levels 1 through 3 are Teemo's entire gameplan: his blind (Q) shuts off your auto-attacks on a 7-second cooldown, and his Move Quick passive lets him dance outside your melee range while his E poison ticks you down. Do not try to close into auto range — every time you step up he blinds and kites back, and you accomplish nothing. Instead, crouch at max Q range and begin charging the moment he steps forward to last-hit; the charged Q is spell damage and fires through his blind entirely, so landing the stun costs him a full combo while he was just trying to CS. Accept the poke tax and let your W-shield absorb a hit before detonating for chip damage — do not blow it empty just to trade with a blinded Sion.","mid":"Level 6 changes everything. Unstoppable Onslaught crosses the full lane gap before Move Quick can escape it — dashes and blinks are what beat your R, and he has neither. Bait him into over-extending by standing at the edge of his poke range, then charge through him the moment he commits to a Q step; knockup into charged Q into E shred is lethal at this HP total. Between all-ins keep charging Q from max range — even a dodged charge pushes him off his mushroom placement. And do not facecheck for him: Guerrilla Warfare holds his invisibility while he moves through brush, so an unwarded bush is a free opening combo and your R cannot be steered onto something you cannot see.","late":"At two items your max-HP total makes shroom ticks an annoyance rather than a kill threat, and Glory in Death is a real deterrent — Teemo cannot stand near a dying undead Sion to collect procs. Two things still catch you. Toxic Shot is on-hit magic damage that scales with his attack speed, so armour and Steelcaps read the trade wrong and magic resist alongside the health is what actually helps. And Guerrilla Warfare lets him keep invisibility while moving through brush, so every unwarded bush is his opener. Buy Control Wards, clear traps before river, and in a straight 1v1 you still win: R in, charged Q, E shred.","whys":["Teemo blinds your autos every 7 seconds, but Roar of the Slayer reaches 800 to his 680 — poke from outside his range and save the charge for his last-hit step.","His Move Quick passive kites your Q telegraph, but the spell still fires through his blind — commit to the channel only from max range so he cannot gap-close out of it.","With W online you can absorb a blind-poke, detonate the shield for chip, and back off; the trade starts to cost him more than it costs you.","Levels 4-5 are a lull — he kites freely and you lack the R gap-close. Farm safely, do not waste your charged Q, and track where shrooms are placed.","Unstoppable Onslaught travels faster than Move Quick can escape — land the R knockup and follow with a charged Q stun before he recovers; this is your first reliable kill window.","Heartsteel's stack gain accelerates through this window — every cannon minion is bonus HP, your W-shield scales with that HP, and his poke now evaporates against your shield value.","At 2+ items your zombie passive deals enough damage in its reanimate window that Teemo cannot stand next to a dying Sion safely — the matchup fully inverts and you close any 1v1 instantly."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"trundle","win":["Trundle","Skill","Sion","Sion","Trundle","Sion","Trundle"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Full kit online — land a charged Q knockup into E armor-shred and Trundle loses a third of his HP before his Q bite can outpace your W-shield."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Subjugate steals a percent of your health, armour and MR and doubles it over 4 seconds — 120 seconds at rank 1, so never all-in while it is up and always once it is spent."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel spike: force fights the instant it completes, strictly on Subjugate's cooldown, and your compounding HP passive makes each W-shield harder to burn through."},{"when":"Late","text":"Subjugate takes a percent of your health, armour and MR and doubles it over 4 seconds — the more you stack, the more it takes, so close through objectives rather than trading stacks."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup when Subjugate is on cooldown, then follow with E armor-shred to delete his resistances","Pop Soul Furnace before his bites — Chomp saps your attack damage, so shield first, then detonate back","Run anti-heal (Thornmail or Bramble Vest) to gut his Q-bite sustain and his Subjugate heal"],"foe":["Land Subjugate to steal a percent of your armor and MR and heal off the difference, flipping every extended trade","Use his W Frozen Domain move-speed zone to chase you down inside his enhanced-attack range after a Q bite","Fight inside Frozen Domain where his attack speed and all of his healing are increased, and drag the brawl past your shield"]},"early":"At Level 1 Trundle has the cleaner opener — Chomp is a 3.5-second auto-reset that saps your attack damage while your shield has not yet come online. Stay at the tip of your Q range and charge in brush so he cannot see the wind-up; do not walk into his auto range to trade freely. At Level 2 Soul Furnace changes the math: pop it right before the bite lands and the shield eats the chunk while your detonation ticks him back. Level 3 is your first real opportunity — with all three abilities up, look for a charged Q knockup into immediate E armour-shred; if it lands he is at a heavy deficit before King's Tribute heals it back off dying minions.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are a Subjugate-cooldown game: when his R is down, run your full pattern aggressively — charged Q stun, E shred, W-shield his retaliatory bites, and keep fighting. The moment Subjugate comes back up, give the trade nothing and back off to your side of the wave. At Level 6 your Unstoppable Onslaught gives you all-in threat and escape, but Trundle's R also comes online with full power, stealing a meaningful slice of your armor and MR for the duration. Never commit to a Level 6 all-in while Subjugate is available — wait for the cooldown, then run him down with R into charged Q.","late":"Anti-heal is not optional — Bramble Vest early strips the sustain out of King's Tribute and out of Frozen Domain, which raises his healing from every source while he stands on it, and that zone is where he wants every fight to happen. Glory in Death is your insurance: even if he lands Subjugate and flips a fight, your reanimation forces him to keep committing. What actually kills you is Pillar of Ice — impassable terrain at 1000 range on a 21-second cooldown at rank 1, and you have no dash, so hold Flash for the wall and not for the damage. Use your R's transit speed to take towers and force objectives before Subjugate's percentage grows with every item you buy.","whys":["Chomp is a 3.5-second auto-reset that saps your attack damage, and your shield is not up yet — charge in fog and do not auto-trade freely.","W-shield now absorbs his Q bite chunk; short trades start to favor you if you pop the shield on his first auto and detonate in return.","Full kit — charged Q knockup into E armor-shred deletes his resistances before his Q bites can compound; this is your first clean window.","Trade around Subjugate's cooldown: when it is down push the pattern hard, when it is up give nothing and let the wave reset.","Subjugate is live at 6 and steals a percent of your armor and MR — never all-in while it is available; wait for the cooldown window.","Heartsteel compounding HP makes each W-shield value larger and your zombie passive harder to ignore — force fights strictly on Subjugate's cooldown.","Subjugate takes a percent of your health, armour and MR and doubles it over 4 seconds — the bigger you build, the more it takes, so close through objectives, not 1v1 brawls."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"tryndamere","win":["Tryndamere","Skill","Sion","Skill","Skill","Sion","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1","text":"Tryndamere stacks fury with free autos before your W is online — keep distance and farm with Q tip until you hit level 2."},{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Full kit means you can E-shred his armor and follow with a charged Q knockup; this is your first real punish window if he overextends."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel compounding HP makes your W-shield enormous — you can absorb a full spin burst and brawl through his crit DPS."},{"when":"Undying Rage","text":"He pops R at 1 HP and spins freely for 5 seconds — never commit your charged Q or E into his R; wait it out and finish."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a full-charge Q knockup when Tryndamere is fury-stacking near your side of the wave","W-shield his crit auto burst to deny the short trade and force him to burn fury for nothing","Outlast his Undying Rage before committing your kill pattern — never all-in while it is available"],"foe":["Stack fury freely with autos and E-spin in once his crits are high to blow Sion's W","Spin-kite backward with his E to bait a telegraphed charged Q then re-engage after the whiff","Pop Undying Rage at low HP to stall through your follow-up cooldowns and spin out alive"]},"early":"Level 1 is his best window — Tryndamere auto-attacks freely to stack fury before your W is even a button, and his crits hit hardest on a low-HP pool. Stay back, last-hit with the tip of your Q, and do not let him auto you three times into a confident all-in. At level 2 your W-shield comes online and the dynamic shifts immediately: pop W before he spins in and his crit burst hits the shield instead of your HP bar. By level 3 you have E to shred his armor — bait him onto your side of the wave, E-shred, then begin a charged Q. He has no hard dodge on your stun, so a clean full-charge lands for massive damage and sets the tempo.\
","mid":"Levels 4-5 are the flattest stretch — your Q charge is readable and he can E-spin out of the telegraph while his fury-loaded crits keep ticking. Trade only when he has low fury and his E is on cooldown; otherwise hold your W and let the wave do the work. At level 6 neither ult is a clean win button: your Unstoppable Onslaught can chase him down or escape a bad spot, but his Undying Rage fully answers a full-pattern kill attempt the moment he dips low. Track the R cooldown at 110 seconds — if it has been more than 90 seconds since he last used it, do not start a committed brawl.\
","late":"Heartsteel makes your W-shield substantial enough to eat an entire crit rotation, and your zombie passive means even a bad fight costs him time and HP. The lane stays roughly even because Tryndamere scales into a split-push monster too — his E gap-close and crit DPS 1v1 him will always be dangerous if you try to fight him without the W up. Anti-heal is mandatory: his passive fury restores a meaningful chunk of HP across a trade, which negates small leads. The win pattern late is simple: W-shield absorbs his opening spin burst, E-shreds his armor, charged Q lands the knockup, and you finish after Undying Rage expires — never before.\
","whys":["Tryndamere free-autos to max fury at level 1 with no ability cost and your W is not yet available — farming with Q tip range avoids giving him a free crit burst.","W-shield comes online and absorbs his crit autos; small trades now cost him more fury than they gain him, evening the field.","E-shred into a full-charge Q knockup is live — he has no dash or dodge to avoid the stun if you commit from your side of the wave.","His E is the trade-breaker: he spins through your charge telegraph to dodge the Q or escape after a bad trade. Only charge Q when his E is visibly on cooldown.","Both ultimates create all-in uncertainty — your R is a chase and engage tool but his R keeps him alive through your follow-ups. Track the 110-second R cooldown before every commit.","Heartsteel HP and a large W-shield let you absorb a full crit burst and brawl back — buy anti-heal first to gut his fury passive regeneration during trades.","His crit DPS still kills you if you fight W-down; stay even by respecting his E gap-close and never all-in while Undying Rage is available."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"urgot","win":["Urgot","Skill","Sion","Sion","Sion","Sion","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your W-shield absorbs Urgot's W shotgun-leg shred and lets you detonate for chip damage — the moment his legs stop firing, land the charged-Q stun for a free E-shred trade."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught turns every Disdain flip into your win condition — he flips you in, you R him right back into his own tower or your team before he can run his leg cycle."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP compounding begins outpacing the raw percent damage from Urgot's W legs — your zombie passive also starts threatening enough kill potential to make him respect the all-in."},{"when":"His E (Disdain)","text":"Disdain (E) is a 16s cooldown at rank 1 and drops you into his Purge leg zone — start charging Q as the flip begins so the stun lands the instant he releases you, and the leg cycle he needs never happens."}],"wants":{"you":["Land charged Q to stun him immediately after his Disdain flip so his W legs never get a full cycle on you","W-shield the opening burst of his shotgun legs, then detonate it for magic damage while he is slowed by your E armor-shred","Stack Heartsteel HP so your zombie passive becomes a genuine threat — Urgot has to respect dying while killing you"],"foe":["Disdain-flip you into his W shotgun-leg zone for a full percent-HP shred cycle","Bait your Q charge with a Disdain feint and flip you mid-channel so the knockup never fires","Win the sustained DPS fight where his percent-HP legs tick through your HP stack faster than your W-shield regenerates"]},"early":"At level 1 Urgot has no safe engage — he needs Disdain (E) to set up his shotgun legs. Farm with Q tip range and let him show whether he is playing to poke or to flip. At level 2, once your W-shield is online, you can trade short: step into his leg range briefly, pop W to absorb the first burst of percent-HP damage, detonate for chip, and back off before a full leg cycle completes. At level 3 the full Q-E-W combo is available: wait for him to commit with Disdain, take the flip, then instantly charge Q from point-blank — the stun interrupts his follow-up leg cycle before the percent-HP shred stacks up, and your E armor-shred makes every auto during the stun window count.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are the flattest part of this lane — neither of you has a major new tool, and Urgot's W legs are working at full efficiency against your current HP total. Respect a second Disdain flip if his cooldown is back; trade only when you can absorb the first leg burst with W-shield. Level 6 changes the math entirely: Unstoppable Onslaught answers his flip. When he Disdains you into a bad position, your R lets you charge through him and reposition before his full leg cycle lands. More importantly, you now threaten a long-range engage he cannot answer — Urgot is immobile and your R covers the full lane, so he has to play back or risk being hit during a shotgun-leg cycle that you then punish with a charged-Q stun.","late":"Late game your Heartsteel HP compounding is the reason 53% of Sions beat Urgot at this stage rather than lose. His W legs deal percent-max-HP, but the absolute number has to outpace both your W-shield value (which also scales with max HP) and your zombie passive — and at 5,000+ HP those shields absorb more raw damage than his legs deal in a short trade window. Force short trades: W-shield his flip, charged-Q stun to interrupt the leg cycle, E-slow for arm-shred, then disengage. His R execute only fires below 25% HP, so staying healthy is the win condition. In a full teamfight your zombie passive guarantees trading value even if he executes you — zone him with the threat of a dying 5,000-HP zombie while your team collapses.","whys":["Urgot has no engage at level 1 — Q tip range to farm and don't let him bait a charged-Q channel with a Disdain flip he hasn't thrown yet.","W-shield is online and absorbs his first shotgun-leg burst; detonate it for chip damage and back off before a full leg cycle completes.","Full Q-E-W combo available: take his Disdain flip, immediately charge Q from point-blank to stun him, and E-shred during the lockdown before his legs cycle.","Neither of you has a new major tool — Disdain is a 15s cooldown here, so count it down and trade only while your 13s W-shield is up for the Purge burst.","Unstoppable Onslaught answers his flip and gives you a long-range engage he cannot match; he has to play back or take a charged-Q stun mid leg-cycle.","Heartsteel HP compounding starts outpacing his per-leg percent-HP damage in short trades, and your zombie passive forces him to respect every all-in even when you're low.","Your W-shield value scales with max HP so his percent-HP legs hit a diminishing return — short trades into charged-Q stun remain favoured as long as you stay above his R execute threshold."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"vayne","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Skill","Sion","Skill","Vayne"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"W-shield comes online and your charged-Q stun range outpaces her Silver Bolts proc window — burst her before she builds three stacks."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught closes the gap she relies on to kite — R into her, then charged-Q the stun before she can Tumble clear."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP compound starts, but every third hit is a Silver Bolts percent-max-HP true-damage chunk your armour cannot touch — the race tightens."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Silver Bolts true damage scales directly with your growing HP total — every stack of Heartsteel feeds her W, and she reclaims the lane decisively."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup to stop her Tumble-kite cold and lock in the full Q-E combo","All-in before first item while her Silver Bolts proc still costs her positioning to stack","Keep her near the wall behind her so Condemn punishes her, not you — trade with your back to open space"],"foe":["Tumble sideways on every Q charge telegraph to avoid the knockup and kite at Silver Bolts range","Stack Silver Bolts passively while spacing out of your melee range until three procs deal true damage","Condemn you into a wall when you charge Q to cancel the cast and punish the slow windup"]},"early":"At level 1 your Q charge is dangerously readable — Vayne sidesteps it with Tumble before the knockup lands, so don't open with a raw charge into her. Instead farm at Q tip range and let her push into you; she has no kill threat at level 1 either. At level 2 your W-shield changes the math: step forward, pop the shield pre-emptively to absorb the Silver Bolts proc she's been fishing for, and throw a short-range E-slow to discourage her from stacking the third bolt. Vayne's early CS is aggressive because she needs spacing to proc Silver Bolts safely — deny that spacing by holding brush angles and forcing her to Tumble reactively rather than toward you.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are her safest window — she kites freely, her Tumble is short-cooldown, and you have no gap-closer yet. Don't overextend Q charges in the open; she Tumbles diagonally and the knockup whiffs. Sit at the edge of your Q range and make her burn Tumble on minions, not on dodging your telegraph. Level 6 is your biggest swing: Unstoppable Onslaught covers the gap her Tumble exploit relies on — R into her from outside Condemn range, make contact, then immediately charge Q while she's staggered and can't Tumble cleanly. If you land the knockup, follow with E armor-shred and brawl — your W-shield absorbs the Silver Bolts burst while the shred does its work. Don't fight near walls; Condemn into a wall is a free kill for her.","late":"At two items Silver Bolts true damage has compounded with your Heartsteel HP — every three-auto proc now deals a chunk of your max HP that W-shield cannot cover, and Tumble's low cooldown means she can dodge every charged Q indefinitely. Your zombie passive is still valuable deterrence, but a clean 1v1 at this stage goes to her. Transition into a frontline threat for your team: R-engage onto her backline, force the Condemn, eat it, and let your carries follow. Your job is no longer to kill Vayne — it's to make her burn Condemn and Tumble on you so she can't position to peel or kite your team.","whys":["At level 1 both sides lack meaningful kill threat — Vayne cannot proc Silver Bolts safely and you cannot land a charged Q without her Tumble dodging it; farm and watch spacing.","W-shield absorbs the Silver Bolts three-stack true damage burst and lets you trade back without taking the full proc — step forward under the shield before she completes three autos.","Charged Q knockup stops her Tumble kite cold and lets E armor-shred land cleanly — your first real kill window if she over-commits for a three-stack proc.","Levels 4-5 she kites freely — Tumble is a 4s cooldown while Condemn sits at 18s, so bait the Tumble with a fake Q charge and commit the real one while Condemn is spent.","Unstoppable Onslaught closes the gap that Tumble exploits — R into her and immediately charge Q while she cannot safely reposition, your hardest all-in window before her items.","Heartsteel HP is compounding but Blade of the Ruined King spike means she now shreds max-HP on every Bolts proc — all-in on Condemn cooldown or concede the 1v1.","Silver Bolts true damage scales with your max HP total — every Heartsteel stack you built now feeds her W, and she 1v1s you cleanly; become a frontline R-engage threat for your team instead."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"vladimir","win":["Skill","Skill","Sion","Skill","Sion","Sion","Vladimir"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Your full kit is online — bait his Sanguine Pool with a fake charged-Q approach, then land the real one when it expires to stun him into your E armor-shred."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught accelerates over long distances; Vladimir has no dash to dodge it and his pool can only save him once — if he pools the R, walk him down with charged Q immediately after."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel bulk arrives, but so does his sustain — Bramble Vest here puts Grievous Wounds on Transfusion and Hemoplague before Rylai's makes his trades unwinnable."},{"when":"Late","text":"Full-AP Vladimir's Q drain and R burst outpace your HP scaling — he kites between pool windows and your charged-Q charge time becomes too readable at this stage."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Sanguine Pool with a fake charged-Q telegraph, then land the real fully-charged stun when pool expires","Stack HP with Heartsteel procs and keep the lane close to deny his roam windows pre-6","Force the all-in at Level 6 before his AP items come online and his drain sustain becomes overwhelming"],"foe":["Pool your charged-Q combo on cooldown to make every trade a skill-dodge and deny your all-in","Sustain through W-shield trades with Q drain and out-poke with E until his HP buys start compounding","Survive into two AP items where his R burst and Q drain outscale your HP pool and W-shield value"]},"early":"Build MR second item and plan for it early — Vladimir's E poke is AP and your W-shield does not stop it, so eating three E's in a row costs you a chunk of HP you will not get back in trades. The key level 1-3 pattern is telegraphing a charged Q to bait his pool, then walking up again once the 28-second cooldown ticks down and landing the real charge for the stun into E armor-shred. His Q drain during your W-shield window barely breaks even, so short trades where you land W-shield then immediately back off are slightly in your favour. Never let him stack three E pokes unopposed, and buy Grievous Wounds early — sustain is how he wins.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are the flattest window: he farms and heals with Q, you farm and stack HP passive kills, and neither of you can force a kill cleanly. At 6 the matchup tips your way — Unstoppable Onslaught covers enough ground that Vladimir cannot simply walk out of it, and pool only saves him once per engagement. The play pattern is R to close the gap, pool baited or eaten, then charged-Q stun into E for the full combo while he has no untargetable escape. If he pools your R instead, stop, charge Q, and catch him the moment pool expires — he is a sitting target for the full 2.5 seconds.","late":"This is Vladimir's game from two items onward — Rylai's plus a second AP item gives his R burst and Q drain enough damage to burn through your HP pool faster than your W-shield can absorb it, and he kites between pool windows at a range your charged-Q charge time cannot reliably close. Your zombie passive (Glory in Death) still taxes him if he overcommits to finish you, but in a clean late-game fight he sets the terms. Play for a front-to-back teamfight where your HP-wall forces his pool early for the team, then let your carries follow up — do not try to solo-kill him at full items.","whys":["Both champions are weak early: Vladimir has no meaningful escape pre-W and Sion's charged Q is telegraphed. Farm, land W-shield on his poke trades, and use first blood priority to deny his Q-last-hit sustain.","W-shield absorbs his E poke, and Riot's own text says charged Tides of Blood is blocked by enemy units — keep minions between you and trade from behind the wave, never in the open.","Full kit online: fake the charged-Q approach to force pool — Sanguine Pool is 28s at rank 1 — then stun him into E armor-shred for a real kill window.","He heals with Q and you stack HP kills; neither can force a clean trade. Control the wave to deny him free Q sustain on minions, and look for Herald with your HP lead.","Unstoppable Onslaught closes the gap before he can reposition; if he pools the engage, charge Q immediately and stun him the moment untargetability ends.","Heartsteel plus HP passive kills stack into a bulk lead his Rylai's can't match yet — keep forcing short trades where W-shield absorbs his E and then back out before his Q drain matters.","Full-AP Vladimir's R burst and Q drain outscale your W-shield value and HP pool; play as an initiation wall for your team rather than trying to solo-kill him."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"volibear","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Skill","Skill","Sion","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your W-shield comes online — short-trade every time he tries to W-bite, then detonate the shield for free chip damage while he heals for nothing."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Stormbringer grants him bonus health and shuts your turret off for the dive — hold charged Q for Thundering Smash, a 12s cooldown, and release as his dash commits."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel + HP passive compounding makes your W-shield enormous — force extended trades, because his W-heal cannot keep pace with your bulk."},{"when":"Late","text":"Both of you become frontline juggernauts; the matchup becomes a mutual brawl where your charged-Q stun breaks his Q-W combo before his bite lands."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup to stun him before his Q-dash connects and resets his W-bite window","Detonate W-shield into his face during every short trade to deny the sustain value of his W-bite heal","Anti-heal (Bramble Vest or Executioner's) to gut his W-bite regen so brawls tilt in your HP-passive favor"],"foe":["Open with the Thundering Smash auto-reset — the wind-up is the tell, not the cast — then double W-bite to heal","Use E Sky Splitter to slow you into his melee range and reduce the damage he takes while standing in the lightning zone","Hit Stormbringer R near your tower to disable it and dive you under a huge personal HP shield"]},"early":"At Level 1 both of you are auto-trading juggernauts — respect his Q dash, which stuns you briefly and resets his W-bite window. If he walks up to Q into you, start charging your own Q the moment you see the dash animation; the knockup either catches him mid-dash approach or forces him off after the stun wears off. Level 2 is your first real edge: pop your W-shield as he tries to W-bite, then detonate it into his face — he heals off the bite but your shield ate the damage and returned magic damage back. By Level 3 with Q-W-E all online you win any short trade confidently; your E armor-shred into a charged-Q stun prevents him from walking out of the brawl with his heal intact.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are even ground — his E Sky Splitter slow is annoying for charging your Q since he picks the engagement angle, and standing inside the blast radius shields him while he does it. Respect that 16s window and angle your Q charge from outside the lightning. At Level 6 his Stormbringer is the real danger: it grants him bonus health and kills your tower for a few seconds, making a dive unexpectedly lethal. If he Rs onto your turret, stay back and brawl only once the ultimate is spent — at 160 seconds a wasted Stormbringer hands you the next three minutes of the lane.","late":"With Heartsteel stacked your brawl easily outlasts his W-bite healing — anti-heal on Bramble Vest makes the matchup tilt decisively in your favor because his sustain is his only edge in extended fights. Your zombie passive means even a failed engage gives his team something to deal with. In teamfights use R to engage from fog-of-war distance, stun him with a charged Q before his Q-stun lands on a carry, and let your E armor-shred soften him for your team; your HP pool absorbs his W-bite repeatedly while he gets nothing back through the Grievous Wounds.","whys":["Level 1 is a mutual auto-trade — respect his Q dash stun and charge your own Q the moment he commits; neither of you has a clean edge yet.","Your W-shield online at Level 2 means he heals off the W-bite but your shield ate the hit and detonates magic damage back — short trades tip your way.","Q-W-E all online: E armor-shred into a charged-Q knockup stuns him before he can re-engage with a second W-bite cycle, winning the extended brawl.","Sky Splitter is a flat 16s cooldown that slows you and shields him only if he stands in the blast — angle your Q charge from outside it and he must pick slow or shield.","Stormbringer disables your turret and grants him bonus health — not a shield, so it does not tick away. Brawl once the 160s ultimate is spent, never under a dead tower.","Heartsteel compounding + anti-heal makes your W-shield enormous and guts his W-bite regen — force the extended trade, because he can no longer heal faster than you bulk up.","Both of you are full frontline at this stage; your charged-Q stun breaks his Q-into-W-bite combo sequence before the double on-hit proc connects, and your HP passive zombie guarantees trade value even if he bursts you."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"warwick","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Sion","Sion","Sion","Skill"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your W-shield comes fully online — trade short, detonate the shield for chip damage, and watch his Q heal accomplish nothing against a target who just absorbed the burst."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught turns every Warwick Blood Hunt ping into a death trap — he chases, you R-charge him into your tower or your team."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel stacks your HP hard — but his bite scales off that same max health, so pair the first item with Bramble Vest or the spike works for him instead."},{"when":"His R window","text":"Infinite Duress is a 1.5s suppressing channel — a charged Q or Unstoppable Onslaught cancels it outright — and staying above 50% HP keeps Blood Hunt from beaconing him onto you."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a fully charged Q knockup to stun him out of his Q-heal cycle and follow with E armor-shred","Keep your HP above 50% so his Blood Hunt W gives him no speed advantage when he dives","Buy Grievous Wounds before more HP — his bite scales off your max health and heals him for what it deals"],"foe":["Drag you into a sustained brawl at low HP where his Q heal and passive regeneration outpace your damage","Wait for you to drop below 50% HP to activate Blood Hunt and catch you out of position with Infinite Duress","Force extended trades so his on-hit autos stack up while your W shield is on cooldown"]},"early":"At level 1 the lane is genuinely even — Warwick's Q heals back your poke and your Q charge time telegraphs every attempt. Respect that he trades efficiently here and focus on CS over trades. The moment W comes online at level 2, start dictating short trades: pop W-shield into his Q-heal burst, absorb it, then detonate the shield for magic chip damage and walk away. He cannot out-trade that exchange because his heal does not outpace the shield's absorption plus the detonation chip. By level 3 you have Q, W, and E — if he steps up, land the charged Q knockup, E-slow him for armor shred, and complete the short trade. He has no meaningful escape at this stage so a landed stun punishes hard.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are your cruising window — his kit has no power spike here while your W-shield efficiency is at its early peak. Force trades whenever his Q is on cooldown; he cannot sustain through your Q-E armor-shred combo without his heal up. At level 6 the matchup swings decisively in your favor: Warwick's R is his only real all-in tool, and Unstoppable Onslaught hard-counters it. If he Blood Hunt-chases you, R-charge back through him and knock him up into your minions or tower. The critical rule is HP management — never fight him below 50% because Blood Hunt gives him a free speed beacon that makes his R suppression trivially landable. Keep your HP up and his power fantasy collapses.","late":"Late game you are a max-HP wall, but not one his kit ignores — Jaws of the Beast bites a percent of your MAXIMUM health and heals him for the damage it deals, so every Heartsteel stack makes his bite hit harder and heal him more. Armour is the wrong answer here; Grievous Wounds is, and it belongs in the build before the fight rather than after it. Your zombie passive still means a successful Infinite Duress often costs him more than he earns, since he must survive your counter-damage AND a zombie phase after. In teamfights, R-charge to engage and use your HP pool as a frontline; Warwick wants isolated low-HP targets and your presence denies him those windows. Stay above 50% and Blood Hunt never lights up.","whys":["Level 1 Warwick's Q already heals on hit — his short-trade sustain matches your damage output before W comes online, so last-hit safely and do not force charged Q into a target who heals back the trade.","W-shield absorbs his Q-heal burst and detonates for magic damage he cannot heal back — the exchange is yours even if he wins the raw auto trade.","Charged Q knockup into E armor-shred undoes his lane pattern — Jaws of the Beast is his only lunge on an 8s cooldown, so once he spends it he cannot leave the stun.","His only spike here is on-hit pressure, and your W-shield is 13s at rank 3 — trade on that timer, not on his, and deny the extended brawl where his passive regen takes over.","Unstoppable Onslaught answers his Blood Hunt W directly — he tries to chase a low-HP target, you R-charge through him and flip the engagement; never fight at sub-50% HP where his W activates.","Heartsteel compounds your HP, but Jaws of the Beast bites a percent of your MAX health and heals him for what it deals — the bigger you get the more it feeds him. Buy Grievous Wounds, not armour.","Your zombie passive (Glory in Death) punishes his Infinite Duress suppress — he suppresses you and eats free zombie damage while you reanimate, making his all-in a losing exchange at full build."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"wukong","win":["Skill","Skill","Sion","Skill","Skill","Wukong","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Your first full combo window: E armor-shred sets up a charged Q knockup on the real body, punishing him before he has clone-escape access mid-trade."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Wukong's Trinity Force or Divine Sunderer spike hits hard — he out-bursts you if his Q armor-shred lands and you take the full E-AA-R combo."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your Unstoppable Onslaught gives you first real all-in threat and splitpush transit; he can't chase you down after a bad trade once R is available."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Your Heartsteel HP stack compounds past the point his burst can delete — W-shield eats his combo and your zombie passive threatens him even on the way down."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a full-charge Q knockup on the REAL body, not the W clone decoy","Use E armor-shred before the Q stun to maximize damage in the stun window","Build enough HP that his Trinity burst cannot one-rotation you even after his Q armor-shred"],"foe":["Drop his W clone to bait your charged Q into thin air, then re-engage unpunished","Open with Crushing Blow's auto-reset — its wind-up is the tell — to shred your armour","Stack armor to blunt your W-shield detonation and reach Trinity Sunderer spike ahead of your Heartsteel compound"]},"early":"At level 1 you are actually the threat — a fully charged Q is a knockup he cannot dodge unless he burns his W decoy early. Play toward that: stand at partial-charge range and force him to either take the Q or waste the decoy. At level 2 he gets his E dash and the real trading pattern begins; W-shield his E-AA combo and counter with your own auto into a partial-charge Q to slow him. By level 3 your full kit is live — E to shred his armor, then charge Q to knock him up while the shred is active. Just remember his Q simultaneously shreds YOUR armor, so every extended trade gets worse for you; land the stun and get out rather than brawling past the knockup.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are genuinely even but tilted by clone discipline. He will drop the W decoy in the middle of a trade to dodge your charged Q; the moment you see the decoy appear, release the charge immediately — if it hits the clone you wasted your whole trade window and he re-engages unpunished. At level 6 his R is a spin knockup that can interrupt your charged Q channel if he times it correctly, so stay out of his spin range while charging. Your own R at 6 can be used to escape a bad trade or threaten a tower dive, which shifts the psychological dynamic; he has to respect you diving him at speed now.","late":"At first item Wukong pulls ahead if he hits Trinity Force — Divine Sunderer's percent-HP proc into your large HP total actually hurts, and his Q armor-shred means your W detonation deals less than expected. Respect this window: do not all-in him when his full combo is up and armor-shred is freshly applied. Past two items the lane swings to you. Your Heartsteel-compounded HP pool means his burst rotation cannot one-shot you, your W-shield absorbs a large chunk of his combo, and your zombie passive still threatens him after death. Play the side lane and let your HP differential force him to commit everything just to trade even.","whys":["Level 1 he has no Warrior Trickster decoy yet, so your charged Q cannot be baited — landing it on him is still the hard part.","Level 2 he gets his E dash and trades more freely; W-shield his all-in burst and partial-charge Q back, but neither of you has decisive kill threat.","Level 3 your E armor-shred into charged Q is your cleanest combo window — land the stun before he can drop his W decoy.","Levels 4-5 are skill-check on clone reads — if you fire your charged Q into empty air the lane swings to him, if you hold it for the real body you stay even.","Level 6 is genuine coin-flip: his R spin can disrupt your Q channel but your R gives escape and dive threat, keeping the window contested.","His Trinity Force or Divine Sunderer first item hits a burst-and-shred spike that out-paces your Heartsteel compound — don't trade into his full rotation here.","Past two items your HP stack outgrows his burst, your W-shield absorbs his combo, and your zombie passive punishes any fight he picks against you."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"yasuo","win":["Skill","Sion","Sion","Skill","Skill","Sion","Yasuo"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your W-shield comes online and you can actually absorb his Steel Tempest poke, then punish his dash-in with a charged Q knockup he cannot Wind Wall."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel completed: your HP passive starts compounding and your W-shield value inflates with max HP, making short trades increasingly lopsided in your favour."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"His Last Breath gives him a free knockup-into-ult combo off any airborne target — your R is your primary engage now, not a gap-closer into his full rotation."},{"when":"2 items","text":"Infinity Edge spike: his crit DPS and Last Breath follow-ups start out-damaging your W-shield and zombie passive, so respect the 1v1 and keep him focused on lane."}],"wants":{"you":["Charge Q from fog or behind the wave so Wind Wall has no angle to block the ground slam","Use E armor-shred immediately after the knockup connects to maximise the free damage window before he dashes away","W-shield his Steel Tempest Q3 tornado before committing so you absorb the hit and trade back on your terms"],"foe":["Stack Steel Tempest charges behind the wave and release Q3 tornado to zone you off CS without dashing in","Drop Wind Wall onto your E shockwave so the armour shred and slow never reach him","Land Last Breath off any knockup source — including your own R — and follow with crit autos while you are still airborne"]},"early":"At level 1 you are both feeling each other out — Yasuo pokes with Steel Tempest autos and you are slow to charge Q in the open without being predictable. Hold the charge behind the wave or in brush so Wind Wall cannot intercept the angle; your Q is a ground slam that bypasses Wind Wall entirely, but only if you release it where there is no wall blocking the trajectory. At level 2 your W-shield goes up and suddenly his poke costs him more than it costs you — take the short trade: absorb a Steel Tempest auto on your shield, detonate the shield for magic damage, and back off. At level 3 the charged Q knockup is your full combo; once he sees a charge he will try to dash through a minion to dodge, so bait his dash first with a half-charge, then immediately start the real charge as he repositions.","mid":"Levels 4 and 5 are the lull for both of you — his damage is fine but not yet crit-scaling, and your stun is readable without minion cover to obscure your charge. Use E armor-shred to create freeze pressure and deny him safe CS steps. At level 6 the math shifts: his Last Breath can follow up any airborne effect, including your own R knockup if you drive through him, so think before you charge in — use R to engage on YOUR terms and land the charged Q stun before he can dash to a new minion and reset his position. Wind Wall is 25 seconds at rank 1 and it only stops your E, never the Q slam; the moment he spends it on your shockwave, commit the full combo.","late":"Your W-shield at two items is enormous and your zombie passive means even a losing fight costs him HP he cannot ignore, but his Infinity Edge crit DPS and constant dashing make the extended 1v1 increasingly uncomfortable for you — he can weave in and out of your Q charge range indefinitely. Play for side-lane pressure with your zombie threat rather than solo-fighting a fed Yasuo; your R is a team fight engage tool and your bulk means you survive long enough for your team to follow. If you are ahead, end the game before his third item — a fully crit-stacked Yasuo with Last Breath back every 30 seconds will outrace your HP stacking.","whys":["Both of you hit level 1 with no meaningful CC — trade auto-for-auto sparingly and start identifying his Steel Tempest charge rhythm so you know when Q3 tornado is coming.","Your W-shield absorbs a Steel Tempest poke and detonates for magic damage — his crit scaling is not online yet so short trades favour your bulk.","Full kit online: charged Q into E armor-shred is your rotation — Wind Wall cannot stop the Q slam but it does eat the E, so land the stun first and shred second.","Neither of you has a meaningful power spike here — use E to shred armor and freeze the wave; avoid open-field Q charges that he can simply sidestep.","His Last Breath goes online and changes the calculus on your R engage — use Unstoppable Onslaught to initiate but land the charged Q stun before he repositions off a minion dash.","Heartsteel is completed and your max-HP W-shield inflates with every stack — short trades are heavily favoured and your zombie passive denies his extended all-ins.","His Infinity Edge crit spike outpaces your W-shield value in the extended 1v1 — respect the damage, play for side-lane bulk presence, and let your team follow your R engages."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"yone","win":["Skill","Skill","Sion","Yone","Sion","Skill","Yone"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3","text":"Your W-shield comes fully online — trade short when Yone's Q3 knockup is on cooldown and detonate the shield before he snaps back from E."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught gives you a kill threat: if Yone uses Soul Unbound (E), hard-commit with R before he can retract, locking him in place for the full charged-Q stun."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Yone's Kraken Slayer spike hits here — its true-damage passive punishes your HP stack every third auto, so watch the stacking application and respect extended fights."},{"when":"2+ items","text":"Full Kraken + Wit's End or Rageblade means Yone's on-hit true damage and %HP shred outpaces your W-shield value; you frontline and zone but cannot 1v1 him at this stage."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Yone's Soul Unbound (E) and punish the retraction window with a fully charged Q knockup before he snaps to safety","Pop W-shield into his Q3 burst and detonate it early in trades to keep the HP trade in your favor","Force short skirmishes where his Kraken Slayer true-damage passive has no time to stack three autos"],"foe":["Stack Kraken Slayer third-auto true damage on your HP pool in every extended trade to punish pure HP stacking","Poke with Soul Unbound and retract before your Q releases — the snapback repeats his spirit damage","Reach 2 items where his on-hit kit outscales your W-shield value and you can no longer match him in a straight duel"]},"early":"Level 1 you are both feeling each other out — Yone has no real all-in and you can land a partial charged Q if he walks up, but he will mostly respect the charge animation. At level 2 activate W-shield early in any trade so the detonation chip lands before he Q1-Q2-Q3s you, and back off before his Q3 knockup resolves. Level 3 is your clearest advantage: W absorbs his Q3-W cleave burst and you can charged Q him during his Soul Unbound (E) window — he is committed until the retraction snaps him back, and the stun catches him mid-flight. Keep trades short; do not let him land three autos and proc Doran's Blade or early Kraken-stacking runepage bonuses.","mid":"Levels 4-5 are rough because Yone's Q cooldown is short and he can weave in-out faster than you can charge. Do not telegraph your Q in the open — position behind your wave and only begin the charge when he has committed his Soul Unbound and cannot freely retract. At level 6, Unstoppable Onslaught changes the equation: if Yone burns E to poke, you can R-gap-close and land the charged Q stun before he retracts, turning his aggression into a kill window. Use R offensively here, not just as an escape — it is your strongest 6 spike in this matchup.","late":"First item onward is where this lane flips. Yone's Kraken Slayer third-auto true damage stacks directly against your max-HP investment — every extended duel he lands three autos gives him true damage that your W-shield cannot absorb. Your job shifts from winning 1v1 duels to zoning: stand in their backline, threaten your zombie passive, and make the enemy team deal with your presence while your carries clean up. Never 1v1 Yone alone at 2 items — the Kraken true-damage, Wit's End or Rageblade on-hit, and his mobility via Q dashes means he simply dismantles your HP pillar faster than you regenerate it through trades.","whys":["Yone has no hard engage at level 1 — he can Q1-Q2 poke but his Q3 knockup requires a third Q cast he rarely reaches safely; stay even by farming at Q range.","Spirit Cleave shields him while it damages you, and its 14s cooldown shortens as his attack speed grows — his shield outlasts yours in a long trade, so take one exchange only.","Soul Unbound is 22s at rank 1 and leaves his body behind — stun the body with a charged Q before the spirit snaps back, and the E shred turns that punish into a real HP lead.","Yone's Q dash pattern lets him trade and disengage before your charge telegraph completes; hold the Q charge behind the minion wave and only release when he is mid-E retraction.","Unstoppable Onslaught hard-closes on a retracting Yone — if he uses Soul Unbound at mid-lane range, your R charge reaches him before he snaps back and locks him into your charged-Q follow-up.","Kraken Slayer's first-item true-damage passive starts punishing your max-HP stacking now; keep trades to one Q-E combo and disengage before three autos land, or your HP advantage evaporates.","At full build Yone's Kraken true damage, on-hit chain, and Q-dash mobility beat your W-shield value outright in a sustained 1v1 — play as a frontline zoner and let your zombie passive do the work, not your dueling."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"yorick","win":["Yorick","Sion","Sion","Sion","Skill","Sion","Yorick"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Full kit online — charge Q from fog-of-war behind the wave and knockup Yorick the instant he steps up to E-poke, before Dark Procession is in play."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Both ultimates come up — Yorick's Eulogy of the Isles threatens a 2v1 environment, but your Unstoppable Onslaught can blow past his ghoul swarm and reach him directly."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel makes your W-shield enormous, but Last Rites heals him on a 6s reset — pair the first item with Grievous Wounds and all-in while the 20s cage is spent."},{"when":"Late","text":"Yorick's Maiden plus two items creates a side-lane situation that runs itself — respect the split and never 1v1 him in a walled corridor where Dark Procession traps you."}],"wants":{"you":["Charge Q in the brushline or behind minions so Yorick cannot see the channel and dodge the knockup","E-slow into a full-charge Q stun the instant Dark Procession is on cooldown — never commit while the cage is available","Keep the wave on your side so Yorick must walk past his tower to reach you, forcing him into your engage range without his ghoul swarm already stacked"],"foe":["Wall you in with Dark Procession after the knockup so you cannot leave the ghoul pile while it beats on you","Shove the wave under your tower with E mist-walker poke so you can never safely charge Q in the open","Summon the Maiden and press a 2v1 side-lane environment that forces your team to send help or cede the tower"]},"early":"At level 1 Yorick's E mist-cage poke is annoying but costs him mana and does not threaten your kill — last-hit at Q tip range and do not give him a clean E trade. Level 2 is your first real window: charge Q in the brushline while he walks up for his next mist-walker throw, then knockup into W-shield the return hit — he has no answer to a stun he did not see coming. By level 3 your full pattern is live; keep the wave slightly on your side so he must overextend into your knockup range, and respect Dark Procession the entire time — if he has the cage available, do not channel a Q in the open.","mid":"The Levels 4-6 window is about discipline: Yorick wins nothing for free here, but he will punish any Q you telegraph in the open with a cage-and-ghoul combo that reverses the trade. Track his Dark Procession cooldown visually — every time it falls off, step up and threaten the charged Q; when it is back up, step off and wave-clear. At level 6 both ultimates shift the math: his Eulogy of the Isles creates a 2v1, and your Unstoppable Onslaught can skip over ghouls to reach him, but do not go for the all-in unless you are ahead and his Dark Procession is down — a cage mid-onslaught still traps you inside the ghoul pile.","late":"With two items Yorick's Maiden plus ghoul swarm creates a side-lane that wins itself with no input from him — never fight him in a pinched corridor where Dark Procession walls you in and the Maiden joins the brawl. Your job late is to use Unstoppable Onslaught as a roaming tool, engage in team fights where his ghouls are out of position, and let your enormous HP pool and zombie passive force the enemy to burn resources killing you twice. If he has committed to a split, match it or collapse on him with your team — never let the Maiden walk down a tower uncontested while you duel him man-to-man.","whys":["Mourning Mist marks you from 700 range and speeds his Walkers onto the mark — last-hit at Q tip, and watch the Last Rites wind-up, not the cast.","Charge Q from the brushline while Yorick walks up to E — his Dark Procession is not yet reliable and a knockup he cannot react to wins the trade cleanly.","Full kit online and Dark Procession is 20 seconds at rank 1 — run E-slow into charged Q, W-shield the counter-hit, and be out before the cage is back up.","Dark Procession cooldown windows are your entire trade pattern here — step up when it is down, back off the instant it recharges, and bank small HP leads.","Both ultimates live — Yorick's Eulogy creates a 2v1 environment that neutralizes your pattern if you are not ahead; neither side owns this stage cleanly.","Heartsteel's HP compound inflates your W-shield and zombie passive damage — force the all-in on Dark Procession's cooldown and you win the full exchange.","Maiden plus two items makes Yorick a side-lane engine that runs without him — never 1v1 him in a corridor; your Unstoppable Onslaught and HP win team fights, not split duels."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"zac","win":["Skill","Zac","Zac","Skill","Skill","Zac","Sion"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 2-3","text":"Your W-shield comes fully online and Zac's early trades are predictable — bait his W blob-slap and answer with a charged Q stun before he can eat the blob back."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Zac's Elastic Slingshot (E) is his only real engage; your Unstoppable Onslaught (R) can intercept his landing zone or punish a whiffed sling."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP stacking starts compounding on top of your passive kill-HP, making you noticeably harder to kill in sustained brawls — force extended trades."},{"when":"Late","text":"Zac's passive (Cell Division) lets him revive if you don't kill all four blobs, so always have your W-shield detonation ready to finish off the bounce-back blobs."}],"wants":{"you":["Land a full-charge Q knockup right as he lands from his E slingshot","Step on his blobs (Cell Division passive) to deny the sustain he needs to out-trade","Force extended brawls with your W-shield active so your HP passive compounds"],"foe":["Open with the Stretching Strikes reset — its wind-up is the tell — then slingshot in behind it","Eat blobs back to sustain through your W-shield detonations","Stall the lane into a sideline-tank role where his CC and bulk match yours"]},"early":"Zac has almost no damage at level 1 — his Q (Stretching Strikes) does poke but he needs blobs from his W (Unstable Matter) and passive to sustain through anything. Step on every blob he drops so his healing is cut off, and use Q at tip range to land free chip without giving him a target. At level 2 your W-shield goes up and you can absorb his first real trade; let him W-slap you, shield it, then answer with a short-charged Q to shove him off. At level 3 his slingshot is online but it telegraphs from far away — point your body toward the likely landing zone and hold the charged Q so you stun him the instant he touches down.","mid":"From levels 4-5 Zac starts using his E more aggressively to fish for CC chains into his team or to escape trades that go wrong; this is his strongest evasion window because his blob sustain lets him absorb chip and retreat. Respect the slingshot range and don't over-extend to charge a Q — if he bails with E you've wasted the commitment. At level 6 your R is an effective counter-slingshot tool: if he burns his E to escape, R into his path to deny the disengage; if he misses the slingshot you get a free all-in, because that is 18 seconds of vulnerability at rank 2.","late":"Late-game Zac is a teamfight tank, not a lane threat, so you trade laning dominance for a relatively even macro. His Cell Division passive (four blobs that revive him on death) is the one mechanic that costs you kills — when you burst him below threshold, immediately W-detonate on the blobs or E-shove them off you so he can't fully recombine. Your zombie passive (Glory in Death) means even if he catches you at low HP with a slingshot, you deal real damage during the reanimate window; in a straight 1v1 he cannot safely trade into that. The matchup stays even because he has more teamfight CC, but your splitpush threat and R-engage give you equal map presence.","whys":["Zac has no damage at level 1 — step on blobs to cut his passive healing and farm safely; neither side can force a kill here.","Unstable Matter is a 5s cooldown that burns a percent of your max HP — the shield absorbs one slap, never the loop, so take a single trade and leave.","Elastic Slingshot is 21s at rank 1 and fully telegraphed — stun him on landing, but the trade only pays if you also step on the blobs he sheds.","Zac kites in and out on E cooldown and sustains blob-by-blob; respect the engage range and don't charge Q into open air where he can disengage.","Your R gives you an intercept tool for his E landing zone and your slingshot-punish window opens — neither side decisively owns this stage.","Heartsteel compounds your HP, but Unstable Matter takes a percent of maximum health — every stack you add makes his slap hit harder, so keep it to one exchange.","His Cell Division blobs give him a revive threat that cancels late kills; step on the blobs or W-detonate them — if you do, your zombie passive still deals damage when he tries to finish you."]},
  {"a":"sion","b":"ziggs","win":["Ziggs","Ziggs","Ziggs","Ziggs","Sion","Skill","Ziggs"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 1-3","text":"Ziggs' Q Bouncing Bomb and E Hexplosive Minefield make it impossible to charge your Q without eating repeated poke — you cannot threaten him at any range he chooses."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Unstoppable Onslaught travels the full lane distance — Ziggs cannot W-knockback a charge already in motion, so this is your only reliable gap-close window all game."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Heartsteel's HP stack starts compounding but Ziggs' range advantage means he can simply stay back and your bulk has no one to punish with it."},{"when":"Late","text":"Short Fuse empowers his autos and Satchel Charge hexplodes a weakened turret outright — he takes the tower from a screen away while you are still walking at him."}],"wants":{"you":["Force a charged-Q knockup with E armor-shred follow-up on any overextension Ziggs makes at tower","Use Unstoppable Onslaught at level 6 to collapse on him before he can W-knockback you away","Stack Heartsteel bulk and be a front-line threat in team fights since you cannot duel him in lane"],"foe":["Keep maximum range and drop E Hexplosive Minefield in your path to punish every charge attempt","Use W Satchel Charge to interrupt or redirect your Unstoppable Onslaught approach angle","Poke your W Soul Furnace shield down before it ever matters so short trades are always his favor"]},"early":"Ziggs out-ranges every tool you own at levels 1 through 3. His Q Bouncing Bomb arcs over minions and his E Hexplosive Minefield sits in your walk-up path, meaning any attempt to charge your Q telegraphs you straight into poke before you can release it. Your W Soul Furnace shield will absorb some of it, but he pokes faster than it cools down. Accept a farm-lane, hug the minion wave to reduce his bomb angles, and never charge Q in open ground — he will walk away and drop another bomb on your head before the stun lands.","mid":"Level 6 is your one window. Unstoppable Onslaught accelerates over distance and Ziggs cannot W-knockback a charge that is already connecting — so line it up from max range when he is farming near tower and you will actually reach him. Outside that R cooldown, levels 4 through 5 are more of the same: he pokes your shield down, you have no answer at his range, and you waste E Roar of the Slayer into thin air. Save your R for an honest all-in or a roam, not a desperation charge into his W escape.","late":"Your Heartsteel bulk makes you a real frontline threat in five-versus-five but this lane never becomes a true duel. Short Fuse empowers his autos and Satchel Charge hexplodes a weakened turret outright, so he wins every side-lane siege while you chase him into his own minefield. Post-6 your best plan is to soak poke, match his split-push pressure by threatening with R transit time, and be the initiation body in grouped fights where your W shield and passive zombie matter. Trying to 1v1 him under his own conditions at any item stage is still not realistic — he stands behind E and Q-bounces you down.","whys":["Bouncing Bomb arcs over minions every 6s and Hexplosive Minefield sits on a flat 16s — between them a charged Q release is near-impossible at level 1.","With W Soul Furnace online you can absorb one trade cycle, but Ziggs' bomb cooldowns are short enough to poke through your shield before you can retaliate at his range.","Your full basic kit is live but he still out-ranges every part of it — E Roar of the Slayer has no target to reach and Q charges telegraph you into his Q-E poke before you release.","Levels 4-5 are the longest lull: no R, no gap-close, and Bouncing Bomb returns every 5s while Soul Furnace takes 13s to come back.","Unstoppable Onslaught travels faster than Ziggs can react and his W Satchel Charge cannot displace a charge already in contact — your first and clearest kill window all game.","Heartsteel compounding means you are harder to kill in skirmishes, but Ziggs' range keeps your HP stack from ever being tested in this lane — call it contested.","Ziggs' Short Fuse siege passive and permanent range advantage let him out-push and out-poke you at two items, reclaiming the lane decisively when you expect to be unkillable."]}
